name': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/18c87493-e7e4-5543-a361-332e646fa072', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18c87493-e7e4-5543-a361-332e646fa072'}, {'abstract': 'Recently introduced varietal oils tap the flavors of grapes ranging from chardonnay to merlot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/dining/oils-made-with-wine-grapes.html', 'snippet': 'Recently introduced varietal oils tap the flavors of grapes ranging from chardonnay to merlot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With access to the raw materials right nearby, Valentin and Nanette Humer have been making their Salute Santé! grape seed oils in Napa, Calif., for 15 years; three years ago they added cold-pressed oils. And they have recently introduced varietal oils: chardonnay, chenin blanc, viognier, riesling, French colombard, sauvignon blanc, merlot, syrah, zinfandel and sangiovese. There are subtle differences between them, with the ones made from white grape vines, notably riesling, sauvignon blanc and French colombard, delivering a more herbal flavor and aroma, as opposed to the merlot and syrah, which are richer, and more buttery. Use them as a finishing touch for various dishes, and consider pairing an oil with whatever wine you’re serving.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/02/dining/02STUFF_OILSPAN/02STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/02/dining/02STUFF_OILSPAN/02STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/02/dining/02STU