icker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-01T12:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/4287c522-53ba-53a9-a7ab-bfff2d6cb9e1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/4287c522-53ba-53a9-a7ab-bfff2d6cb9e1'}, {'abstract': 'In the right place and in the right hands, a flash of humor can be a powerful tool in our writing. Understatement is effective, and we’re not looking for belly laughs. It’s important for editors to serve as a skeptical audience, lest a writer’s wit seem witty mostly to the writer. But here are a few recent examples where an amusing incongruity made a sharp point. And if readers smiled, so much the better.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/01/bright-passages-humor-division/', 'snippet': 'In the right place and in the right hands, a flash of humor can be a powerful tool in our writing. Understatement is effective, and we’re not looking for belly laughs. It’s important for editors to serve as a skeptical audience, lest a writer’s wi...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the right place and in the right hands, a flash of humor can be a powerful tool in our writing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bright Passages (Humor Division)', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-01T13:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'ny