': 'Visas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-01T18:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11359ddf-071e-5caf-a0af-954f88c4a4ed', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11359ddf-071e-5caf-a0af-954f88c4a4ed'}, {'abstract': 'Juilliard School announces its annual Focus! Festival event calendar.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/01/juilliard-focus-festival-announces-winter-concerts/', 'snippet': 'Juilliard School announces its annual Focus! Festival event calendar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Juilliard School’s 29th annual Focus! Festival, “The British Renaissance: British Music Since World War II,” will run from Jan. 25 to Feb. 1, with six free concerts and one preconcert discussion. The festival, directed by Joel Sachs, will have 33 works by as many composers, in a huge range of styles. Comparatively conservative scores like Britten’s “Sinfonia da Requiem” are included, as are a genre-crossing piece by Errollyn Wallen; a vivid evocation of an asteroid bound for earth, by Mark-Anthony Turnage; and Alexander Goehr’s 300th anniversary tribute to Bach, “… a musical offering (J.S.B. 1985).”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Juilliard Focus! Festival Announces Winter Concerts', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Juilliard Announces British Festival Offerings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sachs, Joel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juilliard School', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-01T18:22:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1c48c48-69fe-5f08-926f-3947b8647dd7', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1c48c48-69fe-5f08-926f-3947b8647dd7'}, {'abstract': 'The Internet removed the stigma from vanity publishing and, more important, most of the cost. As a result, millions of people have fulfilled their dream of becoming an author. For a much smaller group, technology is just beginning to enable a related but much more specialized pursuit; they are becoming publishers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/01/publishing-without-perishing/', 'snippet': 'The Internet removed the stigma from vanity publishing and, more important, most of the cost. As a result, millions of people have fulfilled their dream of becoming an author. For a much smaller group, technology is just beginning to enable a rela...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the old days, life for small publishers was a hassle. The economics were such that copies got dramatically cheaper when printed in bulk, but then the books had to be stored, which was expensive. Finding an audience was the hardest part; some independent presses took years or even decades to sell out a modest print run.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/01/business/01bits-whiskypriest/01bits-whiskypriest-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 309, 'width': 408, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/01/business/01bits-whiskypriest/01bits-whiskypriest-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 309, 'width': 408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/01/business/01bits-whiskypriest/01bits-whiskypriest-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/01/business/01bits-whiskypriest/01bits-whiskypriest-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/01/business/01bits-whiskypriest/01bits-whiskypriest-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Publishing Without Perishing', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lulu Enterprises Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-01T18:26:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8c5b002-ef90-5ff7-a8c7-e4948faf3f82', 'word_count': 1437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8c5b002-ef90-5ff7-a8c7-e4948faf3f82'}, {'abstract': 'Ivorian officials Tuesday said that they were baffled as to what set off the stampede, which occurred as revelers were leaving a spr