et-as-oscar-voters-encounter-more-difficulties/', 'snippet': 'As the Oscar voting problems continue, technology may be a much bigger factor this awards season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congress has apparently avoided the fiscal cliff, but the cinematic precipice may still be looming. The Academy pushed back the deadline for Oscar voting, to accommodate members still dealing with the two options this year – traditional paper ballot or new electronic voting – and with problems logging on to the site during an already shortened nominations window. Even paper ballots are coming in slower, according to Reuters. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitting Reset as Oscar Voters Encounter More Difficulties', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T15:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b9d6de8-7347-55c5-95db-f0b4d1fda049', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b9d6de8-7347-55c5-95db-f0b4d1fda049'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/what-were-reading-598/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Adelaide Review: A chef who has studied Australia’s native cooking traditions (and has served emu, wild ginger and water lilies at his Adelaide restaurant) wrote this essay on Australia’s “bush foods.” — Julia Moskin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T15:09:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6efa0d7-e43d-551f-a588-80649697db31', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6efa0d7-e43d-551f-a588-80649697db31'}, {'abstract': 'The Single Stop Veterans Initiative, a collaboration by three groups, helps veterans reintegrate into civilian life with counselors at the Mid-Manhattan Library and six other locations around New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/a-library-joins-a-drive-for-veterans-success/', 'snippet': 'The Single Stop Veterans Initiative, a collaboration by three groups, helps veterans reintegrate into civilian life with counselors at the Mid-Manhattan Library and six other locations around New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a decade, a nonprofit group called Single Stop has provided counseling to poor people trying to navigate a confusing tangle of government and private antipoverty programs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Library Joins a Drive for Veterans’ Success', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Robin Hood Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T15:16:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb620a31-7954-5ab0-9994-87129ad389bb', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb620a31-7954-5ab0-9994-87129ad389bb'}, {'abstract': 'Animals fight over ownership of an egg and learn a powerful lesson about greed, responsibility and regret, in Michel Van Zeveren’s “That’s Mine!”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/books/thats-mine-by-michel-van-zeveren.html', 'snippet': 'Animals fight over ownership of an egg and learn a powerful lesson about greed, responsibility and regret, in Michel Van Zeveren’s “That’s Mine!”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the political realm, the concept of unintended consequences usually refers to some variant of dreary socioeconomic fallout from a misguided policy measure. But in picture-book land, unintended consequences are more often a source of giggle-inducing hilarity. Children love the unexpected — especially when it happens to other people.\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/02/books/02childrens-1/02childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/02/books/02childrens-1/02childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/02/books/02childrens-1/02childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 641,