all, a creature of fashion, seeks a place in the art world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SIPPING a soy latte at the Café Select in SoHo last week, Andrea Mary Marshall was the picture of propriety. Her name and faintly starchy manner conjured a schoolgirl in a sober gray jumper tamely reciting her catechism. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/03/fashion/03UPCLOSE/03zUPCLOSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/03/fashion/03UPCLOSE/03zUPCLOSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2037, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/03/fashion/03UPCLOSE/03zUPCLOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/03/fashion/03UPCLOSE/03zUPCLOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/03/fashion/03UPCLOSE/03zUPCLOSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shape-Shifter Pursuing the Unsavory', 'kicker': 'Up Close', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shape-Shifter Pursuing the Unsavory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marshall, Andrea Mary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T21:19:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84a6ea3e-f4b1-5493-a73d-d923a16bde10', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84a6ea3e-f4b1-5493-a73d-d923a16bde10'}, {'abstract': 'General Motors says the new technology deploys the air bag more efficiently, while providing crash protection that is equivalent to, or better than, more expensive and complex dual-stage air bags, depending upon the type of crash.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/2013-chevrolet-cruze-uses-new-air-bag-technology/', 'snippet': 'General Motors says the new technology deploys the air bag more efficiently, while providing crash protection that is equivalent to, or better than, more expensive and complex dual-stage air bags, depending upon the type of crash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2013 Chevrolet Cruze comes with a new air bag technology: a frontal air bag for the driver that is\xa0designed with a different type of venting. General Motors says the new technology deploys the air bag more efficiently while providing crash protection that is equivalent to, or better than, more expensive and complex dual-stage air bags, depending upon the type of crash.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2013 Chevrolet Cruze Uses New Air Bag Technology', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Insurance Institute for Highway Safety', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T21:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/199ab6d8-171b-571a-8289-99e5e3e95cf3', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/199ab6d8-171b-571a-8289-99e5e3e95cf3'}, {'abstract': 'In Warren Ellis’s “Gun Machine” a murderous psychopath who believes he is a resident of pre-colonial Manhattan is tracked by a messy, isolated police detective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/books/gun-machine-by-warren-ellis.html', 'snippet': 'In Warren Ellis’s “Gun Machine” a murderous psychopath who believes he is a resident of pre-colonial Manhattan is tracked by a messy, isolated police detective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just about everything in “Gun Machine,” Warren Ellis’s dark but pleasingly quirky crime thriller, is a little bit off, not quite what you’d expect. The machine of the title, for example, is not a manufacturing device but an apartment in a rundown tenement on Pearl Street, in Manhattan, that is crammed with guns, and not just ordinary ones. The pistol that once belonged to the Son of Sam killer is here, and so is a flintlock pistol used in the first recorded murder in Rochester. Both have been repurposed for additional killings, and so has every other gun in the place, which is a virtual museum of murders, 20 years’ worth, carefully tended by a figure known only as “the hunter.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 