ltimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Defying Gravity: ‘Wicked’ Soars to Broadway Box Office Record', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Wicked’ Sets Record', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Annie (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rock of Ages (Play)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pacino, Al', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T22:52:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ab662a7-b8d9-575d-be1d-b69f7b7a1ba8', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ab662a7-b8d9-575d-be1d-b69f7b7a1ba8'}, {'abstract': 'Ragnar Kjartansson, whose installation “The Visitors” comes to Chelsea next month, has spent more than a decade exploring the potential of repetitive performance to yield unexpected meanings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/arts/design/the-visitors-by-ragnar-kjartansson.html', 'snippet': 'Ragnar Kjartansson, whose installation “The Visitors” comes to Chelsea next month, has spent more than a decade exploring the potential of repetitive performance to yield unexpected meanings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN “The Visitors,” a nine-screen video installation by Ragnar Kjartansson that will have its first American showing next month, the artist lies in a pedestal bathtub almost in a trance, strumming a guitar as he repeatedly sings a refrain, “Once again, I fall into my feminine ways.” Over the course of an hour his voice falls and rises, on its own and in unison with performers on the other eight screens — each seen as if in a painting, playing an instrument in a different room of a beautiful, run-down mansion and singing the same enigmatic refrain at a dirgelike pace. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06RAGNAR_395/06RAGNAR1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06RAGNAR_395/06RAGNAR1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06RAGNAR_395/06RAGNAR1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06RAGNAR_395/06RAGNAR1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06RAGNAR_395/06RAGNAR1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Never Tiring of Repeating Himself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Never Tiring Of Repeating Himself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheets, Hilarie M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kjartansson, Ragnar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Luhring Augustine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Institute of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-02T22:59:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilarie M. Sheets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bc1ad3c-1c79-5d5d-8cde-a0971f5f2921', 'word_count': 1324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bc1ad3c-1c79-5d5d-8cde-a0971f5f2921'}, {'abstract': 'Even as the world has gotten smaller and more familiar, dramatic Patagonia retains a feeling of being truly far away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/travel/chilean-patagonias-peaks-up-close.html', 'snippet': 'Even as the world has gotten smaller and more familiar, dramatic Patagonia retains a feeling of being truly far away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER four hours of strenuous hiking, we had only just reached the bottom of the Torres del Paine. You can see the three granite monoliths from seemingly a hundred miles away (and on just about every postcard of Patagonia), but the full magnitude of their facades was revealed only after the last turn on the mountainous trail. We sat down, panting, and looked across a glassy, marble-green lake at the summits, reaching more than 9,000 feet into the sky. Despite their size, being so near to them felt strangely intimate.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06NEXT1_SPAN/06NEXT1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06NEXT1_SPAN/06NEXT1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'ra