, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perdue, Beverly E', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T02:16:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/68eef90e-9405-5ce1-a80f-aa3764e7eadf', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68eef90e-9405-5ce1-a80f-aa3764e7eadf'}, {'abstract': 'The new prime minister may revise Japan’s apologies regarding World War II, which would inflame tensions in the region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/opinion/another-attempt-to-deny-japans-history.html', 'snippet': 'The new prime minister may revise Japan’s apologies regarding World War II, which would inflame tensions in the region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few relationships are as important to stability in Asia as the one between Japan and South Korea. Yet Japan’s new prime minister, Shinzo Abe, seems inclined to start his tenure with a serious mistake that would inflame tensions with South Korea and make cooperation harder. He has signaled that he might seek to revise Japan’s apologies for its World War II aggression, including one for using Koreans and other women as sex slaves. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Attempt to Deny Japan’s History', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Attempt to Deny Japan’s History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apologies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T02:19:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7e0f8fd-9fa7-5aeb-8485-d3d29ad37bda', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7e0f8fd-9fa7-5aeb-8485-d3d29ad37bda'}, {'abstract': 'A group empaneled by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo suggested, among other things, extending the number of school days in New York State and making teachers pass the equivalent of a bar exam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/nyregion/education-commission-recommends-core-reforms.html', 'snippet': 'A group empaneled by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo suggested, among other things, extending the number of school days in New York State and making teachers pass the equivalent of a bar exam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forcing teachers to pass a kind of bar exam, like the ones aspiring lawyers and doctors must sit for. Extending the number of hours and days students must spend in school, to break with academic calendars formed in an agrarian age. Consolidating school districts; making schools a hub for health care and social services; and giving 4-year-olds in the state’s poorest areas access to full-day prekindergarten.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commission Recommends Core Changes in Education', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Commission Recommends Core Changes In Education', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduation Rates', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T02:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2aed695e-1adc-5c34-ab5e-3d3c0028406f', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2aed695e-1adc-5c34-ab5e-3d3c0028406f'}, {'abstract': 'The unwillingness of House Republicans to work for the common good suggests that the 113th Congress will be bitterly unproductive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/opinion/dereliction-of-duty-more-fiscal-battles-ahead.html', 'snippet': 'The unwillingness of House Republicans to work for the common good suggests that the 113th Congress will be bitterly unproductive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans won more in the fiscal-cliff deal than they had any right to expect. Permanent tax cuts for the middle class and millions of wealthy families, a permanent break on the estate tax and dividends, a five-year limit on tax credits for low-income families, and only a single year of extended unemployment insurance. Yet 64 percent of House Republicans voted against it Tuesday night.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Battles Ahead', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Dereliction Of Duty', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The fiscal battles ahead are likely to be even harder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 