scal showdown drew to a close this week, as the president signed a bill to raise taxes on the wealthiest Americans. But the sense of relief among businesses was tempered by the anticipation of political battles to come. Next month, Congress is set to negotiate over raising the debt ceiling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bracing for the Next Political Standoff', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T12:45:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca5310d4-2220-52ca-aef4-05573c8ff775', 'word_count': 1688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca5310d4-2220-52ca-aef4-05573c8ff775'}, {'abstract': 'Narendra Modi, India’s would-be prime minister, has an exclusionary approach to governance aimed at marginalizing Muslims.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/the-hardships-of-being-muslim-under-indias-modi/', 'snippet': 'Narendra Modi, India’s would-be prime minister, has an exclusionary approach to governance aimed at marginalizing Muslims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DELHI — To think of Narendra Modi, the four-term chief minister of Gujarat, as a potential prime minister and the man who might revive the flagging fortunes of the B.J.P., India’s main opposition party, is to ignore the reality of Gujarat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Modi and the Muslims’ Malaise', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'ma