': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Cane and Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe369985-616d-55ed-82b4-83fc7497fb61', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe369985-616d-55ed-82b4-83fc7497fb61'}, {'abstract': 'In an interview, Shirley MacLaine, who is joining the “Downton Abbey” cast as a tart-tongued American, showed there was nothing she placed off-limits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/arts/television/shirley-maclaine-on-downton-abbey.html', 'snippet': 'In an interview, Shirley MacLaine, who is joining the “Downton Abbey” cast as a tart-tongued American, showed there was nothing she placed off-limits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS if the impending nuptials of Lady Mary and Matthew Crawley, not to mention all the social and economic upheaval of the early 1920s, were not enough to set servants scurrying and sherry glasses crashing in the decorous halls of “Downton Abbey,” that period drama is getting one more shock to its system when its third season begins Sunday on PBS’s “Masterpiece.” This year Shirley MacLaine joins its cast as Martha Levinson, the mother of Cora, Countess of Grantham (Elizabeth McGovern), who arrives from America bringing a tart tongue and liberal attitudes unfamiliar to her daughter’s aristocratic household.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06MCLAINE_SPAN/06MCLAINE_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'MacLaine’s Next Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'MacLaine’s Next Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacLaine, Shirley', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Downton Abbey (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T12:55:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b2e98b9-fa1e-50f1-a15c-206b390d003e', 'word_count': 1463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b2e98b9-fa1e-50f1-a15c-206b390d003e'}, {'abstract': '“Downton Abbey,” a quintessentially British dramedy of manners, has become a hit in a dozens of locales worldwide where viewers wouldn’t know a dowager from a dogsbody.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/arts/television/downton-abbey-reaches-around-the-world.html', 'snippet': '“Downton Abbey,” a quintessentially British dramedy of manners, has become a hit in a dozens of locales worldwide where viewers wouldn’t know a dowager from a dogsbody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOT long ago the actor Jim Carter replaced the crisp butler’s uniform he wears on “Downton Abbey” with a snug Lycra ensemble for a getting-away-from-it-all cycling trip in Cambodia. Shooting had ended on the third season, which begins Sunday on PBS, and he celebrated with a long ride along the Mekong River. Amid the ancient temples of Angkor Wat he found himself wilting in the steamy climate — and suddenly swarmed by a group of Asian tourists screaming, “Mr. Carson!”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06DOWNTOWN1_SPAN/06DOWNTOWN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06DOWNTOWN1_SPAN/06DOWNTOWN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06DOWNTOWN1_SPAN/06DOWNTOWN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 6