ado and Erica Berenstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mac', 'middlename': 'William', 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Pedro', 'middlename': 'Rafael', 'lastname': 'Rosado', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f5bb87b7-b361-5e5a-8212-a9d90b9f06ca', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f5bb87b7-b361-5e5a-8212-a9d90b9f06ca'}, {'abstract': 'By New Year’s Eve, 65 banks had registered their derivatives business with regulators and turned over heaps of real-time trading data to outside warehouses, fulfilling a centerpiece of the Obama administration’s financial regulatory crackdown.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/banks-face-new-checks-on-derivatives-trading/', 'snippet': 'By New Year’s Eve, 65 banks had registered their derivatives business with regulators and turned over heaps of real-time trading data to outside warehouses, fulfilling a centerpiece of the Obama administration’s financial regulatory crackdown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After spending two years and millions of dollars to temper a regulatory crackdown, the world’s biggest banks are now resigned to a wave of new oversight. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banks Face New Checks on Derivatives Trading', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-03T16:07:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf3e8355-a337-599d-b166-069c44cb52dc', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf3e8355-a337-599d-b166-069c44cb52dc'}, {'abstract': 'The brutal, beautiful vision of a great American storyteller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/magazine/george-saunders-just-wrote-the-best-book-youll-read-this-year.html', 'snippet': 'The brutal, beautiful vision of a great American storyteller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a little sushi restaurant in Syracuse, George Saunders conceded that, sure, one reality was that he and I were a couple guys talking fiction and eating avocado salad and listening to Alanis Morissette coming from the speaker above our heads. Another was that we were walking corpses. We’d been on the subject of death for a while. A friend I loved very much died recently, and I was trying to describe the state I sometimes still found myself in — not quite of this world, but each day a little less removed — and how I knew it was a good thing, the re-entry, but I regretted it too, because it meant the dimming of a kind of awareness that doesn’t get lit up very much. I was having some trouble articulating it, but Saunders was right there, leaning in and encouraging. He has a bushy blond mustache and goatee going gray, and sometimes, when he’s listening intently, he can look a little stern, as if he just stepped out of a tent at Antietam. But then he starts talking and the eyebrows go up and it’s all Chicago vowels and twinkly Doug Henning eyes, and if you didn’t know that he was more or less universally regarded as a genius, you might peg him as the superfriendly host of a woodworking show on daytime public access.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 466}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1164, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/magazine/06subsaunders1/06subsaunders1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'George Saunders Has Written the Best Book You’ll Read This Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "‘Stay Open, Forever, So Open It Hurts'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saunders, George', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovell, Joel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'