bject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T00:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randall S. Kroszner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randall', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Kroszner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/519f93e7-68d5-5c0c-9792-42b55f285eec', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/519f93e7-68d5-5c0c-9792-42b55f285eec'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a myth that a majority of our health care spending goes to the final year of life.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/better-if-not-cheaper-care/', 'snippet': 'It’s a myth that a majority of our health care spending goes to the final year of life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT is conventional wisdom that end-of-life care is an increasingly huge proportion of health care spending. I’ve often heard it said that people spend more on health care in the year before they die than they do in the entire rest of their lives. If we don’t address these costs, the story goes, we can never control health care inflation.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Better, if Not Cheaper, Care', 'kicker': 'Ezekiel J. Emanuel', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Better, if Not Cheaper, Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospice Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T00:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ezekiel J. Emanuel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ezekiel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Emanuel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebf0ecd4-0806-5f18-9ad3-4877a70dbc9a', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebf0ecd4-0806-5f18-9ad3-4877a70dbc9a'}, {'abstract': 'Toshifumi Fujimoto has been taking pictures with his cameras and a compact video camera in Aleppo, the heart of the Syrian conflict.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/japanese-man-vacations-on-syrian-front-lines/', 'snippet': 'Toshifumi Fujimoto has been taking pictures with his cameras and a compact video camera in Aleppo, the heart of the Syrian conflict.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toshifumi Fujimoto’s vacation pictures, posted on his Facebook page, show him in poses familiar to any tourist. He lines up with interesting people, tries local activities and shows the sights. Except in the case of Mr. Fujimoto, a Japanese tourist, that means images of what appear to be Syrian rebel fighters engaged in battle, of himself firing an assault rifle and of the corpses of some of the 60,000 people the United Nations has said have died in Syria since a civil war began there early last year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japanese Man Vacations on Syrian Front Lines', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T01:07:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b033f92-6127-5219-998e-3784df2b8655', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b033f92-6127-5219-998e-3784df2b8655'}, {'abstract': 'Dozens of people jump and fall to their deaths on New York City’s tracks every year, and many train operators say the images are vivid, even years later.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/nyregion/subway-deaths-haunt-those-at-trains-controls.html', 'snippet': 'Dozens of people jump and fall to their deaths on New York City’s tracks every year, and many train operators say the images are vivid, even years later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late one December morning in 2005, Tracy Moore was pulling her R train into the Steinway Street station in Queens. It is a sloping stretch of track, undulating “like a roller coaster,” Ms. Moore said. She was traveling about 30 miles per hour. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/nyregion/Subway/Subway-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/04/nyregion/Subway/Subway-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/nyregion/Subway/Subway-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/nyregion/Subway/Subway-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height':