 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Massachusetts Institute of Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T15:39:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1e75169-f7ce-53b9-9633-e87f6b2cc311', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1e75169-f7ce-53b9-9633-e87f6b2cc311'}, {'abstract': 'A new graduate-school graduate with a new job and a young child is worried about the long business trips (three to six weeks) that are the norm in the field. How do parents handle the travel?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/a-parents-business-travel-how-long-is-too-long/', 'snippet': 'A new graduate-school graduate with a new job and a young child is worried about the long business trips (three to six weeks) that are the norm in the field. How do parents handle the travel?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A newly working parent wrote me with a dilemma. At the very beginning of a post-graduate-school career, the family’s bread-winner (for now) and the parent of an 18-month-old, this parent is facing the fact that to really excel at work, travel will be required. Not short jaunts to nearby cities, but longer trips, three to six weeks at a time, to developing countries. Taking a spouse and child along is not an option. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Parent’s Business Travel: How Long Is Too Long?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T15:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8d29da0-17c4-5ceb-941b-97ba9b0586c2', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8d29da0-17c4-5ceb-941b-97ba9b0586c2'}, {'abstract': 'The action covers the Cadillac Escalade, Escalade ESV and Escalade EXT; Chevrolet Avalanche, Express, Silverado (1500 and heavy duty), Suburban and Tahoe; GMC Savana, Sierra (1500 and heavy duty) and the Yukon and Yukon XL. All are 2013 models.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/g-m-recalling-54700-vehicles-because-of-roll-away-danger/', 'snippet': 'The action covers the Cadillac Escalade, Escalade ESV and Escalade EXT; Chevrolet Avalanche, Express, Silverado (1500 and heavy duty), Suburban and Tahoe; GMC Savana, Sierra (1500 and heavy duty) and the Yukon and Yukon XL. All are 2013 models.', 'lead_paragraph': 'General Motors is recalling almost 54,700 of its 2013 trucks and sport utility vehicles because of a roll-away danger, according to a report the automaker filed Friday with the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'G.M. Recalling 54,700 Vehicles Because of Roll-Away Danger', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T15:50:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c35caef-6188-53b7-8544-8478d7c13b18', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c35caef-6188-53b7-8544-8478d7c13b18'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Sullivan talks about new media’s new Darwinism and making at least some readers pay.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/andrew-sullivan-on-going-back-to-future-as-an-indie-blogger/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Sullivan talks about new media’s new Darwinism and making at least some readers pay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday, Andrew Sullivan, one of the pioneers of the blogging Web, decided to end his relationship with The Daily Beast, and by the way, with advertising as well. His decision made quite a splash, in part because others wonder whether he is pointing a way forward at a time when advertising rates on the Web would not seem to support a gumball habit, let alone professionally produced content. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Andrew Sullivan on Going Back to Future as an Indie Blogger', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Andrew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Daily Beast, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T15:54:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/214a8920-2a6f-5a6c-8542-4cdc680856d0', 'word_count': 1347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/214a8920-2a6f-5a6c-8542-4cdc680856d0'}, {'abstract': 'For estate and gift taxes, all but the richest of the rich will probably be able to protect their holdings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/your-money/fiscal-deal-ends-decade-of-uncertainty-over-gift-and-estate-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'For estate and gift taxes, all but the richest of the rich will probably be able to protect their holdings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR many of the wealthy, the American Taxpayer Relief Act, passed this week by Congress, is aptly named. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/business/Wealth1/Wealth1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/05/business/Wealth1/Wealth1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 838}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'