T15:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Winerip', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winerip', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0495acd4-9cf0-510b-9011-6a4bb4aee5a1', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0495acd4-9cf0-510b-9011-6a4bb4aee5a1'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen, jazz critics for The Times, discuss the most noteworthy albums, performances and developments in jazz in 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/01/04/arts/music/04popcast_pod.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen, jazz critics for The Times, discuss the most noteworthy albums, performances and developments in jazz in 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen, jazz critics for The Times, discuss the most noteworthy albums, performances and developments in jazz in 2012.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Jazz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T16:03:00+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/ee1cdeed-63a1-5378-a1bd-48e7fd304993', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/ee1cdeed-63a1-5378-a1bd-48e7fd304993'}, {'abstract': 'Does the fiscal cliff deal increase or reduce the deficit? It depends on whom you ask.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/having-it-both-ways/', 'snippet': 'Does the fiscal cliff deal increase or reduce the deficit? It depends on whom you ask.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pretty much everyone inside and outside of Washington is disappointed in the fiscal cliff deal. Some argue it doesn’t do enough. Others think it goes too far. There’s even disagreement on a crucial point: Does the deal increase the deficit or reduce it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Having It Both Ways', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T16:04:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teresa Tritch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tritch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04730031-6d5e-52f4-85fa-aa718d0cf360', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04730031-6d5e-52f4-85fa-aa718d0cf360'}, {'abstract': 'Erick Erickson’s new agenda and the future of right-wing populism.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/a-populism-worthy-of-the-name/', 'snippet': 'Erick Erickson’s new agenda and the future of right-wing populism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A big part of the story of the fiscal cliff negotiations, now half-resolved and half-postponed, was that the populist wing of the Republican Party couldn’t quite figure what it wanted to actually achieve, or how. The broad goals of less spending and lower taxes were clear enough, but on anything more specific the politicians and activists on the grassroots right seemed confused about what they thought the party should to be negotiating toward, or what they expected that their calls for harder hardball and/or cliff-diving would actually win in the end. (The scenarios being spun out tended to either envision extracting never-gonna-happen concessions from the Democrats across weeks of brand-destroying brinksmanship, or embracing a truly massive defeat on taxes as a “the worse, the better” step toward eventual conservative resurgence.) This confusion was obvious throughout, but particularly during the brief window on Tuesday when there was talk of House Republicans sending the bill back to the Senate with some amendments tacked on. If there had been any kind of strategically-plausible consensus about how conservatives actually wanted the compromise amended, then that gambit might have made some sense. But there wasn’t, so it didn’t.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Populism Worthy of the Name', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T16:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39900246-4d48-5a9e-bda5-c51db71c4f79', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39900246-4d48-5a9e-bda5-c51db71c4f79'}, {'abstract': 'A Foreign Ministry spokeswoman said Chris Buckley’s visa application had been filed incorrectly and foreign news outlets were to blame for his departure from mainland China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/world/asia/china-says-reporter-chris-buckley-was-not-expelled.html', 'snippet': 'A Foreign Ministry spokeswoman said Chris Buckley’s visa application had been filed incorrectly and foreign news outlets were to blame for his departure from mainland China.', '