type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Russ Juskalian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Russ', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Juskalian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fce04bc9-74d5-5aa7-9de4-b2612b9fb3e6', 'word_count': 1855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fce04bc9-74d5-5aa7-9de4-b2612b9fb3e6'}, {'abstract': 'John Glassie’s subject, an intensely curious 17th-century thinker, tried to explain a rapidly changing world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/a-man-of-misconceptions-by-john-glassie.html', 'snippet': 'John Glassie’s subject, an intensely curious 17th-century thinker, tried to explain a rapidly changing world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists talk about “paradigm shifts” as if they happen suddenly. One day, there’s this paradigm. The next day, Bam! another one comes along. But, oh, how that leaves out the fun parts! The gray zones. The in-between periods of experimentation, play and openness. When it comes to the history of the so-called scientific revolution, that binary telling would certainly skip over one of history’s more bizarre and largely forgotten thinkers, the 17th-century Jesuit priest Athanasius Kircher.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106ABUMRAD/0106ABUMRAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106ABUMRAD/0106ABUMRAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1459, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106ABUMRAD/0106ABUMRAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106ABUMRAD/0106ABUMRAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106ABUMRAD/0106ABUMRAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mistakes? He Made a Few', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mistakes? He Made a Few', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abumrad, Jad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glassie, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kircher, Athanasius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T16:57:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jad Abumrad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abumrad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8edad45d-f72d-562b-b862-2617dc22bf4b', 'word_count': 1023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8edad45d-f72d-562b-b862-2617dc22bf4b'}, {'abstract': 'Combining various narrative styles, Royall Tyler translates a medieval Japanese saga of pride, romance and warfare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/the-tale-of-the-heike-translated-by-royall-tyler.html', 'snippet': 'Combining various narrative styles, Royall Tyler translates a medieval Japanese saga of pride, romance and warfare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Encountering Homer in a vivid translation made Keats feel like an astonished astronomer watching a new planet swim into view. Readers unfamiliar with medieval Japanese literature — and that must mean most of us — may feel a kindred excitement on first looking into “The Tale of the Heike,” in a taut new rendering by Royall Tyler. If “beauty and elegance,” as Tyler remarks, pervade the atmosphere of the earlier “Tale of Genji” (which he has also translated), the fall of the Heike warrior clan, during the late 12th century, calls to mind the riotous bloodletting of the “Iliad.” And just as Homer’s epic has inspired a thousand spinoffs, much of what draws us to Japanese culture — samurai and geisha, anime and manga, woodblock prints and Kurosawa films — comes right out of the exploits recounted in this book. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1349, 'width': 1890, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Benfey/0106-Benfey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Martial Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Martial Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benfey, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyler, Royall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T16:58:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a7d071c-ffbd-5f81-a3bd-4083c214f315', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a7d071c-ffbd-5f81-a3bd-4083c214f315'}, {'abstract': 'Kurt Weill and Lotte Lenya inhabited several countries and genres.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/love-song-the-lives-of-kurt-weill-and-lotte-lenya.html', 'snippet': 'Kurt Weill and Lotte Lenya inhabited several countries and genres.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time, not so long ago, when Kurt Weill was known mainly as the composer of a handful of hit songs, usually heard in versions far removed from their original, often transgressive contexts. Louis Armstrong, Frank Sinatra and Ella Fitzgerald transformed “Mack the Knife” into an upbeat jazz standard, and Bobby Darin made it a smooth pop hit. Broadway fans knew “September Song,” a survivor from the long-forgotten 1938 “Knickerbocker Holiday.” And the rock generation knew the Doors’ take on “Alabama Song,” unaware that it was all the rage in Berlin — and the centerpiece of an opera — in 1930.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 632, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1263, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106KOZINN/0106KOZINN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 