ennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c24458df-f97a-581a-8aac-1809218e4209', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c24458df-f97a-581a-8aac-1809218e4209'}, {'abstract': 'Her marriage in trouble, a young New York professional turns to the self-help book she loved as a child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/love-is-a-canoe-by-ben-schrank.html', 'snippet': 'Her marriage in trouble, a young New York professional turns to the self-help book she loved as a child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the book business, everyone gets slapped around. As Ben Schrank intimates in his energetic new novel, “Love Is a Canoe,” even writers who exude calm and wisdom often hide wounded souls. But in Schrank’s world, authors have nothing on Manhattan’s viciously seasoned professionals — publishers and editors whose corner offices harbor enough sad secrets, bruised egos and busted hearts to fill the slush pile for decades.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106BAKOPOULOS/0106BAKOPOULOS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 886, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106BAKOPOULOS/0106BAKOPOULOS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1738, 'width': 1504, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106BAKOPOULOS/0106BAKOPOULOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106BAKOPOULOS/0106BAKOPOULOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106BAKOPOULOS/0106BAKOPOULOS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Same Boat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Same Boat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schrank, Ben', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:01:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Bakopoulos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakopoulos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d540950-68ae-5b5b-a8ab-165b64d34bd9', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d540950-68ae-5b5b-a8ab-165b64d34bd9'}, {'abstract': 'Bernard Bailyn argues that the early settlers were a “mixed multitude” who created disparate American cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/the-barbarous-years-by-bernard-bailyn.html', 'snippet': 'Bernard Bailyn argues that the early settlers were a “mixed multitude” who created disparate American cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In theory, Bernard Bailyn should need no introduction. In actuality, the intellectual landscape is so fragmented that highly literate Americans may not know of this prolific Harvard historian, the recipient of two Pulitzers, a National Book Award, a National Humanities Medal and a Bancroft (the most important professional prize for American historians). Bailyn’s most influential work probably still is his “Ideological Origins of the American Revolution” (1967), which forever changed views of our nation’s beginnings. But my favorite is “The Ordeal of Thomas Hutchinson” (1974), a superb portrait of the last royal governor of Massachusetts, a conscientious but obtuse public servant whose inability to grasp his subjects’ resentments led to his becoming the most hated man in the colonies. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106MANN/0106MANN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106MANN/0106MANN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106MANN/0106MANN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106MANN/0106MANN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106MANN/0106MANN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Into the Wilderness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Into the Wilderness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bailyn, Bernard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:01:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles C. Mann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Mann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6fe4b70-513a-5c27-b2df-f70c0da5feed', 'word_count': 1854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6fe4b70-513a-5c27-b2df-f70c0da5feed'}, {'abstract': 'In Jojo Moyes’s novel, the young assistant to a quadriplegic devises adventures they can undertake together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/me-before-you-by-jojo-moyes.html', 'snippet': 'In Jojo Moyes’s novel, the young assistant to a quadriplegic devises adventures they can undertake together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I finished this novel, I didn’t want to review it; I wanted to reread it. Which might seem perverse if you know that for most of the last hundred pages I was dissolved in tears. Jojo Moyes, the writer who produced this emotional typhoon, knows very well that “Me Before You” — a novel that has already floated high on Britain’s best-seller lists — is, as British critical consensus affirms, “a real weepy.” And yet, unlike other novels that have achieved their mood-melting powers through calculated infusions of treacle — Erich Segal’s “Love Story” comes immediately to mind — Moyes’s story provokes tears that are redemptive, the opposite of gratuitous. Some situations, she forces the reader to recognize, really are worth crying over.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106SCHILLINGER/0106SCHILLINGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 875, 'widt