 'The New York Observer picks Ken Kurson to become its sixth editor in seven years.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/new-york-observer-hits-reset-again-names-ken-kurson-new-editor/', 'snippet': 'The New York Observer picks Ken Kurson to become its sixth editor in seven years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It will come as no surprise to those who follow the Manhattan media scene that The New York Observer has picked a new editor. After all, the newspaper has already had five editors in the seven years since Jared Kushner, a New York real estate developer, acquired the newspaper at the age of 25 in 2006.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Observer Hits Reset Again, Names Ken Kurson New Editor', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The New York Observer Names Author as Editor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kurson, Ken', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Jared', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Observer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0816156-17a7-5881-8fe3-ed064a2f6d52', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0816156-17a7-5881-8fe3-ed064a2f6d52'}, {'abstract': 'Physicists have applied their science (successfully) to some of the thorniest problems in economics, James Owen Weatherall argues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/the-physics-of-wall-street-by-james-owen-weatherall.html', 'snippet': 'Physicists have applied their science (successfully) to some of the thorniest problems in economics, James Owen Weatherall argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since the financial crisis, James Owen Weatherall writes in his new book, “The Physics of Wall Street,” “words like ‘quant,’ ‘derivative’ and ‘model’ have taken on some nasty connotations.” He is out to change that. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 702}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1649, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Norris/0106-Norris-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Solving for $', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Solving for $', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weatherall, James Owen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd58f733-db8a-5f98-a0bc-fef2558a8808', 'word_count': 1134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd58f733-db8a-5f98-a0bc-fef2558a8808'}, {'abstract': 'Originally published in 1933, Hans Keilson’s autobiographical first novel paints a bleak picture of life in Weimar Germany.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/life-goes-on-by-hans-keilson.html', 'snippet': 'Originally published in 1933, Hans Keilson’s autobiographical first novel paints a bleak picture of life in Weimar Germany.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Life Goes On” is both a very good first novel and a chilling reminder of the Nazis’ success at destroying Germany’s rich literary culture for a generation, if not longer. The author, Hans Keilson, wrote this lightly fictionalized account of his life about 80 years ago, when he was in his early 20s. To give just one example of how much the world has changed since then: Keilson tells us in an afterword that the impetus to start the novel was the fury he felt after he’d been turned away as a patient by the powerful Berlin Psychoanalytic Institute. (A few years later, the institute would be taken over by a cousin of Hermann Goering, and most of its analysts would flee to Palestine.) The publisher, the great S. Fischer Verlag, accepted the novel for publication in December 1932. The extremely poor and extremely elated Keilson took his advance and went skiing for the first time in his life. When he came back, the Reichstag was on fire, the Nazis had begun arresting Communists, and the publisher told Keilson to change his ending, which had a Communist twist. The novel appeared, with the altered ending, in 1933. It was banned in 1934. And that is the story, Keilson explains in the afterword, of “the last debut by a Jewish writer from the old S. Fischer Verlag.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Shulevitz-Sub/0106-Shulevitz-Sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Shulevitz-Sub/0106-Shulevitz-Sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1871, 'width': 1998, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Shulevitz-Sub/0106-Shulevitz-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Shulevitz-Sub/0106-Shulevitz-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Shulevitz-Sub/0106-Shulevitz-Sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Displaced Persons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Displaced Persons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shulevitz, Judith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keilson, Hans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Searls, Damion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:03:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Shulevitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shulevitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5fb6dc4-6267-54b6-b10e-b2e885ed936e', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5fb6dc4-6267-54b6-b10e-b2e885ed936e'}, {'abstract': 'How a man and his mother connected during her final illness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/the-end-of-your-life-book-club-by-will-schwalbe.html', 'snippet': 'How a man and his mother connected during her final illness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Mary Anne Schwalbe learned she had advanced pancreatic cancer, in 2007, her son Will suggested she start a blog to keep family and friends informed. Schwalbe liked the idea but worried it was “unseemly” to broadcast news about herself, so she asked her son to write it instead. The blog became “Will’s Mary Anne \xadSchwalbe News.” When Mary Anne decided it would be “easier” if she composed the first entry, she dictated in the third person for Will to type and post — a practice that continued until the blog’s, and Mary Anne’s, last days.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Beha/0106-Beha-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Beha/0106-Beha-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Beha/0106-Beha-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Beha/0106-Beha-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Beha/0106-Beha-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long Goodbye', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Long Goodbye', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwalbe, Will', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:03:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher R. Beha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Beha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/adcafe84-9846-58a1-aee7-88cc08358bfc', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adcafe84-9846-58a1-aee7-88cc08358bfc'}, {'abstract': 'This property, which opened last March, occupies a former brewery and includes a lounge stacked with 33,000 books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/travel/hotel-review-b2-boutique-hotel-spa-in-zurich.html', 'snippet': 'This property, which opened last March, occupies a former brewery and includes a lounge stacked with 33,000 books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE UPSHOT', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1601, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/travel/06CHECKIN/06CHECKIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hotel Review: B2 Boutique Hotel + Spa in Zurich', 'kicker': 'Check In, Check Out', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Zurich: The B2 Boutique Hotel + Spa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Zurich (Switzerland)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:03:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ratha Tep', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ratha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tep', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/573ad6ce-30ca-5fbd-abea-e14f0cd628ff', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/573ad6ce-30ca-5fbd-abea-e14f0cd628ff'}, {'abstract': 'Diana Wagman’s heroine encounters a deranged iguana owner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/diana-wagmans-care-and-feeding-of-exotic-pets.html', 'snippet': 'Diana Wagman’s heroine encounters a deranged iguana owner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Diana Wagman’s fourth novel, “The Care and Feeding of Exotic Pets,” is about a Los Angeles divorcée kidnapped by a deranged iguana owner, and it’s Wagman’s best to date — a quick, engaging story, half thriller and half wry domestic drama. Winnie’s ex-husband is a celebrity game-show host who wants to be a “serious” actor, with a yoga-teaching trophy wife and a longing for his surfing days. Their teenage daughter keeps busy resenting her mother and fabricating outrageous abuse stories for an older, online boyfriend she’s never met. Winnie herself, meanwhile, is stalled out in the center of a featureless plain of middle-aged torpor, with few desires or ambitions. (She would admit as much: her self-\xadassessment is down to earth.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Millet/0106-Millet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 644, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Millet/0106-Millet-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1090, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Millet/0106-Millet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Millet/0106-Millet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Millet/0106-Millet-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Housebroken', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Housebroken', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagman, Diana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:03:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Millet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Millet', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eed3bce9-6919-5c28-9bcf-c1c4235a91d0', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eed3bce9-6919-5c28-9bcf-c1c4235a91d0'}, {'abstract': 'In “Daddy Love,” Joyce Carol Oates writes about a young boy snatched from the parking lot of a mall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/daddy-love-by-joyce-carol-oates-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In “Daddy Love,” Joyce Carol Oates writes about a young boy snatched from the parking lot of a mall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After all these years, Joyce Carol Oates can still give me the creeps. And the wellspring of terror she taps in DADDY LOVE (Mysterious Press/Grove/Atlantic, $24) was recently brought home to the entire country — a child’s fear of losing a parent and a parent’s fear of losing a child. Five-year-old Robbie Whitcomb lives one form of that nightmare when he’s snatched from the parking lot of a mall in Ypsilanti, Mich., by a whiskered man who attacks the boy’s mother and drives over her in his van, leaving her a “pitiful broken thing.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Crime/0106-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 818, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/books/review/0106-Crime/0106-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1345, 'width': 1075, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'super