d_paragraph': 'THE MIDDLESTEINS,  by Jami Attenberg. (Grand Central, $24.99.) Attenberg’s heroine is killing herself through overeating, but this moving novel’s underlying subject is a suburban Jewish family, and how it reacts to the disaster unfolding in its midst.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:08:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b006ddb-b2b6-5a62-802d-ab8a0069d507', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b006ddb-b2b6-5a62-802d-ab8a0069d507'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALIBIS: Essays on Elsewhere,  by André Aciman. (Picador, $16.) These beguiling essays explore time, place and identity — from an extended aria on the memories evoked by the scent of lavender to elegiac meditations on Barcelona, Rome, Paris and New York. Aciman “is a spirited guide, sensitive to history but alive also to food, sunshine, art and aimless wandering,” Teju Cole wrote in the Book Review.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T17:08:11+0000', 'docu