nday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Maher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b204d17-112a-5d5d-b806-717db3f6aa9b', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b204d17-112a-5d5d-b806-717db3f6aa9b'}, {'abstract': 'Film Forum’s series “New Yawk New Wave” presents the city’s tradition of gritty experimentation, location shooting and cinéma vérité between 1953 and 1973.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/movies/the-new-yawk-new-wave-series-at-film-forum.html', 'snippet': 'Film Forum’s series “New Yawk New Wave” presents the city’s tradition of gritty experimentation, location shooting and cinéma vérité between 1953 and 1973.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE past 20 years or so have turned indie filmmaking into a brand as much as an ethos, and one side effect has been a certain amnesia. Long before indie became the trademark of a festival in Utah (much less a term at times as contentious as hipster), a revolution in cinema occurred on several fronts in New York. Shot on location, often on meager budgets, these films tapped into the city’s cultural and social energies: its streets, its people, its artistic fervor. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06YAWK4_SPAN/06YAWK4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06YAWK4_SPAN/06YAWK4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/arts/06YAWK4_SPAN/06YAWK4_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'j