-smiths-highest-compliment/', 'snippet': 'Ol Parker, screenwriter of “Best Exotic Marigold Hotel,” says the greatest moment on the set of that film involved Maggie Smith’s request for a rewrite.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 43, Ol Parker was not exactly the obvious choice to adapt the screenplay for “The Best Exotic Marigold Hotel,” about retirees in India, so when the producer Graham Broadbent approached him, he initially wondered, “Why me?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maggie Smith’s Highest Compliment', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Ol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T20:29:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74c86d0a-6a07-5313-bb54-b1604eae985e', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74c86d0a-6a07-5313-bb54-b1604eae985e'}, {'abstract': 'Writing a “Compare & Contrast” is not unlike sex in a marriage after three decades. Sometimes you gotta fake it till you make it.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/how-we-create-those-compare-contrast-columns/', 'snippet': 'Writing a “Compare & Contrast” is not unlike sex in a marriage after three decades. Sometimes you gotta fake it till you make it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric Spitznagel, who writes the Compare & Contrast column for The One-Page Magazine, asks and answers his own questions about his process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/magazine/04-lena-lean-cuisine/04-lena-lean-cuisine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 719, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/magazine/04-lena-lean-cuisine/04-lena-lean-cuisine-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 719, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/magazine/04-lena-lean-cuisine/04-lena-lean-cuisine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/04/magazine/04-lena-lean-cuisine/04-lena-lean-cuisine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/04/magazine/04-lena-lean-cuisine/04-lena-lean-cuisine-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 137, 'width': 138, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How We Create Those ‘Compare & Contrast’ Columns', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T20:31:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Spitznagel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spitznagel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e97d8ec8-b7f4-547d-be29-2e2c8b8d947b', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e97d8ec8-b7f4-547d-be29-2e2c8b8d947b'}, {'abstract': 'The distant past seems to fall into tidy categories. Not so the first decade of the 2000s, which we haven’t even agreed on a name for.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/fashion/what-will-we-miss-when-its-2033.html', 'snippet': 'The distant past seems to fall into tidy categories. Not so the first decade of the 2000s, which we haven’t even agreed on a name for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PICK a decade, any decade, from the 1920s to the 1990s. Say you’re throwing a theme party with all the sartorial, musical and follicular trimmings. The clichéd cultural signifiers spring to mind: Flappers in bob haircuts with cigarette holders doing the Lindy hop! Long-tressed hippies in jangly, rainbow-colored necklaces, acoustically strumming “For What It’s Worth”! Causeless rebels in white undershirts with slicked-back hair, milling around a mass of crew-cut men in gray flannel suits at the sock hop!', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/fashion/06DECADES_SPAN/06DECADES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/fashion/06DECADES_SPAN/06DECADES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/fashion/06DECADES_SPAN/06DECADES_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/fashion/06DECADES_SPAN/06DECADES_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1109, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/fashion/06DECADES_SPAN/06DECADES_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': '