al': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26502211-d4ad-5b11-80cd-e89105cb240c', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26502211-d4ad-5b11-80cd-e89105cb240c'}, {'abstract': 'A show of unity between rival Palestinian factions posed a diplomatic quandary for the United States, which considers Hamas a terrorist organization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/world/middleeast/fatah-celebration-in-gaza-signals-easing-of-rift-with-hamas.html', 'snippet': 'A show of unity between rival Palestinian factions posed a diplomatic quandary for the United States, which considers Hamas a terrorist organization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Turning the streets of Gaza City into a swarm of sunshine-yellow flags, hundreds of thousands of Palestinians celebrated the anniversary of the Fatah faction on Friday in the heartland of its militant Islamist rival, Hamas, the latest in a series of signals heralding possible reconciliation between the parties after their bitter five-year rift. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rally Signals Fatah’s Rift With Hamas May Ease', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rally in Gaza Hailing Fatah May Ease Rift', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudoren, Jodi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fatah, Al', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABBAS, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meshal, Khaled', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haniya, Ismail', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-04T22:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e537f0ed-f40e-5ba6-ad3e-59af5a5e9622', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e537f0ed-f40e-5ba6-ad3e-59af5a5e9622'}, {'abstract': 'As seas rise, some architects suggest, the next generation of waterfront designs should draw inspiration from the intricate ways that plants and animals have adapted to their situations.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/04/will-biomimicry-offer-a-way-forward-post-sandy/', 'snippet': 'As seas rise, some architects suggest, the next generation of waterfront designs should draw inspiration from the intricate ways that plants and animals have adapted to their situations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As neighborhoods devastated by Hurricane Sandy begin drafting plans for reconstruction, some progressive architects and urban planners are arguing that the emerging science of biomimicry offers a way forward. The notion is that the next generation of waterfront designs could draw inspiration from the intricate ways that plants and animals have adapted to their situations over hundreds of millions of years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Biomimicry Offer a Way Forward, Post-Sandy?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'k