onors on Friday, only a handful of companies were on the list of several hundred contributors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/us/politics/inaugural-contributors-identified.html', 'snippet': 'When President Obama’s inaugural planners released the names of donors on Friday, only a handful of companies were on the list of several hundred contributors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama drew criticism last month for allowing unlimited corporate donations to finance his inaugural festivities. But when his inaugural planners released the names of donors late Friday afternoon, only a handful of companies were on the list of several hundred contributors.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inaugural Contributors Identified', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inaugural Contributors Identified', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inaugurations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genentech Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Innovations Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'StreamLine LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whittier Trust Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T00:50:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b35a4b8-09a0-5f95-93b2-a1d0bb6e8255', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b35a4b8-09a0-5f95-93b2-a1d0bb6e8255'}, {'abstract': 'The authorities in São Paulo, Brazil’s most populous state, said they would start detaining addicts of crack cocaine and sending them against their will to treatment centers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/world/americas/brazil-state-to-make-addicts-get-help.html', 'snippet': 'The authorities in São Paulo, Brazil’s most populous state, said they would start detaining addicts of crack cocaine and sending them against their will to treatment centers.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The authorities in São Paulo, Brazil’s most populous state, said they would start detaining addicts of crack cocaine and sending them against their will to treatment centers. The new measure, which was announced Thursday and is set to be put into effect this month, is aimed at combating a crack epidemic visibly on display in blighted parts of the city of São Paulo, the state capital, where roving groups of addicts consume the drug in broad daylight. Officials across Brazil are searching for solutions to the problem, with Brazil ranking among the world’s largest cocaine markets. Security forces in Rio de Janeiro have been forcing crack addicts into treatment centers since 2011. On a federal level, President Dilma Rousseff’s government is moving forward with a $2 billion anti-crack initiative, including awareness campaigns and enhanced efforts to combat the trafficking of cocaine into Brazil from neighboring countries. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil: State to Make Addicts Get Help', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil: State to Make Addicts Get Help', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocaine and Crack Cocaine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T00:51:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b714f170-112c-509d-a7ae-f1b0b1443e7b', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b714f170-112c-509d-a7ae-f1b0b1443e7b'}, {'abstract': 'The F.T.C. decision permits Google to continue to use its own judgment in analyzing search requests and presenting pertinent results, a victory for online expression.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/opinion/is-google-like-gas-or-like-steel.html', 'snippet': 'The F.T.C. decision permits Google to continue to use its own judgment in analyzing search requests and presenting pertinent results, a victory for online expression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER a two-year investigation, the Federal Trade Commission concluded this week that Google’s search practices did not violate antitrust law. Those who wanted to see an epic battle like the one the government fought with Microsoft in the 1990s were sorely disappointed. But the analogy to the browser war of the Web’s early days was never the right one. It failed to capture the dangers free speech would have faced if regulators had agreed with Google’s critics.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/opinion/05op-ed-art/05op-ed-art-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/opinion/05op-ed-art/05op-ed-art-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/opinion/05op-ed-art/05op-ed-art-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/05/opinion/05op-ed-art/05op-ed-art-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/opinion/05op-ed-art/05op-ed-art-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Google Like Gas or Like Steel?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is Google Like Gas or Like Steel?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Bruce D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Associated Press', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Hugo L', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T00:53:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce D. Brown and Alan B. Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d4b8355-1f4d-51ad-8a02-32932daf7063', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d4b8355-1f4d-51ad-8a02-32932daf7063'}, {'abstract': 'The conductor Manfred Honeck made his debut with the New York Philharmonic at Avery Fisher Hall on Thursday with a program that mainly stuck to the staples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/arts/music/manfred-honeck-leads-new-york-philharmonic-at-avery-fisher.html', 'snippet': 'The conductor Manfred Honeck made his debut with the New York Philharmonic at Avery Fisher Hall on Thursday with a program that mainly stuck to the staples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given the success that the Austrian-born conductor Manfred Honeck has had as music director of the Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra, where he is now in his fifth season, it is surprising that it took until Thursday night for him to make his debut with the New York Philharmonic at Avery Fisher Hall. Yet for this career milestone appearance, Mr. Honeck stuck mainly to the staples. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/arts/05PHIL/05PHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/05/arts/05PHIL/05PHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/05/arts/05PHIL/05PHIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub