of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/2b909a67-2620-53dd-bf23-266a0fa171bb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/2b909a67-2620-53dd-bf23-266a0fa171bb'}, {'abstract': 'Workers in 10 states will get a raise this year, but a long overdue increase in the federal minimum wage would help many more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/opinion/meanwhile-at-the-bottom-of-the-wage-scale.html', 'snippet': 'Workers in 10 states will get a raise this year, but a long overdue increase in the federal minimum wage would help many more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly a million low-wage workers in 10 states will get a modest raise this year. In Rhode Island, a new law has raised the state’s minimum wage by 35 cents an hour, to $7.75, which will work out to an average annual raise of $510 for 11,000 Rhode Islanders. In nine other states — Arizona, Colorado, Florida, Missouri, Montana, Ohio, Oregon, Vermont and Washington — laws that peg the minimum wage to inflation will result in increases of 10 cents to 15 cents an hour, for hourly wages ranging from $7.35 in Missouri to $9.19 in Washington. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And at the Bottom of the Wage Scale ...', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And at the Bottom of the Wage Scale...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T02:22:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/672d849f-6f20-5617-af3a-f5c2257975bb', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/672d849f-6f20-5617-af3a-f5c2257975bb'}, {'abstract': 'Recent attacks, possibly by militants, are jeopardizing critical efforts to wipe out polio in Pakistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/opinion/the-war-on-pakistans-aid-workers.html', 'snippet': 'Recent attacks, possibly by militants, are jeopardizing critical efforts to wipe out polio in Pakistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The murders of charity and aid workers in Pakistan has dealt a devastating blow to a national campaign to wipe out polio and other deadly diseases. On Tuesday, assassins ambushed and killed seven Pakistani teachers and health workers, six of them women, as they returned home from work at a children’s community center in the Swabi district of the northwestern province of Khyber-Pakhtunkhwa. They were affiliated with a private Pakistani aid group, Support With Working Solution, that runs health and education projects in Pakistan’s poor and lawless tribal region.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The War on Pakistan’s Aid Workers', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The War on Pakistan’s Aid Workers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tehrik-e-Taliban', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poliomyelitis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Support With Working Solution', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T02:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c0cc2d8-1291-5b4c-a450-6a50a4018959', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c0cc2d8-1291-5b4c-a450-6a50a4018959'}, {'abstract': '“Georgia O’Keeffe in New Mexico,” an exhibition at the Montclair Art Museum, looks at the second third of the artist’s life, when she became engaged with Native American arts and culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/nyregion/a-review-of-georgia-okeeffe-in-new-mexico-at-the-montclair-art-museum.html', 'snippet': '“Georgia O’Keeffe in New Mexico,” an exhibition at the Montclair Art Museum, looks at the second third of the artist’s life, when she became engaged with Native American arts and culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much has been written about the early life of Georgia O’Keeffe: her role as  one of the first American abstract painters; her discovery by Alfred Stieglitz, who put on her first solo exhibition at his 291 Gallery in New York in 1917; their subsequent marriage; and the nude photographs Stieglitz took of O’Keeffe, which led many critics to interpret her paintings of flowers, bones and mountains as chiefly erotic — much to O’Keeffe’s dismay.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ARTSNJ2/06ARTSNJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1105, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ARTSNJ2/06ARTSNJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1105}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ARTSNJ2/06ARTSNJ