age Truck in Manhattan', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T04:56:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55e3c9b6-8bf7-543f-9d0f-3a086cbf6384', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55e3c9b6-8bf7-543f-9d0f-3a086cbf6384'}, {'abstract': 'Mark O’Connor, a violin composer, has his first meal on Sundays at lunch, works on perfecting his method in his “cave” apartment and may end the night playing fiddle in Greenwich Village with friends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/nyregion/mark-oconnor-violin-composer-finds-inspiration-in-his-cave.html', 'snippet': 'Mark O’Connor, a violin composer, has his first meal on Sundays at lunch, works on perfecting his method in his “cave” apartment and may end the night playing fiddle in Greenwich Village with friends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The career of Mark O’Connor, who won the Grand Master Fiddler Championship at age 13, has included his years as a much-sought-after session musician in Nashville, as a composer and as an educator. His “Improvised Violin Concerto” will have its premiere this spring, and he continues to write new volumes of the O’Connor Violin Method, an American-themed alternative to the Suzuki approach. Although he is often on the road or at a country house in Pennsylvania with his partner — also a string player — and their daughter, New York City is his base. On Sundays, Mr. O’Connor, 51, concentrates on writing and composing in his “cave,” an apartment on West 57th Street. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1405, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06ROUTINE2_SPAN/06ROUTINE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fiddler in the Cave', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fiddler in the Cave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connor, Mark", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T05:06:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Harrison Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Harrison', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8af3c1c0-39cf-53ea-b1d3-f66412f9119d', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8af3c1c0-39cf-53ea-b1d3-f66412f9119d'}, {'abstract': 'You wouldn’t think that New Year’s Eve would be the most romantic of holidays. But, at least according to the Internet, you would be wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/nyregion/craigslist-missed-connections-poetry-on-new-years-eve.html', 'snippet': 'You wouldn’t think that New Year’s Eve would be the most romantic of holidays. But, at least according to the Internet, you would be wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You wouldn’t think — with the noise, the crowds and the performance-impairing quantities of alcohol — that New Year’s Eve would be the most romantic of holidays. But, at least according to the Internet, you would be wrong. The following poems about love discovered — or lost — on New Year’s Eve were “found” last week in the Missed Connections section of newyork.craigslist.org. They are printed verbatim with only line and stanza breaks added; their titles are their subject headings.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06POETRY_SPAN/06POETRY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/06/nyregion/06POETRY_SPAN/06POETRY_SPAN-articleLarge.