or': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:25:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Morgenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morgenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79f622f6-164b-5b9e-8550-cf67ffa28550', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79f622f6-164b-5b9e-8550-cf67ffa28550'}, {'abstract': 'When picking a school, you can focus on ranking, reputation, ivy. Or you can ask yourself where you’ll really be forced to grow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/opinion/sunday/bruni-how-to-choose-a-college.html', 'snippet': 'When picking a school, you can focus on ranking, reputation, ivy. Or you can ask yourself where you’ll really be forced to grow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MY niece Leslie is still more than nine months away from sending in a college application and more than 18 from stepping into her first college class, but already she’s swimming in numbers: the average SAT scores for one university’s student body; the percentage of applicants another school admits; how much money, on average, the graduates of yet another school tend to make once they’ve been in the work force awhile. This is the kind of information spotlighted in the articles and books that are supposed to guide her and her peers. These are the types of factoids that the adults around them often focus on.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'xlarg