tps://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/dying-for-freedom/', 'snippet': 'Why did Lincoln ignore the refugee crisis unleashed by emancipation?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Emancipation Proclamation, which Abraham Lincoln signed on Jan. 1, 1863, was primarily a military tool. When he issued it in preliminary form in September 1862, it was meant to be a warning to the South: give up, or your slaves will be set free.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dying for Freedom', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dying for Freedom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emancipation Proclamation (1863)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c649562-88d0-5fda-aee6-457ca8074728', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c649562-88d0-5fda-aee6-457ca8074728'}, {'abstract': 'Expanding our ethical care to include all of humanity is a nice idea, but it involves a misunderstanding of the source of our empathy: emotions.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/the-myth-of-universal-love/', 'snippet': 'Expanding our ethical care to include all of humanity is a nice idea, but it involves a misunderstanding of the source of our empathy: emotions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that the year-end holidays have passed, so have the barrage of entreaties to nurture a sense of “good will to all mankind,” to extend our love and care to others beyond our usual circle of friends and family. Certainly, this is a message we are meant to take to heart not just in December but all year long. It is a central ideal of several religious and ethical systems. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Myth of Universal Love', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Myth Of Total Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Empathy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rifkin, Jeremy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singer, Peter (1946- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen T. Asma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Asma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0448ff1-d5f2-5a30-a111-9e9b78e77186', 'word_count': 2378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0448ff1-d5f2-5a30-a111-9e9b78e77186'}, {'abstract': 'A Tokyo-based sushi restaurant chain owner paid 155.4 million yen, or about $1.76 million, for a 488-pound bluefin, or about $3,600 per pound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/world/asia/new-high-for-tuna-at-tokyo-fish-sale.html', 'snippet': 'A Tokyo-based sushi restaurant chain owner paid 155.4 million yen, or about $1.76 million, for a 488-pound bluefin, or about $3,600 per pound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Tokyo’s main fish market ushered in the new year with an auction on Saturday that resulted in the highest price paid here, and probably anywhere, for a tuna.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New High for Tuna at Tokyo Fish Sale', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New High for Tuna At Tokyo Fish Sale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:55:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb79e4c2-e0c6-5e28-b20b-cd118c8334af', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb79e4c2-e0c6-5e28-b20b-cd118c8334af'}, {'abstract': 'I have a compulsion: I am addicted to looking things up.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/rapturous-research/', 'snippet': 'I have a compulsion: I am addicted to looking things up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may pity me, if you wish, but my compulsion is relatively mild. As a longtime publisher of scholarly and scientific reference works, I am addicted to looking things up.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rapturous Research', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rapturous Research', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:55:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean Pidgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pidgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b86d06e-4ada-56d3-8185-29899204af99', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b86d06e-4ada-56d3-8185-29899204af99'}, {'abstract': 'The Constitution gives the president alone the power to grant pardons. It is time for President Obama to use this singular responsibility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/opinion/sunday/the-quality-of-mercy-strained.html', 'snippet': 'The Constitution gives the president alone the power to grant pardons. It is time for President Obama to use this singular responsibility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barack Obama has rarely exercised presidential clemency to grant pardons and restore the civil rights of convicted criminals, a power that Abraham Lincoln, Franklin D. Roosevelt and other presidents used with dedication to correct injustices in the legal system. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Quality of Mercy, Strained', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Quality of Mercy, Strained', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Ronald L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:56:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f4d184d-5354-5598-acfd-8ba2fc0a3223', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f4d184d-5354-5598-acfd-8ba2fc0a3223'}, {'abstract': 'One of Ray Lewis’s former teammates, Trevor Pryce, writes about what makes the Ravens linebacker, who plans to retire after the season, extra special.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/ray-lewis-linebacker-leader/', 'snippet': 'One of Ray Lewis’s former teammates, Trevor Pryce, writes about what makes the Ravens linebacker, who plans to retire after the season, extra special.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To know Ray Lewis is to have a story about him. I have hundreds. But the first time I heard his name and the last time it is announced at M&T Bank Stadium are the two I think are the best.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ray Lewis: Linebacker, Leader', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Ray', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-05T18:57:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trevor Pryce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trevor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pryce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ebe4755-7096-5ec0-80a5-9b1d0e7bd3af', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ebe4755-7096-5ec0-80a5-9b1d0e7bd3af'}, {'abstract': 'The journalistic ideal of impartiality is coming under attack.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/06/public-editor/when-reporters-get-personal.html', 'snippet': 'The journalistic ideal of impartiality is coming under attack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BILL GRUESKIN remembers being an editor at The Wall Street Journal in 2004 when Farnaz Fassihi’s e-mail, meant for a few friends’ eyes only, began to circle the globe. Ms. Fassihi, an Iranian-American, was a reporter for The Journal, and the exposure of her views about the deteriorating situation in Iraq, provocative and incisive, was shocking. Published outside the normal bounds of painfully balanced journalism, her missive gave readers an unfiltered blast of reality.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/public-editor/MSullivan-2/MSullivan-2-articleLarge.png', 'height': 895, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/04/public-editor/MSullivan-2/MSullivan-2-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 895}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/public-editor/MSullivan-2/MSullivan-2-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 913, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/public-editor/MSullivan-2/MSullivan-2-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 913, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/public-editor/MSullivan-2/MSullivan-2-thumbStan