ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-06T23:01:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aa5363d-2158-556d-b4d4-c6dbf399ae36', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aa5363d-2158-556d-b4d4-c6dbf399ae36'}, {'abstract': '“The Dude and the Zen Master” is an incomplete and spotty guide to Zen philosophy, where sometimes the Dude-ism and the Buddhism mix in a helpful way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/07/books/jeff-bridges-and-bernie-glassmans-dude-and-the-zen-master.html', 'snippet': '“The Dude and the Zen Master” is an incomplete and spotty guide to Zen philosophy, where sometimes the Dude-ism and the Buddhism mix in a helpful way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I come late to everything. My first R.E.M. album was “Green.” I just started “Deadwood.” I have not seen the entire “Gangnam Style” video. The great exception to my tardiness is “The Big Lebowski.” While most fans of that Los Angeles story from Coen brothers about mistaken identity, German nihilists, Sabbath observance and bowling were straphangers on the bus — the movie was no hit when it was released — I grokked its genius on first viewing, at a cineplex in North Haven, Conn., in 1998. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/arts/subbookbridges/subbookbridges-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/arts/subbookbridges/subbookbridges-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 13