 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-07T07:38:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/538f253c-288e-50c8-989f-e510f67ca7ec', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/538f253c-288e-50c8-989f-e510f67ca7ec'}, {'abstract': 'Syria’s president speaks; banks get a reprieve; C.I.A. officer is sentenced: Qatar makes a play for the world stage; Indonesia seeks more religion in schools; the N.H.L. ends its lockout.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/07/iht-quick-read-jan-7/', 'snippet': 'Syria’s president speaks; banks get a reprieve; C.I.A. officer is sentenced: Qatar makes a play for the world stage; Indonesia seeks more religion in schools; the N.H.L. ends its lockout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWS Sounding defiant, confident and, to critics, out of touch, President Bashar al-Assad of Syria on Sunday used his first public address in six months to justify his crackdown, rally his supporters and leave recent efforts toward a political resolution of the civil war in tatters. Anne Barnard reports from Beirut. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/global-home/7syria-image/7syria-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/global-home/7syria-image/7syria-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 's