r a defiant speech on Sunday by President Bashar al-Assad of Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/world/middleeast/bashar-al-assads-speech-leaves-un-leader-disappointed.html', 'snippet': 'Ban Ki-moon, the United Nations secretary general, expressed dismay over a defiant speech on Sunday by President Bashar al-Assad of Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ban Ki-moon, the United Nations secretary general, expressed dismay over a defiant speech on Sunday by President Bashar al-Assad of Syria, in which Mr. Assad justified his attempts to crush the nearly two-year-old uprising against him, ignored demands that he step down to allow for a political transition and declared that his armed opponents were foreign puppets. In a statement released on Monday, Mr. Ban said Mr. Assad’s speech had not contributed “to a solution that could end the terrible suffering of the Syrian people.” Yet there was no indication that Lakhdar Brahimi, the special Syria envoy from the United Nations and the Arab League, would abandon his efforts. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Assad Speech Leaves U.N. Leader Disappointed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | United Nations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assad Speech Leaves U.N. Leader Disappointed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ban Ki-moon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T01:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4fffd85-791d-5017-b9f7-786bc103dab6', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4fffd85-791d-5017-b9f7-786bc103dab6'}, {'abstract': 'The patriarch of the Russian Orthodox Church encouraged Russians to adopt orphans, as the country adjusts to a decision to bar Americans from adopting Russian children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/world/europe/russia-orthodox-leader-presses-for-more-adoptions.html', 'snippet': 'The patriarch of the Russian Orthodox Church encouraged Russians to adopt orphans, as the country adjusts to a decision to bar Americans from adopting Russian children.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The patriarch of the Russian Orthodox Church used his Christmas address on Monday to encourage Russians to adopt orphans, as the country adjusts to a decision to bar Americans from adopting Russian children. The ban, which is retaliation for American legislation intended to punish Russian human rights violators, drew criticism, including from some government officials, largely because 120,000 children in Russia are awaiting adoption. “How important it is that our people should gladly, with a special feeling of gratitude to God, take orphans into their families,” Patriarch Kirill I said in a recorded address broadcast for Orthodox Christmas on Monday. “We should not have orphans in our country,” he added. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Orthodox Leader Presses for More Adoptions', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Orthodox Leader Presses for More Adoptions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADOPTIONS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Orthodox Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Orphans and Orphanages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirill I', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T01:53:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8a7cc0a-62b2-58f5-8435-e53889b68423', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8a7cc0a-62b2-58f5-8435-e53889b68423'}, {'abstract': 'Weld North plans to expand Organic Avenue, a juice and raw foods purveyor with eight stores in the New York area, into cities like Los Angeles and Miami and gain distribution in grocery stores and other retail outlets.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/07/weld-north-takes-controlling-stake-in-juice-chain/', 'snippet': 'Weld North plans to expand Organic Avenue, a juice and raw foods purveyor with eight stores in the New York area, into cities like Los Angeles and Miami and gain distribution in grocery stores and other retail outlets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The juice cleanse is all the rage, even in investment circles. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weld North Takes Controlling Stake in Juice Chain', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juices', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T02:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64d4dfb1-1fd6-50ae-bfd9-8f8f7c05d072', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64d4dfb1-1fd6-50ae-bfd9-8f8f7c05d072'}, {'abstract': 'An argument for the double standard, abetted by a new book about fairness.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/07/favoritism-is-good/', 'snippet': 'An argument for the double standard, abetted by a new book about fairness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the columns I have written that have been negatively received, none has been greeted with more outrage than last March’s “Two Cheers for Double Standards.” The case for a single standard is familiar and easy to make. If you’re in a position either to dispense rewards (a promotion, a bonus, an appointment) or level sanctions, you should do so impartially; that is, without being influenced in your decision by family membership, friendship, religious affiliation, ethnic solidarity or any of the other considerations that can skew judgment. When a comrade and someone in the other camp engage in the same behavior, your praise or blame should be independent of the personal feelings you may have for either. Don’t give your friends a pass you wouldn’t give to your enemies. Don’t give your brother the job if someone else not related to you at all is more qualified.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Favoritism Is Good', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T02:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/193b9db8-9ece-55bd-9e32-2b42d5e128c5', 'word_count': 1496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/193b9db8-9ece-55bd-9e32-2b42d5e128c5'}, {'abstract': 'The Consumer Electronics Show officially begins on Tuesday, but a tension that has run through the show for the last several years was already on display Monday, as many companies held press conferences showing off their newest gadgets.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/07/the-c-e-s-reality-gap/', 'snippet': 'The Consumer Electronics Show officially begins on Tuesday, but a tension that has run through the show for the last several years was already on display Monday, as many companies held press conferences showing off their newest gadgets.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Consumer Electronics Show in Las Vegas officially begins on Tuesday, but a tension that has run through the show for the last several years was already on display Monday, as many companies held news conferences showing off their newest gadgets. In many cases, the companies were touting larger, clearer — and more expensive — televisions. On the one hand, these televisions were impressive technological achievements; on the other, it is unclear how relevant they are to the average consumer. (Brian X. Chen wrote about how the television industry is struggling with this dynamic on Monday.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/technology/07bits-ces/07bits-ces-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/07/technology/07b