': 'Steven Petrow, the author of “Steven Petrow’s Complete Gay & Lesbian Manners,” (Workman, 2011), addresses questions about gay and straight etiquette for a boomer-age audience.  Send questions for Civil Behavior to stevenpetrow@earthlink.net. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/08/booming/08civil-booming-heirlooms/08civil-booming-heirlooms-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 837, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/08/booming/08civil-booming-heirlooms/08civil-booming-heirlooms-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1273, 'width': 1557, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/08/booming/08civil-booming-heirlooms/08civil-booming-heirlooms-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/08/booming/08civil-booming-heirlooms/08civil-booming-heirlooms-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/08/booming/08civil-booming-heirlooms/08civil-booming-heirlooms-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Closing the Heirloom Gender (and Gay) Gap', 'kicker': 'Civil Behavior', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T13:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Petrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5804afe-ba9e-5972-b262-1488cf8f6c3b', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5804afe-ba9e-5972-b262-1488cf8f6c3b'}, {'abstract': 'A common parallelism problem arises when two modifiers are used together, connected by a conjunction, but one is a phrase and the other is a full clause with its own verb. The effect is awkward and jarring. In careful writing, the two elements should be grammatically parallel or should be reworked into a single clause. Once the problem is diagnosed, the fix is usually simple.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/parallel-problems-2/', 'snippet': 'A common parallelism problem arises when two modifiers are used together, connected by a conjunction, but one is a phrase and the other is a full clause with its own verb. The effect is awkward and jarring. In careful writing, the two elements sho...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A common parallelism problem arises when two modifiers are used together, connected by a conjunction, but one is a phrase and the other is a full clause with its own verb. The effect is awkward and jarring. In careful writing, the two elements should be grammatically parallel or should be reworked into a single clause. Once the problem is diagnosed, the fix is usually simple. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parallel Problems', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T13:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ddc42b0-e5b3-5604-8a70-170152e89e11', 'word_count': 1499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ddc42b0-e5b3-5604-8a70-170152e89e11'}, {'abstract': '“Downton Abbey” began it third season on PBS by drawing nearly 8 million viewers; Led Zeppelin may be coming to an online streaming service soon; the canceled ABC soap operas “All My Children” and “One Life to Live” are given a new lease on life; and a new advertising campaign begins for the Crohn’s and Colitis Foundation of America.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/the-breakfast-meeting-strong-ratings-for-downton-and-new-life-for-two-soaps/', 'snippet': '“Downton Abbey” began it third season on PBS by drawing nearly 8 million viewers; Led Zeppelin may be coming to an online streaming service soon; the canceled ABC soap operas “All My Children” and “One Life to Live” are given a new lease on life; ...', 'lead_paragraph': '“Downton Abbey” began it third season on PBS by drawing nearly 8 million viewers to local public broadcasting stations. As Brian Stelter reports, preliminary ratings suggest that the show catapulted PBS into the same league as commercial broadcasters like ABC and NBC — at least for a night. With 7.9 million viewers, the premiere on Sunday night “quadrupled the average PBS primetime rating” and exceeded the average rating of the second season premiere by 96 percent, according to PBS and member station WGBH. While “Downton Abbey” was on, PBS outrated Fox, ABC and NBC, according preliminary Nielsen ratings. (CBS still ranked No. 1 for the night with episodes of “The Good Wife” and “The Mentalist” that had 10 and 10.7 million viewers, respectively.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Strong Ratings for ‘Downton,’ and New Life for Two Soaps', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colitis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Crohn's Disease", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings (Audience Measurement)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soap Operas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brenneman, Amy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kwatinetz, Jeff', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deezer', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Directors Guild of America', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DraftFCB', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Led Zeppelin', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rdio', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rhapsody.com', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 20, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WGBH', 'rank': 21, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T13:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'origin