till exist, DNA analysis of the carrion that flies eat could help guide research.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/in-flies-stomachs-vital-clues-to-biodiversity/', 'snippet': 'If camera traps and other tools cannot pick up on small mammals that still exist, DNA analysis of the carrion that flies eat could help guide research.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How many mammal species live in the forest? It sounds like a simple question, but the actual distributions of shy, small or rare mammals are often murky, confounding conservationists seeking to protect them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Flies’ Innards, Vital Clues to Biodiversity', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cameras', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mammals', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ivory Coast', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madagascar', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T13:07:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Yandell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yandell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea6b93a8-dd97-