'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7640cd7b-0355-5eff-b928-c4d1745137da', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7640cd7b-0355-5eff-b928-c4d1745137da'}, {'abstract': 'The release of Ali Harzi dramatized the lack of progress in any investigation into the attack on Sept. 11 that killed the American ambassador to Libya and three other Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/world/africa/lone-suspect-held-in-benghazi-attack-is-freed-in-tunisia.html', 'snippet': 'The release of Ali Harzi dramatized the lack of progress in any investigation into the attack on Sept. 11 that killed the American ambassador to Libya and three other Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The only known suspect jailed in connection with the deadly attack on the United States diplomatic mission in Benghazi, Libya, was freed on Tuesday by the Tunisian authorities who had held him. His lawyer said he was released for lack of evidence.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lone Suspect Held in Benghazi Attack Is Freed in Tunisia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lone Suspect Held in Benghazi Attack Is Freed in Tunisia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirkpatrick, David D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevens, J Christopher', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harzi, Ali Awni al- (d 2015)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:26:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/898d8481-78f4-5755-83c3-153addc6664b', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/898d8481-78f4-5755-83c3-153addc6664b'}, {'abstract': 'Israel unveils a digital logo for itself, in the hope of replacing its image as a warzone with a new reputation for innovation and edginess.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/extreme-makeover-israel/', 'snippet': 'Israel unveils a digital logo for itself, in the hope of replacing its image as a warzone with a new reputation for innovation and edginess.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEL AVIV — When you’re in Tel Aviv — touring its busy beach cafés by day or its busy bars at night — it’s easy to think of Israel as an attractive place. As of last week, Israel is also an attractive place with an attractive logo: the Foreign Ministry has just released “ISRAEL,” a multicolored rebranded look for the country’s name.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Extreme Makeover, Israel', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Logos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walt, Stephen M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tel Aviv (Israel)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:37:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b1ddbd2-6759-5895-90f5-c1d25793a059', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b1ddbd2-6759-5895-90f5-c1d25793a059'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (13, 49) THE EDUCATION OF MICHELLE RHEE “Frontline” examines the legacy of Ms. Rhee, right, the former chancellor of the Washington public schools and one of the most admired and reviled education reformers in the country. In a report released on Monday, StudentsFirst, the advocacy group Ms. Rhee leads, issued failing grades to 12 states on their public school policies. Her memoir, “Radical: Fighting to Put Students First,” is to be published next month.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:43:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfc66961-3422-5fb0-a1c7-55585da9f3a8', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfc66961-3422-5fb0-a1c7-55585da9f3a8'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/05/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) THE THREE STOOGES (2012) Will Sasso stars as Curly, Sean Hayes is Larry, and Chris Diamantopoulos is Moe (pictured above, from left) in this fictionalized origin story for the mostly lovable fools, and paean to the art of the eye poke, from Peter and Bobby Farrelly. Divided into “episodes,” the tale follows the Stooges from infancy — when they’re tossed out of a speeding car at the feet of a nun, Sister Mary-Mengele (Larry David) — to adulthood, when they’re forced to try to save the orphanage in which they were reared from the faltering economy and other calamities. Jane Lynch plays the Mother Superior who sends them out into the world, where they meet a femme fatale (Sofia Vergara) and her lover (Craig Bierko); the handsome guy from the Old Spice commercials (Isaiah Mustafa); and some of the cast of “Jersey Shore.” “The three leads in ‘The Three Stooges’ play dumb very well, particularly Mr. Sasso,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. She added: “Much of the pleasure in ‘The Three Stooges’ comes from watching and hearing (the boings and thumps are terrific) grown men smack each other silly in Rube Goldberg-like formations and without suffering so much as a single black eye, enduring psychological damage or, as bad, being forced to change. Like Wile E. Coyote and those inflatable clowns that bounce back after every punch, the Three Stooges take plenty of hits but keep on coming. They are, as the Farrellys understand, testaments to human resilience, one slap and tickle at a time.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "What's On Today", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/411d9ac9-5201-5270-ab3e-6b531a4a7cde', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/411d9ac9-5201-5270-ab3e-6b531a4a7cde'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/03/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:30 P.M. (13) AT THE PALEY CENTER In “She’s Making News,” this series focuses on the role of women in journalism and the innovative use of media through interviews with Christiane Amanpour, Maria Bartiromo, Gayle King, Soledad O’Brien, Paula Zahn and, in this premiere episode, Katie Couric, above. Pat Mitchell, the president and chief executive of the Paley Center for Media, hosts each episode, which recounts the guest’s life story using examples of her work culled from the center’s collection.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e67ea8e-978f-5fe2-bf1d-d96100b01fe0', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e67ea8e-978f-5fe2-bf1d-d96100b01fe0'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/02/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HGTV) COUSINS ON CALL The Kitchen Cousins — the real-life cousins and best friends Anthony Carrino and John Colaneri, above, of Brunelleschi Construction — deliver a new show in which they surprise the owners of outdated homes with fresh renovations. In this series premiere, the men team up with Ellen DeGeneres to make over the house of Megan and Jonathan, volunteer first responders in New Jersey whose home was destroyed while they were helping others during Hurricane Sandy. But the cousins have only five days to complete the job before the project is revealed to the couple on “The Ellen DeGeneres Show.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:46:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4d205a7-014a-5175-96fe-64bb07771cb5', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4d205a7-014a-5175-96fe-64bb07771cb5'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/30/arts/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:30 P.M. (MTV) SCOTT PILGRIM VS. THE WORLD (2010) In this film, billed as “an epic of epic epicness,” Scott Pilgrim (Michael Cera, above) falls for Ramona Flowers (Mary Elizabeth Winstead) and must fight, outwit and defeat her evil ex-boyfriends, played by Chris Evans, Brandon Routh and Jason Schwartzman, among others. Edgar Wright directed and wrote, with Michael Bacall, this comedy based on the graphic novels by Bryan Lee O’Malley. Writing in The New York Times, A. O. Scott said that the film offers “fast cuts, off-kilter puns, sight gags and sound effects in such profusion that you may want to see it again as soon as it’s over.” He added: “At the end, the movie comes home to the well-known territory of the coming-of-age story, with an account of lessons learned and conflicts resolved. But you’ll swear you’ve never seen anything like it before.” ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T15:47:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a1cb2b6-a833-5001-b8cb-b1e677d66338', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a1cb2b6-a833-5001-b8cb-b1e677d66338'}, {'abstract': 'TimesCast: The global effort to keep Bashar al-Assad from using chemical weapons. | Gretchen Morgenson on how big banks win on foreclosure abuse. | A Chicago neighborhood endures economic hardship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/100000001992378/rare-cooperation-over-syria.html', 'snippet': 'TimesCast: The global effort to keep Bashar al-Assad from using chemical weapons. | Gretchen Morgenson on how big banks win