orator’s case for black emigration during the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By late 1862 Washington was abuzz with talk of colonization. Bolstered by a $600,000 appropriation from Congress over the summer, Abraham Lincoln prepared a contract to resettle African-Americans in Panama and openly pitched the scheme to a free black delegation at the White House on Aug. 14. Few were surprised when Lincoln pledged to see colonization through in the preliminary announcement of his Emancipation Proclamation on Sept. 22, but a lingering question remained. Lincoln stipulated that all colonization was to be voluntary, though as of yet few had indicated an interest or willingness to go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Separate Peace', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-08T17:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip W. Magness', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Magness', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82942bc3-6b4a-5428-ab3f-0e55c9d45a29', 'word_count': 1597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82942bc3-6b4a-5428-ab3f-0e55c9d45a29'}, {'abstract': 'Alabama’s championship football team is a study in homegrown success, with roughly half of its roster from in-state and t