rvention and treatment, not drawn-out court punishments, work best.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/08/for-drug-users-hope-in-a-swift-response/', 'snippet': 'An initiative in Vermont has shown that, for drug offenders, quick intervention and treatment, not drawn-out court punishments, work best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben W. Joseph was a trial judge in the Chittenden County Criminal Court, in Vermont, and he had a problem — one shared by judges across the country that results in billions of dollars of wasteful spending by governments every year. It was 2008, and substance abuse was soaring, particularly among young people whose drugs of choice were pharmaceuticals like OxyContin, a highly addictive pain killer that has an effect similar to heroin but is much easier to obtain. Each month in his Burlington courtroom, Joseph saw defendants who’d been arrested for drug-related offenses — from driving while heavily intoxicated to stealing to feed an OxyContin habit (an 80-milligram pill can cost $100 on the street).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Drug Users, a Swift Response Is the Best Medicine', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T02:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53d5d5e7-6a29-574d-8686-b7e0df30d34b', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53d5d5e7-6a29-574d-8686-b7e0df30d34b'}, {'abstract': 'The American contractor, hired by the military to provide translation services, has agreed to pay a total of $5 million to scores of detainees at the Abu Ghraib prison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/world/middleeast/contractor-settles-case-in-iraq-prison-abuse.html', 'snippet': 'The American contractor, hired by the military to provide translation services, has agreed to pay a total of $5 million to scores of detainees at the Abu Ghraib prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — An American contractor hired by the military to provide translation services for interrogators at the Abu Ghraib prison in Iraq has reached a $5 million settlement with scores of detainees who accused its employees of complicity in abusing them, according to financial disclosure documents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Contractor Settles Case in Iraq Prison Abuse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Contractor Settles Case in Iraq Prison Abuse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cushman, John H Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense Contracts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ABU GHRAIB (IRAQ)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T02:35:39+00