by agency executives, CBS has sold most of its Super Bowl spots for an average of $3.7 million to $3.8 million for each 30 seconds. (By comparison, the average for Super Bowl XLVI, broadcast by NBC on Feb. 5, 2012, was $3.5 million.) Leslie Moonves, chairman and chief executive of the CBS Corporation, made the announcement at an event at the CBS Broadcast Center on the West Side of Manhattan that was billed as Super Bowl XLVII Media Day. Mr. Moonves, as Stuart Elliott reports, said room could still be found to include commercials for movie studios — which often wait somewhat longer than other marketers to decide whether to buy Super Bowl commercials — but at a price. “If one of those movie companies wants to come in and pay five or six million,” he said, “we will find room.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Super Bowl Ads and a Bet by HBO on ‘Enlightened’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dern, Laura', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerger, Paula', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moonves, Leslie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Naegle, Sue', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'WHITE, MIKE', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Bro