ns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuomo and Legislative Leaders Say They’re Close to Deal on New Gun Laws', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the State Message (NYS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T17:56:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan and Danny Hakim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Danny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hakim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4db7eef0-963b-5233-af49-cb8d0986eb92', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4db7eef0-963b-5233-af49-cb8d0986eb92'}, {'abstract': 'The Sanitation Department’s annual curbside collection, in which Christmas trees are turned into compost, will end on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/pack-up-your-christmas-trees-recycling-program-ends-saturday/', 'snippet': 'The Sanitation Department’s annual curbside collection, in which Christmas trees are turned into compost, will end on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s an urban version of the tree-falling-in-the-forest question: If you don’t leave your Christmas tree at the curb by Saturday, will it be recycled? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pack Up Your Christmas Trees: Recycling Program Ends Saturday', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas Trees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sanitation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T17:56:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fd88b5d-304c-5558-b1c2-80b866e82b5a', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fd88b5d-304c-5558-b1c2-80b866e82b5a'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Khuzami, a former terrorism prosecutor who revamped the Securities and Exchange Commission’s enforcement unit after the financial crisis, is stepping down from the agency, ending a four-tenure that included significant actions against some of Wall Street’s largest firms.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/s-e-c-enforcement-chief-khuzami-steps-down/', 'snippet': 'Robert Khuzami, a former terrorism prosecutor who revamped the Securities and Exchange Commission’s enforcement unit after the financial crisis, is stepping down from the agency, ending a four-tenure that included significant actions against some ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'After becoming the public face of Wall Street oversight and reining in firms like Goldman Sachs, Robert S. Khuzami is walking away from the spotlight.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Khuzami, S.E.C. Enforcement Chief Who Reinvigorated Unit, to Step Down', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Khuzami, S.E.C. Enforcement Chief Who Reinvigorated Unit, to Step Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khuzami, Robert S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T17:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ea7d089-edcf-5bca-9c35-6740c8764986', 'word_count': 1457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ea7d089-edcf-5bca-9c35-6740c8764986'}, {'abstract': 'The terms were stark: an investor wanted 75 percent of the company. In return, he offered a $250,000 infusion of capital and the opportunity to earn back 15 percent of the business if certain financial goals were met.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/why-the-founder-of-a-start-up-chose-to-give-up-control/', 'snippet': 'The terms were stark: an investor wanted 75 percent of the company. In return, he offered a $250,000 infusion of capital and the opportunity to earn back 15 percent of the business if certain financial goals were met.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, we published a case study about a start-up founded by a young Australian expatriate who faced a difficult choice — just how difficult was revealed by the scores of conflicting reader comments that poured in to this blog. The choice belonged to Jacqui Rosshandler, who was about to run out of money: she could mothball her breath freshener business, Eatwhatever, and find a job, or she could accept the financial lifeline offered by Arthur Shorin, longtime owner of the Topps Company, who had sold his company and was nurturing start-ups.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the Founder of a Start-Up Chose to Give Up Control', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Start-Up Founder\r\nGives Up Control', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosshandler, Jacqui', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Topps Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T18:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Grossmann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grossmann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28f4fdb0-a409-5d50-bfdf-809718695905', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28f4fdb0-a409-5d50-bfdf-809718695905'}, {'abstract': 'The musical revue is scheduled to open on Broadway in the fall.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/cotton-club-parade-will-march-onto-broadway/', 'snippet': 'The musical revue is scheduled to open on Broadway in the fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After two popular engagements at City Center, “Cotton Club Parade” – a musical revue celebrating the Harlem nightclub during the Duke Ellington era – will move to Broadway this fall, its producers announced on Wednesday. With minimal spoken text, this 90-minute show recreates many of the big-band swing and blues numbers of the Cotton Club during the 1920s and ‘30s, with the score including works by Jimmy McHugh and Dorothy Fields (“I Can’t Give You Anything but Love”), Harold Arlen (“Stormy Weather,” “I’ve Got the World on a String”) and Ellington (“Rockin’ in Rhythm,” “Cotton Club Stomp”).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Cotton Club Parade’ Will March Onto Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Cotton Club Parade’ to March to Broadway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARLEN, HAROLD', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlyle, Warren', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellington, Duke', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fields, Dorothy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsalis, Wynton', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Center Encores', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Center Theater', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T18:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ff10c4d-3214-5e07-b69b-fc7725de93a1', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ff10c4d-3214-5e07-b69b-fc7725de93a1'}, {'abstract': 'Billed as a new “Modern Family,” the NBC presidential-family sitcom “1600 Penn” seems more like “Growing Pains,” a time capsule of the Ford, Carter and Reagan eras.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/arts/television/1600-penn-on-nbc-with-pullman-gad-and-elfman.html', 'snippet': 'Billed as a new “Modern Family,” the NBC presidential-family sitcom “1600 Penn” seems more like “Growing Pains,” a time capsule of the Ford, Carter and Reagan eras.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something funny about “1600 Penn,” but mostly in the sense of odd. It’s an NBC sitcom on Thursday about a fictional first family that the network is promoting as a blend of “Modern Family” and “The West Wing.” Actually “1600 Penn” seems more like a time capsule of the presidencies of Gerald Ford, Jimmy Carter and Ronald Reagan and period sitcoms like “Growing Pains.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/09/arts/penn2/penn2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thu