{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denver (Colo)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T23:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/c4e675a9-9989-5551-95e1-622617a5ac4c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/c4e675a9-9989-5551-95e1-622617a5ac4c'}, {'abstract': 'The Pig on the Wings stool pays homage to Meret Oppenheim and Pink Floyd.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/garden/a-stool-inspired-by-pink-floyd.html', 'snippet': 'The Pig on the Wings stool pays homage to Meret Oppenheim and Pink Floyd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A meeting of a Swiss Surrealist and a British rock band in the imagination of an Italian designer was the genesis for Pig on the Wings. The cloven-hoof stool by Michael Breschi, founder of the Milan-based (and soon to be Miami-based) studio Gentle Giants, pays homage to Meret Oppenheim’s 1939 Traccia table, whose legs terminate in a pair of bird feet. And the name is a slightly scrambled reference to “Pigs on the Wing,” a two-part love song on Pink Floyd’s 1977 “Animals” album. (Mr. Breschi listened to “Animals” a lot while designing the piece.)', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10CURR5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 893, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10CURR5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1174, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':