o the bulb, ready to be released with another squeeze.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10ROADTEST_SPAN/10ROADTEST_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Casting Off the Yolk', 'kicker': 'Currents | Road Test', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Casting Off the Yolk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T23:48:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74ac99ae-e29a-5bbd-ac55-4530efa4f617', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74ac99ae-e29a-5bbd-ac55-4530efa4f617'}, {'abstract': 'An unlucky man and his friends live in a kind of down-and-out glamour in “Beijing Flickers,” directed by Zhang Yuan and presented as part of Global Lens 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/movies/beijing-flickers-directed-by-zhang-yuan.html', 'snippet': 'An unlucky man and his friends live in a kind of down-and-out glamour in “Beijing Flickers,” directed by Zhang Yuan and presented as part of Global Lens 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I regret eating glass,” San Bao, the depressed young hero of Zhang Yuan’s “Beijing Flickers,” says in a voice-over, the bottom half of his face covered in a white bandage. More things he regrets: checking into a hospital, where he can’t pay the bill, and also checking out of the hospital. After all, life in the big city isn’t going so well. His girlfriend has left him (for a richer man), he’s lost his job, and his dog — Lucky, no less — has run away. He can’t even kill himself properly — a suicide attempt on a train track turns into a Buster Keaton gag. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1297, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/arts/10FLICKERS/10FLICKERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Deadpan Gaze at the Down and Out in China', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Deadpan Gaze at the Down and Out in China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-09T23:50:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7223f535-b2fe-537a-9766-45811aca207a', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7223f535-b2fe-537a-9766-45811aca207a'}, {'abstract': '“Isn’t It Romantic: Contemporary Design Balancing Between Poetry and Provocation” opens at the Museum of Applied Art in Cologne.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/garden/isnt-it-romantic-a-contemporary-design-show-opens-in-cologne.html', 'snippet': '“Isn’t It Romantic: Contemporary Design Balancing Between Poetry and Provocation” opens at the Museum of Applied Art in Cologne.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can modernist design be dreamy? A show that runs Tuesday through April 21 at MAKK, the Museum of Applied Art in Cologne, Germany, makes a strong case for the affirmative. “Isn’t It Romantic: Contemporary Design Balancing Between Poetry and Provocation” focuses on what its curator, Tulga Beyerle, considers the softer side of a rigorous aesthetic movement. “Design is escaping from functionalism,” Ms. Beyerle said. “Young designers are critical of industrial production.” Included are projects by newcomers like Julien Carretero from France (his ladder, above right, which is made from oak excavated from a medieval Dutch city wall, will appear in a section devoted to concepts), but also work by established designers who have turned away from modernist strictures. Among the veteran offerings are Maurizio Galante’s Cactus sofa for Cerruti Baleri (above left) and Studio Makkink & Bey’s Birdwatch cabinet (above center). The catalog (about $32) is available at dermuseumsshop.de. Information: 011-49-221-2212-3860. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10SHOWS_SPAN/10SHOWS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/10/garden/10SHOWS_SPAN/10SHOWS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'ran