California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:32:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c3364e3-7195-59bf-a6b6-67c952924223', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c3364e3-7195-59bf-a6b6-67c952924223'}, {'abstract': 'New watered-down rules suggest banks are again dictating public policy in ways that could risk another crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/opinion/a-step-backward-in-bank-regulations.html', 'snippet': 'New watered-down rules suggest banks are again dictating public policy in ways that could risk another crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A committee of central bankers and regulators from more than two dozen countries, including the United States, has disappointingly given in to lobbying by big banks and watered down important rules meant to strengthen the global financial system. The change will let banks include risky financial instruments like corporate bonds and mortgage-backed securities as part of their liquid asset reserves, which are meant to cover up to 30 days of cash outflows during crises. And the banks have until 2019, not 2015, to comply fully with the easier standards. Each nation will decide when and how to implement the rules.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Step Backward in Bank Regulations', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Step Backward in Bank Regulations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Basel Committee on Banking Supervision', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Mervyn A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:32:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f66eb98-27e7-5549-8f37-e8ac47d057d4', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f66eb98-27e7-5549-8f37-e8ac47d057d4'}, {'abstract': 'A pack of feral dogs may be responsible for the deaths of as many as five people in a Mexico City park, the authorities said Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/world/americas/mexico-killer-dog-case-widens.html', 'snippet': 'A pack of feral dogs may be responsible for the deaths of as many as five people in a Mexico City park, the authorities said Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A pack of feral dogs may be responsible for the deaths of as many as five people in a Mexico City park, the authorities said Wednesday. Last week, the police said dogs had killed four people, including a woman and a baby boy, whose mutilated bodies were found in the park in December. Now, prosecutors are investigating if the wounds found on a fifth person, a 15-year-old girl who died in December, are consistent with dog bites. The authorities have captured several dogs, spurring an online campaign to save them, but it is not clear if any are the culprits. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Killer Dog Case Widens', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Killer Dog Case Widens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:35:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karla Zabludovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zabludovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/156e6bda-e740-58bc-a5a5-0aeb32f357a0', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/156e6bda-e740-58bc-a5a5-0aeb32f357a0'}, {'abstract': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission said auditors failed to review an audit of the bank holding company TierOne, which failed because of bad loans to real estate developers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/business/sec-seeks-to-penalize-2-auditors-in-bank-case.html', 'snippet': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission said auditors failed to review an audit of the bank holding company TierOne, which failed because of bad loans to real estate developers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In its first case against auditors stemming from the financial crisis, the Securities and Exchange Commission on Wednesday took action against two KPMG employees who had given a clean audit opinion to a Nebraska-based bank holding company that later failed because of bad loans it had made to real estate developers in Nevada and Florida.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.E.C. Seeks to Penalize 2 Auditors in Bank Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'S.E.C. Seeks To Penalize 2 Auditors In Bank Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KPMG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d54938bd-3a88-5619-9422-00217e84ce94', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d54938bd-3a88-5619-9422-00217e84ce94'}, {'abstract': 'The businessman convicted in an embezzlement and vote-buying scheme tied Brazil’s former president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, received a 40-year sentence in October.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/world/americas/brazil-prosecutor-to-analyze-testimony-by-suspect-about-former-president.html', 'snippet': 'The businessman convicted in an embezzlement and vote-buying scheme tied Brazil’s former president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, received a 40-year sentence in October.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The prosecutor general, Roberto Gurgel, said Wednesday that he would analyze testimony in which a businessman convicted in an embezzlement and vote-buying scheme tied Brazil’s former president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, to the scandal. The businessman, Marcos Valério de Souza, received a 40-year sentence in October after he was found to have operated much of the scheme, the mensalão, or “big monthly allowance,” named after payments received by legislators. The Supreme Court’s trial of suspects in the scheme resulted in the conviction of senior figures, including Mr. da Silva’s former chief of staff, who was sentenced to nearly 11 years in prison. In testimony, Mr. de Souza said he deposited funds in 2003 for Mr. da Silva’s “personal spending,” in an account controlled by another aide to the former president. After Mr. de Souza’s assertion, Mr. da Silva said he could not “respond to a lie.” Mr. Gurgel said no decision had been made on a formal investigation.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil: Prosecutor to Analyze Testimony by Suspect About Former President', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil: Prosecutor to Analyze Testimony By Suspect About Former President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, Simon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Da Silva, Luiz Inacio Lula', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/35c51827-543d-5bdc-a3c7-ac172f5e2a14', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35c51827-543d-5bdc-a3c7-ac172f5e2a14'}, {'abstract': 'To block extremists, Speaker John Boehner should open up votes to lawmakers of both parties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/opinion/democracy-in-the-house.html', 'snippet': 'To block extremists, Speaker John Boehner should open up votes to lawmakers of both parties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The only reason that income taxes on 99 percent of Americans did not go up this month was that Speaker John Boehner briefly broke with an iron rule of Republican control over the House. He allowed the fiscal-cliff deal to be put to a full vote of the House even though a strong majority of Republicans opposed it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Democracy in the House', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Democracy in the House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hastert, J Dennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:42:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae3b5ffd-637b-5c3d-8409-a1af3ea523bd', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae3b5ffd-637b-5c3d-8409-a1af3ea523bd'}, {'abstract': 'Wesley Wang’s information helped convict 10 people, prosecutors said in praising his assistance.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/09/probation-for-ex-sac-analyst-who-cooperated-in-insider-trading-inquiries/', 'snippet': 'Wesley Wang’s information helped convict 10 people, prosecutors said in praising his assistance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A former analyst at SAC Capital Advisors who has become a crucial informant in the government’s insider trading investigations avoided prison on Wednesday after a judge sentenced him to probation.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Probation for Ex-SAC Analyst Who Cooperated in Insider Trading Inquiries', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Probation for Ex-SAC Analyst Who Cooperated in Insider Trading Inquiries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SAC Capital Advisors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T01:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d5c573e-3522-5b59-b84c-d163d911362b', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d5c573e-3522-5b59-b84c-d163d911362b'}, {'abstract': 'The tea company is spending an estimated $10 million to lure 20- and 30-year-olds to its black tea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/business/media/liptons-campaign-returns-to-its-core-black-tea.html', 'snippet': 'The tea company is spending an estimated $10 million to lure 20- and 30-year-olds to its black tea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BACK in the “Mad Men” days, a trade organization known as the Tea Council of the U.S.A. celebrated the beverage its members sold with ads that carried the theme “Take tea and see.” ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/business/Adco/Adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/10/business/Adco/Adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 841}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013