 {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mid-Atlantic States (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T17:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5280730e-8643-573d-adf7-55594d6bc763', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5280730e-8643-573d-adf7-55594d6bc763'}, {'abstract': 'Arrogant and incompetent, or a Civil War hero wronged by a petty former commander?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/10/the-spectacular-rise-and-fall-of-fitz-john-porter/', 'snippet': 'Arrogant and incompetent, or a Civil War hero wronged by a petty former commander?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 10, 1863, a military tribunal of nine Union generals found Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter guilty of two charges stemming from his actions at the Second Battle of Manassas the previous August. Eleven days later, Porter was dismissed from the Army and “forever disqualified from holding any office or trust or profit under the Government of the United States.” After signing the order cashiering Porter, President Lincoln remarked, “In any other country but this, the man would have been shot.” His uncharacteristically harsh assessment reveals the controversy and political machinations that surrounded Porter’s court martial as well as the lingering drama accompanying the recent removal of his friend and patron, George McClellan, from command of the Union armies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spectacular Rise and Fall of Fitz John Porter', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T17:19:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Beard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87b0b1e9-9a04-507d-9568-bb9e2ba8a139', 'word_count': 1582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87b0b1e9-9a04-507d-9568-bb9e2ba8a139'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s William Neuman reports on the quasi-presidential inauguration in Venezuela that President Hugo Chávez is unable to attend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/americas/100000001996985/swearing-in-postponed-for-ill-chavez.html', 'snippet': 'The Times’s William Neuman reports on the quasi-presidential inauguration in Venezuela that President Hugo Chávez is unable to attend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times’s William Neuman reports on the quasi-presidential inauguration in Venezuela that President Hugo Chávez is unable to attend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/world/video-tc10-130110-chavez/video-tc10-130110-chavez-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/10/world/video-tc10-130110-chavez/video-tc10-130110-chavez-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/10/world/video-tc10-130110-chavez/video-tc10-130110-chavez-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/10