 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13brooks-SUB/13brooks-SUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tribal Lessons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tribal Lessons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diamond, Jared', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The World Until Yesterday: What Can We Learn From Traditional Societies? (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indigenous People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T21:29:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b64ec197-dbe4-56d2-9e69-d9854cf69498', 'word_count': 1349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b64ec197-dbe4-56d2-9e69-d9854cf69498'}, {'abstract': 'At the Museum of Modern Art, the Japanese video artist Meiro Koizumi explores the idea of blindness in the installation “Defect in Vision,” part of the new exhibition “Projects 99.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/arts/design/projects-99-meiro-koizumi-at-museum-of-modern-art.html', 'snippet': 'At the Museum of Modern Art, the Japanese video artist Meiro Koizumi explores the idea of blindness in the installation “Defect in Vision,” part of the new exhibition “Projects 99.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be hard to find another work of contemporary art that protests war with such soulful grace and understated wit as Meiro Koizumi’s “Defect in Vision” (2011), the main attraction of a small show at the Museum of Modern Art. Projected in somewhat different versions on opposite sides of a single screen in lustrous black and white, Mr. Koizumi’s 12-minute video meditates on the doomed, myth-saturated mind of Japan’s warrior class in the last days of World War II. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11KOIZUMI_SPAN/11KOIZUMI_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Screen, Two Points of View', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Screen, Two Points of View', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koizumi, Meiro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-10T21:34:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f31ffd74-3e2a-563e-ae35-d8ece01e4066', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f31ffd74-3e2a-563e-ae35-d8ece01e4066'}, {'abstract': 'What about the painters who had turned the American landscape into a national symbol? “The Civil War and American Art” explores how they responded to the bloodshed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/arts/design/the-civil-war-and-american-art-smithsonian-american-art.html', 'snippet': 'What about the painters who had turned the American landscape into a national symbol? “The Civil War and American Art” explores how they responded to the bloodshed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — “It is a beautiful country. I never had the pleasure of seeing it before.” So said the abolitionist John Brown gazing out over the Shenandoah Valley in Virginia, on a December morning in 1859. Americans have always reacted that way to their native land. It was their great possession. Europe had history stretching back into time. America had earth, here and now all potential, paradisally green.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11WARART3_SPAN/2DSUBWARART3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11WARART3_SPAN/2DSUBWARART3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/11WARART3_SPAN/2DSUBWARART3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '