for an enigmatic French artist who may hold insights into her mother’s long-ago death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:13:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/6385abf0-65d1-5c40-ab0e-4abedb0e42de', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6385abf0-65d1-5c40-ab0e-4abedb0e42de'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE TWELVE TRIBES OF HATTIE,  by Ayana Mathis. (Knopf, $24.95.) Mathis’s deeply felt first novel works at the rough edges of history, within a brutal and poetic allegory of a black family beset by tribulations after the Great Migration to the North.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/15745a40-6e2c-568a-ba85-6168b4e2d4f6', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15745a40-6e2c-568a-ba85-6168b4e2d4f6'}, {'abstract': 'In these essays, Mary Ruefle muses on language, writing and speaking sentences that last lifetimes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/madness-rack-and-honey-by-mary-ruefle.html', 'snippet': 'In these essays, Mary Ruefle muses on language, writing and speaking sentences that last lifetimes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last summer and fall, the Museum of Modern Art displayed a work called “Tapestry of the Thousand Longest Rivers in the World.” A witty, complex piece by an Italian artist named Alighiero e Boetti, it deserved the long look that museum\xadgoers gave it. But visitors who saw the work during a previous exhibition, in 2008, were at least as tickled by the text of an accompanying plaque: “In 1968 Alighiero Boetti changed his name by inserting an ‘e’ (‘and’ in Italian) between his first and last names to indicate that he (and by extension, anyone) was not a single but a multiple self.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KIRBY/13KIRBY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 712, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KIRBY/13KIRBY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1424, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KIRBY/13KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KIRBY/13KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KIRBY/13KIRBY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Priests of the Invisible', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Priests of the Invisible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruefle, Mary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:14:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kirby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f0a28e9-8a25-526f-8ad3-ebfb683fc006', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f0a28e9-8a25-526f-8ad3-ebfb683fc006'}, {'abstract': 'William Styron’s letters, co-edited by his wife, offer a full portrait of the writer and the man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/selected-letters-of-william-styron.html', 'snippet': 'William Styron’s letters, co-edited by his wife, offer a full portrait of the writer and the man.', 'lead_paragraph': '“For a person whose sole burning ambition is to write — like myself — college is useless beyond the sophomore year,” William Styron wrote to his father in 1946, after returning from Platoon Commander School in Quantico, Va., to resume his studies at Duke. Styron would go on to graduate — he was a nice boy, and eager to please his doting father — but he wasn’t kidding about his monomaniacal focus on writing, and in many ways the early pages of this splendid book are the hardest. They are very earnest, albeit not in the Hemingway sense — that is to say, there’s nothing here about getting one’s leg nearly blown off in Italy or falling in love with one’s nurse or trout fishing in Michigan. In the young Styron’s case, it was all but entirely about the writing. Happily, his diligence soon bore fruit: by Page 21 his mentor at Duke, William Blackburn, has already brought Styron’s work to the attention of Hiram Haydn, the editor at Bobbs-Merrill who would eventually publish Styron’s first novel, “Lie Down in Darkness,” in 1951, when the author was only 26.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 779}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1579, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13BAILEY/13BAILEY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Styron Visible', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Styron Visible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Styron, William', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Styron, Rose', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilpin, R Blakeslee', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Blake Bailey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Blake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bailey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b51ef583-b1f7-56a1-b12a-245a3bb1f834', 'word_count': 1692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b51ef583-b1f7-56a1-b12a-245a3bb1f834'}, {'abstract': 'Decades after the cold war, nuclear weapons are an established fact of regional and global strategy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/five-myths-about-nuclear-weapons-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Decades after the cold war, nuclear weapons are an established fact of regional and global strategy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the striking features of our long coexistence with nuclear weapons is how the fear of them has receded. In the 1950s and ’60s, when Hiroshima and Nagasaki were vivid memories, these novel and monstrous weapons loomed over our politics and penetrated our popular culture. The nuclear doomsday thriller “On the Beach” filled us with visceral dread. The dark satire of “Dr. Strangelove” played on real concerns. The laughter provoked by the twisted genius of Tom Lehrer was nervous laughter:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1626, 'width': 1528, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/13KELLER/13KELLER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rethinking the Unthinkable', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rethinking the Unthinkable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bracken, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keller, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Ward', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patrikarakos, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e125ae77-f8b3-54fe-9401-b1483081224f', 'word_count': 1750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e125ae77-f8b3-54fe-9401-b1483081224f'}, {'abstract': 'Philip Hensher’s Bengali narrator looks back on his childhood during Bangladesh’s struggle for independence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/scenes-from-early-life-by-philip-hensher.html', 'snippet': 'Philip Hensher’s Bengali narrator looks back on his childhood during Bangladesh’s struggle for independence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere in the middle of “Scenes From Early Life,” Philip Hensher’s circuitous new novel of wartime Bangladesh, a lawyer and his wife are arguing over the chilies and tomatoes and mangos that have been left out to dry on their balcony.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Sethi/0113-Sethi-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 765, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Sethi/0113-Sethi-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1947, 'width': 1455, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Sethi/0113-Sethi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Sethi/0113-Sethi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Sethi/0113-Sethi-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dacca Dreams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dacca Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sethi, Ali', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hensher, Philip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:18:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ali Sethi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ali', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sethi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/237e2d18-e476-5f95-b112-859525049740', 'word_count': 1158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/237e2d18-e476-5f95-b112-859525049740'}, {'abstract': 'The narrator of Howard Jacobson’s novel is a frustrated writer who laments life in a “postliterate age.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/zoo-time-by-howard-jacobson.html', 'snippet': 'The narrator of Howard Jacobson’s novel is a frustrated writer who laments life in a “postliterate age.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Words are everything to Guy Ableman, a novelist for whom prose celebrates “our lower instincts” and is therefore greater than poetry. His subject is sex (a giddy reviewer praises one of his novels as “a verbal spermfest”), and when he’s not actually writing he’s preparing to, by “mouth-\xadwriting” on solitary London walks. Despite being an accomplished and celebrated writer with a beautiful wife and an equally beautiful mother-in-law, he is deeply unhappy. Why? Because Guy Ableman has been born into a postliterate age.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Willett/0113-Willett-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Willett/0113-Willett-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Willett/0113-Willett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Willett/0113-Willett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/books/review/0113-Willett/0113-Willett-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ableman Unbound', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ableman Unbound', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobson, Howard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:18:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jincy Willett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jincy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f75a761-291e-5852-b302-00ed27fcb721', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f75a761-291e-5852-b302-00ed27fcb721'}, {'abstract': 'Two books for middle schoolers depict children of World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/gingersnap-and-navigating-early.html', 'snippet': 'Two books for middle schoolers depict children of World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here are two new novels by prizewinning authors, both set during World War II and featuring a brother in the armed services who is missing in action. Both are for middle-grade readers, though one is more or less intended for girls and the other for boys. Patricia Reilly Giff is the author of many books for children, two of which (“Lily’s Crossing” and “Pictures of Hollis Woods”) have won Newbery Honor awards. This is Clare Vanderpool’s second novel; her first, “Moon Over Manifest,” won the Newbery Medal in 2011.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Front', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Home Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giff, Patricia Reilly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vanderpool, Clare', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Griswold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griswold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45da361a-a781-59de-905c-fa00f665998f', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45da361a-a781-59de-905c-fa00f665998f'}, {'abstract': 'In Gayle Forman’s young adult novel, an American teenager in Europe falls for an elusive actor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/just-one-day-by-gayle-forman.html', 'snippet': 'In Gayle Forman’s young adult novel, an American teenager in Europe falls for an elusive actor.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Just One Day,” the new novel by Gayle Forman (author of the well-received “If I Stay” and “Where She Went”), has an epigraph from “As You Like It”: “All the world’s a stage, and all the men and women merely players.” The story of Allyson, a “good girl” on a European tour, and Willem, an adventurous Dutch actor, is about playing and playacting in all their many senses. After seeing him perform in “Twelfth Night,” Allyson impulsively accepts Willem’s invitation to spend a day together in Paris. He calls her Lulu, the nickname of the silent film actress Louise Brooks, and neither asks her real name nor discloses much about himself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Midnight in Paris', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Midnight in Paris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forman, Gayle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:19:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Priscilla Gilman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Priscilla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a5f5ded-704d-5899-807f-ed31770bbc0e', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a5f5ded-704d-5899-807f-ed31770bbc0e'}, {'abstract': 'The hero of this middle-grade fantasy novel possesses special talents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/books/review/jinx-by-sage-blackwood.html', 'snippet': 'The hero of this middle-grade fantasy novel possesses special talents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Creating a middle-grade fantasy world comes at the price of scrutiny and comparison. There are so many on the shelves these days; any new one must work hard to justify its existence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Wizard in Training', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Wizard in Training', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blackwood, Sage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T15:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ridley Pearson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ridley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pearson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'org