h milk — a vast improvement over its gluelike competitors. Hershey added more and more milk to his confection, until he’d transformed his candy into a buttery dollop. Consumers went wild for it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 938, 'width': 1508, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/magazine/13wmt/13wmt-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Hershey Bar?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Hershey Bar)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Pagan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hershey Foods Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hershey, Milton Snavely (1857-1945)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T16:21:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d39f562-cd2a-5649-bc12-f891d2daeb05', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d39f562-cd2a-5649-bc12-f891d2daeb05'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not the e-mail address. It’s the unexpected things you can do with an e-mail address.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/when-should-a-child-get-an-e-mail-account/', 'snippet': 'It’s not the e-mail address. It’s the unexpected things you can do with an e-mail address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I created an e-mail address for my 11-year-old two years ago. My now 8-year-old got one at 7, as a reward for completing a “reading chart” for second grade. (If I’d known she wanted one as badly as she did, I’d have set the bar much higher.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Should a Child Get an E-Mail Account?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T16:26:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3100ceb-e2c5-5ebf-b130-6dc783d91f08', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3100ceb-e2c5-5ebf-b130-6dc783d91f08'}, {'abstract': 'Recent excavations have uncovered fragments indicating that the terracotta head in question was illicitly removed in the 1970s from a site of a sanctuary of Demeter.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/getty-to-return-ancient-artifact-to-sicily/', 'snippet': 'Recent excavations have uncovered fragments indicating that the terracotta head in question was illicitly removed in the 1970s from a site of a sanctuary of Demeter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After two years of research, the J. Paul Getty Museum has decided to voluntarily return a terracotta head believed to depict the god Hades to Sicily, where recent excavations have uncovered fragments indicating that the head was illicitly removed in the 1970s from a site of a sanctuary of Demeter.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getty to Return Ancient Artifact to Sicily', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getty Decides to Return Ancient Artifact to Sicily', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tempelsman, Maurice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value':