agraph': 'The information technology sector, which once disdained paying dividends to shareholders, now pays more dividends than any other sector in the Standard & Poor’s 500.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Charts: High Tech, High Dividends', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Standard & Poor's 500-Stock Index", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T17:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/948845e9-0cd4-5c31-993e-a53972a75d14', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/948845e9-0cd4-5c31-993e-a53972a75d14'}, {'abstract': 'Someone Still Loves You Boris Yeltsin, an indie pop band originally from Missouri, has been invited to play a couple of gigs in Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/bands-name-punches-its-ticket-to-russia/', 'snippet': 'Someone Still Loves You Boris Yeltsin, an indie pop band originally from Missouri, has been invited to play a couple of gigs in Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sometimes a witty name can take you places. Just ask the American indie pop band Someone Still Loves You Boris Yeltsin. On Sunday, the quartet will headline the Old New Rock Festival in Yekaterinburg, Russia. Boris Yeltsin, the former Russian president, was a native of the region around Yekaterinburg.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Band’s Name Punches Its Ticket to Russia', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Someone Loves a Band Named for Boris Yeltsin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N