 Long Island. Items for the calendar should be sent at least three weeks in advance to lical@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events on Long Island', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Island', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T23:25:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b88f38c-df7d-5b0b-bbee-b075bf83c313', 'word_count': 3329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b88f38c-df7d-5b0b-bbee-b075bf83c313'}, {'abstract': 'The Hall of Fame voting brought up an inadvertent point: members of the news media should not be involved in selecting honors for the sports they cover.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/get-the-media-out-of-the-honoring-business/', 'snippet': 'The Hall of Fame voting brought up an inadvertent point: members of the news media should not be involved in selecting honors for the sports they cover.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is no great sacrifice for me to say that those who regularly cover major league baseball should get the heck out of the business of Hall of Fame voting. That’s because I work for a newspaper, The New York Times, that doesn’t allow its reporters to participate in the business — and that’s exactly what it is — of electing ballplayers to a club that will not only elevate their historic standing, but will enhance their earning power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Get the Media Out of the Honoring Business', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baseball Hall of Fame', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cooperstown (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T23:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Araton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Araton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f7b44d3-be27-5c7a-9049-b39c042a3bb9', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f7b44d3-be27-5c7a-9049-b39c042a3bb9'}, {'abstract': 'In a complicated deal, Consolidated Edison would sell 3.4 acres next to Brooklyn Bridge Park and near the Manhattan Bridge and split the profit with its customers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/11/dont-own-it-still-make-money-on-land-sale-near-brooklyn-bridge/', 'snippet': 'In a complicated deal, Consolidated Edison would sell 3.4 acres next to Brooklyn Bridge Park and near the Manhattan Bridge and split the profit with its customers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hipsters are not the only ones reaping benefits from the soaring value of real estate in the Dumbo section of Brooklyn. All of Consolidated Edison’s customers could soon share in a minor windfall from the sale of the next piece of Brooklyn Bridge Park.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Own It? Still Make Money on Land Sale Near Brooklyn Bridge', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Bridge Park (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-11T23:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10b60f6c-a599-5a5e-82b8-c1512ab1330b', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10b60f6c-a599-5a5e-82b8-c1512ab1330b'}, {'abstract': 'David Wampach’s “Sacre,” at the Coil festival, is so odd that the repetitions encourage you to slap on meanings, making it more interesting than it is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/12/arts/dance/david-wampachs-sacre-at-the-invisible-dog-art-center.html', 'snippet': 'David Wampach’s “Sacre,” at the Coil festival, is so odd that the repetitions encourage you to slap on meanings, making it more interesting than it is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Performed by two people wearing combinations of leotards and medieval attire, “Sacre,” by the French choreographer David Wampach, is fairly silly, slightly boring and very peculiar. The boring and the peculiar aspects actually work to the piece’s advantage. What little happens keeps happening again and again — but it is so sheerly odd that the repetitions encourage you to slap on meanings, making it more interesting than it is. Still, despite its bizarre sides, the work is ultimately both flimsy and fatuous.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/12/arts/dance/12SACRE2/12SACRE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarg