s_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f22e345-f04d-5a83-85a0-c75a556b5e66', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f22e345-f04d-5a83-85a0-c75a556b5e66'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Johnson’s dance installation “Niicugni,” at the Baryshnikov Arts Center, is a detailed exploration of her Yupik heritage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/12/arts/dance/emily-johnsons-niicugni-at-baryshnikov-arts-center.html', 'snippet': 'Emily Johnson’s dance installation “Niicugni,” at the Baryshnikov Arts Center, is a detailed exploration of her Yupik heritage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So much care has gone into making Emily Johnson’s “Niicugni” that you can almost smell it. At the Baryshnikov Arts Center, where this dance installation had its New York premiere on Wednesday as part of Performance Space 122’s Coil Festival, 51 lanterns hand-sewn from fish skins hang over the stage and audience. A map inserted in the program specifies not just which of the many volunteers made which lamp but also how many thousands of miles the fish might have traveled in its migrations. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/dance/12JOHNSON/12JOHNSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 736, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/11/arts/dance/12JOHNSON/12JOHNSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1472, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub