ancial clout to get a major company to disclose its political contributions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/12/opinion/new-yorks-pension-muscle.html', 'snippet': 'New York State’s comptroller, Thomas DiNapoli, wants to use his financial clout to get a major company to disclose its political contributions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the United States Supreme Court issued its flawed 2010 decision in the Citizens United case, unleashing millions of dollars in corporate spending on political campaigns, there seemed to be one small prescription for relief. Justice Anthony Kennedy, writing for the majority, noted that prompt disclosure of political expenditures would allow stockholders and citizens to hold corporations accountable. Shareholders, he said, could determine whether the corporation’s financing of campaigns “advances the corporation’s interest in making profits.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York’s Pension Muscle', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York’s Pension Muscle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiNapoli, Thomas P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Qualcomm Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-12T01:57:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original