'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bobsledding', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volvo Golf Champions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-12T05:21:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4c79dca5-692a-5db3-ab03-91f80dc6954d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4c79dca5-692a-5db3-ab03-91f80dc6954d'}, {'abstract': 'The Ford Motor Company said that it planned to hire 2,200 salaried workers in the United States to support new Ford product introductions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/12/business/ford-plans-to-hire-2200-salaried-workers-in-us.html', 'snippet': 'The Ford Motor Company said that it planned to hire 2,200 salaried workers in the United States to support new Ford product introductions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Ford Motor Company said on Friday that it planned to hire 2,200 salaried workers in the United States this year, its largest increase in new salaried workers in more than a decade, to support new Ford product introductions. The hiring is in addition to the more than 8,100 combined hourly and salaried jobs Ford created in the United States in 2012, as the company increased production capacity and expanded engineering, manufacturing and other areas to meet the growing demand for its vehicles, the company said. About 1,000 of the positions are hourly jobs brought back to Ford plants in the United States from other locations, including Japan and Mexico. \xa0Ford said it was more than halfway to the 12,000 new jobs it committed to deliver in the United States by 2015 as part of its 2011 contract with the United Automobile Workers. “As we expand our product lineup of fuel-efficient vehicles, we need more people in critical areas, such as in a range of engineering activities, vehicle production, computer software and other I.T. functions, to ensure we deliver the vehicles people want and value,” Joseph R. Hinrichs, Ford’s president for the Americas, said in a news release. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford Plans to Hire 2,200 Salaried Workers in U.S.', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Automobiles', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ford Plans To Hire 2,200 Salaried Workers In U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-12T05:46:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b6c9462-ff25-5fc9-b33f-0d38108920fb', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b6c9462-ff25-5fc9-b33f-0d38108920fb'}, {'abstract': 'New technologies are changing the way cars are driven.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/12/science/advanced-systems-are-on-the-move.html', 'snippet': 'New technologies are changing the way cars are driven.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New technologies are changing the way cars are driven. — John Markoff', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Advanced Systems Are on the Move', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Advanced Systems Are on the Move', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 2, 'ma