:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Mutual Funds', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbe12c3d-e9f6-5ab4-9d90-558da4fc8aa4', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbe12c3d-e9f6-5ab4-9d90-558da4fc8aa4'}, {'abstract': 'Why don’t mutual funds say more about  corporate governance? One reason may be that fund managers have many short-term incentives to outperform, but few to create long-term value.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/business/mutfund/mutual-funds-arent-wielding-much-power-over-executive-pay.html', 'snippet': 'Why don’t mutual funds say more about  corporate governance? One reason may be that fund managers have many short-term incentives to outperform, but few to create long-term value.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE stock market swings up and down, yet executive pay seems to go only higher. Rising executive compensation has been something of a constant in modern capitalism — and so far, at least, managers of mutual funds haven’t had much of an effect on it.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/business/13-PAY2/13-PAY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/business/13-PAY2/13-PAY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/business/13-PAY2/13-PAY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {