eyton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-13T05:43:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/494062c0-f740-5e03-9bbb-9b342b07de96', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/494062c0-f740-5e03-9bbb-9b342b07de96'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (NBC) THE 70TH ANNUAL GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS Tina Fey and Amy Poehler (above, from left) host these awards — considered predictors of the Oscars — sponsored by the Hollywood Foreign Press Association and broadcast live from the Beverly Hilton Hotel in Los Angeles. In the motion picture drama division, nominees include “Argo,” “Django Unchained,” “Life of Pi,” “Lincoln” and “Zero Dark Thirty” for best picture; Jessica Chastain, Marion Cotillard, Helen Mirren, Naomi Watts and Rachel Weisz for best actress; and Daniel Day-Lewis, Richard Gere, John Hawkes, Joaquin Phoenix and Denzel Washington for best actor. In the comedy or musical division, the nominees are “The Best Exotic Marigold Hotel,” “Les Misérables,” “Moonrise Kingdom,” “Salmon Fishing in the Yemen” and “Silver Linings Playbook” for best picture; Emily Blunt, Judi Dench, Jennifer Lawrence, Maggie Smith and Meryl Streep for best actress; and Jack Black, Bradley Cooper, Hugh Jackman, Ewan McGregor and Bill Murray for best actor. Jodie Foster will receive the Cecil B. DeMille Award. Awards will also be handed out for television work. Coverage from the red carpet begins at 6 on E! and at 7 on NBC; E! will visit after-parties at 11.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-13T06:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a18d8aa-fa3e-50b7-a9c7-6b40f89bbbae', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a18d8aa-fa3e-50b7-a9c7-6b40f89bbbae'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Patterson was a Pulitzer Prize winner who fought racial injustice in his columns for The Atlanta Constitution and was later an editor at The Washington Post and The St. Petersburg Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/us/eugene-c-patterson-editor-and-civil-rights-crusader-dies-at-89.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Patterson was a Pulitzer Prize winner who fought racial injustice in his columns for The Atlanta Constitution and was later an editor at The Washington Post and The St. Petersburg Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eugene C. Patterson, the Pulitzer Prize-winning editor of The Atlanta Constitution during the civil rights conflicts of the 1960s and later the managing editor of The Washington Post and editor of The St. Petersburg Times in Florida, died on Saturday in St. Petersburg. He was 89.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/us/patterson-obit-1/pat