24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. McFadden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/830a8c43-7127-5713-9c2c-457622ba7a19', 'word_count': 1397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/830a8c43-7127-5713-9c2c-457622ba7a19'}, {'abstract': 'As the air quality in China’s capital, and elsewhere in the country, worsened to perhaps new records over the weekend, anger grew among ordinary Chinese — and one family coped with the mess.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/13/breathing-in-beijing-coping-with-chinas-smog/', 'snippet': 'As the air quality in China’s capital, and elsewhere in the country, worsened to perhaps new records over the weekend, anger grew among ordinary Chinese — and one family coped with the mess.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — With Beijing’s air pollution soaring to seemingly new, awful records this weekend, the classic parenting dilemma of “What shall we do with the kids?” had a grimly obvious answer: Slap on the antipollution face masks and go shopping for another air purifier. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/world/asia/13pollution1-rdv/13pollution1-rdv-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/13/world/asia/13pollution1-rdv/13pollution1-rdv-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/13/world/asia/13pollution1-rdv/13pollution1-rdv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy':