'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-13T21:58:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a67f931-0510-5614-83dc-6d35e8441928', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a67f931-0510-5614-83dc-6d35e8441928'}, {'abstract': 'The second most successful team at the Edgar Kaplan Winter Regional in Manhattan last month was Justine Cushing, Melih Ozdil and Joel Wooldridge of New York City, and Adam Kaplan of New Port Richey, Fla.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/crosswords/bridge/bridge-edgar-kaplan-winter-regional-in-manhattan.html', 'snippet': 'The second most successful team at the Edgar Kaplan Winter Regional in Manhattan last month was Justine Cushing, Melih Ozdil and Joel Wooldridge of New York City, and Adam Kaplan of New Port Richey, Fla.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The second most successful team at the Edgar Kaplan Winter Regional in Manhattan last month was Justine Cushing, Melih Ozdil and Joel Wooldridge of New York City, and Adam Kaplan of New Port Richey, Fla. In five events they had three wins, one second and one fourth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Edgar Kaplan Winter Regional in Manhattan', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-13T22:05:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aaf44a1-47ff-5e5b-bab1-80d314550172', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aaf44a1-47ff-5e5b-bab1-80d314550172'}, {'abstract': 'The celebrated shift to smartphones, once welcomed with an outpouring of investments, is now making some investors nervous as monetization proves harder for mobile devices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/technology/start-up-investors-grow-wary-of-tech-ventures-after-facebooks-ipo.html', 'snippet': 'The celebrated shift to smartphones, once welcomed with an outpouring of investments, is now making some investors nervous as monetization proves harder for mobile devices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — Call it the Facebook effect. Until recently, investors had been all too eager to pour millions into any Web start-up with rapid growth, regardless of whether it made money or even had plans to do so down the road. But after Facebook’s rocky initial public offering and flameouts at Zynga and Groupon, venture capitalists are entering a picky phase.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/14/world/jpStartups/jpStartups-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/14/world/jpStartups/jpStartups-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/14/world/jpStartups/jpStartups-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':