re’ over the meaning of the word ‘hacktivism.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'The untimely death of the young Internet activist Aaron Swartz, apparently by suicide, has prompted an outpouring of reaction in the digital world. Foremost among the debates being reheated — one which had already grown in the wake of larger and more daring data breaches in the past few years — is whether Swartz’s activities as a “hacktivist” were being unfairly defined as malicious or criminal. In particular, critics (as well as Swartz’s family in a formal statement) have focused on the federal government’s indictment of Swartz for downloading millions of documents from the scholarly database JSTOR, an action which JSTOR itself had declined to prosecute. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is a ‘Hacktivist’?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anonymous (Internet Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lulz Security', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'OpenLeaks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T01:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Ludlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ludlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aed6ca36-92fc-5daf-af89-4cc56b38cc71', 'word_count': 1758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aed6ca36-92fc-5daf-af89-4cc56b38cc71'}, {'abstract': 'The planes were sent to support an attempt by F