anization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d48d8a6-aaf2-59a6-9ab6-99ba1b010925', 'word_count': 36, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d48d8a6-aaf2-59a6-9ab6-99ba1b010925'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Armstrong is presumably going to offer some version of a confession about doping when he meets with Oprah Winfrey, which may constitute one of the more ungainly U-turns in the history of sport.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/armstrong-and-winfrey-familiar-names-in-a-time-honored-ritual/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Armstrong is presumably going to offer some version of a confession about doping when he meets with Oprah Winfrey, which may constitute one of the more ungainly U-turns in the history of sport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lance Armstrong has been a remarkable gift to the media in many ways. An unlikely American victor in the Eurocentric sport of pro cycling, he won the Tour de France seven times after being stricken by cancer, which had threatened to end his racing career entirely. He started a cancer foundation, Livestrong, whose name appeared on bracelets worn by many Americans. And then he ran into deep trouble over accusations that he had doped his way to the top of the sport.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Armstrong and Winfrey: Familiar Names in a Time-Honored Ritual', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tour de France (Bicycle Race)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, LANCE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/490be685-3244-5bc8-94ec-2e9f8f3b6406', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/490be685-3244-5bc8-94ec-2e9f8f3b6406'}, {'abstract': 'Today the Cadillac ATS was named the North American car of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/in-the-round-cadillac-ats/', 'snippet': 'Today the Cadillac ATS was named the North American car of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today the Cadillac ATS was named the North American car of the year. To control the view of the car above, click on the image and move the cursor left or right.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Round: Cadillac ATS', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:12:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45ac0277-3e7b-541c-abc5-20acc5ee1868', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45ac0277-3e7b-541c-abc5-20acc5ee1868'}, {'abstract': 'A weekly capsule of travel news curated by our writers and editors.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/walkabout-114-travel-restrictions-loosen-for-cubans-and-beijing-gets-a-new-much-needed-airport/', 'snippet': 'A weekly capsule of travel news curated by our writers and editors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Restrictions Loosen New Cuban migration rules will allow islanders to spend more time overseas before they forfeit their Cuban residency, a concession that reflects the government’s desire for closer ties with millions of Cubans who live abroad. (The New York Times)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Walkabout — 1/14: Travel Restrictions Loosen for Cubans, and Beijing Gets a New, Much-Needed Airport', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:13:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88461911-3111-53e1-a62a-020fe4258cc5', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88461911-3111-53e1-a62a-020fe4258cc5'}, {'abstract': 'During a particular spate of Anglophilia, certain image obsessed upper-class Neapolitan gents of the early 20th century sent their tailors to London to learn English style. A fine example was Frida Giannini’s fall 2013 show this afternoon for Gucci.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/at-gucci-italian-with-an-english-accent/', 'snippet': 'During a particular spate of Anglophilia, certain image obsessed upper-class Neapolitan gents of the early 20th century sent their tailors to London to learn English style. A fine example was Frida Giannini’s fall 2013 show this afternoon for Gucc...', 'lead_paragraph': 'During a particular spate of Anglophilia, certain image-obsessed upper-class Neapolitan gents of the early 20th century sent their tailors to London to learn English style. Having analyzed Englishmen’s tastes for suits with high armholes, a squared-off shoulder, a suppressed waist and armoring woolens, they came home and completely Italianized what they had learned. The results were slouchy and unconstructed renditions of a classic Savile Row suit. By the 1980s, this adaptation came to seem inherently Italian and thus gave the world the career of Giorgio Armani.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Gucci, Italian With an English Accent', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giannini, Frida', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gucci', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:18:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Trebay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a0609af-df0d-5474-9a0e-60dae5ce7a34', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a0609af-df0d-5474-9a0e-60dae5ce7a34'}, {'abstract': 'Bernd Heinrich, an emeritus biology professor at the University of Vermont, has been studying how animals die.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/science/death-and-dying-the-animal-way.html', 'snippet': 'Bernd Heinrich, an emeritus biology professor at the University of Vermont, has been studying how animals die.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For much of the year, Bernd Heinrich spends his time at a cabin he built in a remote forest in western Maine. The cabin has no indoor plumbing and no electricity, he says — just a tree growing inside it. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '