 '2013-01-14T19:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Dreifus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dreifus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffdbe7e1-27da-5646-a52d-9edf8d617c0f', 'word_count': 1227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffdbe7e1-27da-5646-a52d-9edf8d617c0f'}, {'abstract': 'The struggling computer maker needs to revamp, and a sale to private equity would allow big changes without having to keep shareholders happy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/dell-shares-surge-after-report-of-possible-buyout/', 'snippet': 'The struggling computer maker needs to revamp, and a sale to private equity would allow big changes without having to keep shareholders happy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Public investors are excited about the prospect of Dell going private.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dell Shares Rise on News Company May Go Private', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dell’s Shares Rise on News Company May Go Private', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dell, Michael S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dell Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:20:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced and Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d3730d8-0c0d-5ee3-a98b-6e04a9d89e64', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d3730d8-0c0d-5ee3-a98b-6e04a9d89e64'}, {'abstract': 'The new government may be using the aggressive reporting to demonstrate a commitment to transparency and accountability as well as use this crisis to further the difficult reforms toward a more sustainable development model, the author writes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/questions-about-chinas-growth-censorship-and-air-pollution/', 'snippet': 'The new government may be using the aggressive reporting to demonstrate a commitment to transparency and accountability as well as use this crisis to further the difficult reforms toward a more sustainable development model, the author writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week’s column discussed the Southern Weekend censorship mess. The incident appears to have fizzled with no significant retreat by the censors or penalties for the journalists involved. A recent podcast, whose guests include Ian Johnson of The New York Times Beijing bureau, provides a good overview of the resolution and possible ramifications of the story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions About China’s Growth, Censorship and Air Pollution', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Times', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Daily", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern Weekend (Chinese Newspaper)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tencent Holdings', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-14T19:23:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/885441c9-3105-545a-ba8c-a4ee66bdd656', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/885441c9-3105-545a-ba8c-a4ee66bdd656'}, {'abstract': 'If you are a child in my family feeling your very rottenest, your tender mother (me) is likely to bolt out of the room with her fingers in her ears.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/emetophobia-vomiting-children-and-me/', 'snippet': 'If you are a child in my family feeling your very rottenest, your tender mother (me) is likely to bolt out of the room with her fingers in her ears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Being phobic about barfing and having children is like being afraid of heights and giving birth to the Empire State Building. You are, to put it simply, screwed. Kids throw up. A lot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/14/style/motherlode-barf/motherlode-barf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'sup