n_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b7ab82c-a4db-5c08-a63b-8dca07f9be0e', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b7ab82c-a4db-5c08-a63b-8dca07f9be0e'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers examining trials of cancer treatments found that the more effective a treatment was said to be, the more likely the bad side effects were minimized.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/14/clinical-trials-flawed-by-biased-reporting/', 'snippet': 'Researchers examining trials of cancer treatments found that the more effective a treatment was said to be, the more likely the bad side effects were minimized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Randomized placebo-controlled clinical trials are the gold standard for demonstrating the efficacy and toxicity of new treatments and drugs, but according to a new study, most of them are compromised by poor or biased reporting of benefits and harms.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 215, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 215, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'ima