'snippet': 'Meals on Wheels advocates have always believed that something as fundamental as a hot meal, a greeting and another set of eyes can help keep people in their homes longer. A study says they are right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s a simpler idea than Meals on Wheels? Older, lower-income people who have trouble driving, cooking or shopping — or paying for food — sign up with a local agency. Each day, volunteers or paid staff come by and drop off a hot lunch. Federal and state dollars and local charities foot the bill.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study: More to Meal Delivery Than Food', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Meals on Wheels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T10:57:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72a0f711-fa06-5c97-bea0-be5abb1cacae', 'word_count': 850, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72a0f711-fa06-5c97-bea0-be5abb1cacae'}, {'abstract': 'Creating a $1 trillion coin to address the nation’s debt ceiling would simply have been business as usual for the government, writ large, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/accounting-for-a-1-trillion-platinum-coin/', 'snippet': 'Creating a $1 trillion coin to address the nation’s debt ceiling would simply have been business as usual for the government, writ large, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul. He is the author of “The Benefit and the Burden: Tax Reform – Why We Need It and What It Will Take.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coins, Bills and the National Debt', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Currency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T11:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/278703ef-ea28-50b5-9a66-07fd4b3924b6', 'word_count': 962, 'uri': 'nyt://article/278703ef-ea28-50b5-9a66-07fd4b3924b6'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, and a look at the latest happenings in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/the-early-word-executive-decisions/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, and a look at the latest happenings in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Executive Decisions', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T11:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fe434da-97e0-5552-ae50-95977e75699b', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fe434da-97e0-5552-ae50-95977e75699b'}, {'abstract': 'OKCupid, one of the more popular dating sites, is rolling out a product that aims to set up members on blind dates, giving them little information to go on about the other person — not even a full photograph.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/okcupid-wants-to-send-members-on-a-crazy-blind-date/', 'snippet': 'OKCupid, one of the more popular dating sites, is rolling out a product that aims to set up members on blind dates, giving them little information to go on about the other person — not even a full photograph.', 'lead_paragraph': ' OkCupid, the dating site, has spent the last few years experimenting with ways to appeal to increasingly tech-savvy and pressed-for-time singles. In 2011, the company rolled out a “locals” feature that showed other singles nearby who were available to get a drink or see a movie. Last summer, the company introduced offline events that brought members together for gatherings like a beer tasting or a barbecue cook-off, to mingle and perhaps inspire a love connection.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/15/technology/15bits-okcupid/15bits-okcupid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank':