ming in May', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Dan Brown Novel Is Coming in May', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Dan (1964- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Random House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:17:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e67575e3-8e7e-51fd-8d61-b4d0175039f5', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e67575e3-8e7e-51fd-8d61-b4d0175039f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets don’t have a general manager, but they still have their quarterback, and that remains their biggest problem.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/sunk-costs-mark-sanchez-and-the-concorde/', 'snippet': 'The Jets don’t have a general manager, but they still have their quarterback, and that remains their biggest problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We haven’t forgotten about the Jets, who still don’t have a general manager, or an offensive coordinator, or many of their other assistant coaches. Most important, they don’t have a quarterback.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunk Costs: Mark Sanchez and the Concorde', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanchez, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:18:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e504e97d-c432-5c72-a05b-7a5051e43456', 'word_count': 837, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e504e97d-c432-5c72-a05b-7a5051e43456'}, {'abstract': 'In his news conference Monday, President Obama suggested that Washington will have tamed the government’s debt problems if the two parties can agree on another $1.5 trillion or so in spending cuts and tax increases.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/in-debt-game-an-early-move-from-obama/', 'snippet': 'In his news conference Monday, President Obama suggested that Washington will have tamed the government’s debt problems if the two parties can agree on another $1.5 trillion or so in spending cuts and tax increases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a high-stakes negotiation, the most important moves often come not in the end game but at the very start, when one side or the other prevails in defining what is on the table. If you listened closely, you might have heard President Obama try to do just that in his news conference on Monday, when he suggested that Washington will have tamed the government’s debt problems if the two parties can agree on another $1.5 trillion or so in spending cuts and tax increases.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Debt Game, an Early Move From Obama', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Debt Game, An Early Move From Obama', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Campaign to Fix the Debt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:22:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard W. Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7742727-5836-5de7-8574-201469aacf81', 'word_count': 1088, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7742727-5836-5de7-8574-201469aacf81'}, {'abstract': 'Hollywood studios negotiate their way through China’s censorship process, Gigi Pritzker opens her own film production company, and Lance Armstrong confides in Oprah Winfrey.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/the-breakfast-meeting-hollywood-navigates-china-and-a-pritzker-heir-navigates-hollywood/', 'snippet': 'Hollywood studios negotiate their way through China’s censorship process, Gigi Pritzker opens her own film production company, and Lance Armstrong confides in Oprah Winfrey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Hollywood studios look to tap into the fast-growing Chinese film market, they are increasingly having to navigate their way through that country’s sometimes inscrutable censorship process, Michael Cieply and Brooks Barnes write in The New York Times. As they do so, they are discovering some details and themes that won’t pass muster, and that other things can be negotiated so that both the filmmakers and the Chinese censorship board are satisfied. Billions of dollars are riding on the outcome, because international box office revenue is such a crucial element in the success or failure of a picture. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Hollywood Navigates China, and a Pritzker Heir Navigates Hollywood', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, LANCE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:23:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12684919-cba6-5f41-8476-7e91762006d7', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12684919-cba6-5f41-8476-7e91762006d7'}, {'abstract': 'A hybrid-powered concept that we are assured signals the look of future Nissan crossovers and could indicate the appearance of the new Murano.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/resonance-concept-signals-design-direction-for-nissan/', 'snippet': 'A hybrid-powered concept that we are assured signals the look of future Nissan crossovers and could indicate the appearance of the new Murano.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduced on Tuesday, Jan. 15: Nissan Resonance Concept', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Resonance Concept Signals Design Direction for Nissan', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e687a139-9038-5d3e-9110-d30babc2012a', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e687a139-9038-5d3e-9110-d30babc2012a'}, {'abstract': 'Bad grammar explained, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/memes-of-the-moment-silly/', 'snippet': 'Bad grammar explained, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having a low-energy morning. So, if you’re finding some of the blogospheric discussion puzzling right now, a brief guide.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Memes of the Moment (Silly)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:31:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da28e0df-881c-506f-b857-43d91d919ef4', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da28e0df-881c-506f-b857-43d91d919ef4'}, {'abstract': '“There’s a great opportunity for the Petersen to expand from Southern California car culture into the world of cars as art, cars as freedom, and cars as solutions to transportation problems,” said Peter Mullin, the new chairman of the Petersen Automotive Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/new-chairman-of-petersen-museum-sees-expansion-ahead/', 'snippet': '“There’s a great opportunity for the Petersen to expand from Southern California car culture into the world of cars as art, cars as freedom, and cars as solutions to transportation problems,” said Peter Mullin, the new chairman of the Petersen Aut...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new leadership team plans to make the 20th anniversary of the Petersen Automotive Museum in Los Angeles more than a milestone on the calendar. The anniversary — in June 2014 — provides an opportunity “to lift the Petersen into global recognition as one of the great automotive museums in the world,” said Peter Mullin, the new chairman of the museum’s board of directors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Chairman of Petersen Museum Sees Expansion Ahead', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Petersen Automotive Museum', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T14:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edsall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfedd79e-5ac2-5014-8245-8422a3fc9bd3', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfedd79e-5ac2-5014-8245-8422a3fc9bd3'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York told President Obama on Tuesday that he was optimistic that he could vote for the confirmation of Chuck Hagel as secretary of defense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/us/politics/schumer-says-hes-satisfied-with-hagel-on-mideast.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York told President Obama on Tuesday that he was optimistic that he could vote for the confirmation of Chuck Hagel as secretary of defense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — In a boon for the Obama administration’s efforts to advance the nomination of Chuck Hagel as secretary of defense, Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York told President Obama on Tuesday that he was optimistic that he could vote for Mr. Hagel’s confirmation based on his grilling of Mr. Hagel on a variety of issues pertaining to Israel and Iran.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schumer Says He’s Satisfied With Hagel on Mideast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagel, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T15:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0cadb40-ca8f-500b-9c14-89ec5270691f', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0cadb40-ca8f-500b-9c14-89ec5270691f'}, {'abstract': 'The actress performs a scene from the Broadway revival of William Inge’s 1953 drama.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/in-performance-elizabeth-marvel-in-picnic/', 'snippet': 'The actress performs a scene from the Broadway revival of William Inge’s 1953 drama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In William Inge’s 1953 play, “Picnic,” the arrival of Hal, a handsome drifter, unleashes resentment and sexual longing in the residents of a small Kansas town on Labor Day. In this scene Elizabeth Marvel plays Rosemary, an old-maid schoolteacher who confronts Hal in a drunken rage. The Broadway revival of “Picnic” runs through Feb. 24 at the American Airlines Theater.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Elizabeth Marvel in ‘Picnic’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Inge, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marvel, Elizabeth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T15:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8efb02f-0b76-5dad-8457-e4864273a58f', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8efb02f-0b76-5dad-8457-e4864273a58f'}, {'abstract': 'Journalists had their own questions for Lance Armstrong, who taped an interview with Oprah Winfrey that will be broadcast over two nights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/sports/awaiting-lance-armstrongs-confession-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'Journalists had their own questions for Lance Armstrong, who taped an interview with Oprah Winfrey that will be broadcast over two nights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You do have to hand it to Lance Armstrong and Oprah Winfrey, two icons — one disgraced, the other faded — who have found a way to milk several days’ worth of attention out of awaiting the Great Confession. The good news is, we have been dripped on by the leak that Armstrong did indeed admit he used performance-enhancing drugs. (In other news: the world is round.) That gives us two more days to figure out where the Oprah Winfrey Network is in our cable lineup before Thursday’s broadcast, which is now being dragged out into a "two-night event," so a few people might remember it exists for another day. The bad news is, the interview has been taped, so all of our great suggestions for what Winfrey should ask Armstrong are going to go for naught. Had she only called first.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/16leading1/16leading1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/16leading1/16leading1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/16leading1/16leading1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/16leading1/16leading1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1354, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/16leading1/16leading1-thumbS