r': None, 'print_headline': 'Skirmishes Threaten Pakistan-India Thaw', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Timmons, Heather', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Bikram', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Manmohan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:18:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5222b93b-9483-5b1c-aacd-4890cbde092f', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5222b93b-9483-5b1c-aacd-4890cbde092f'}, {'abstract': 'A Web page published by The Atlantic around lunchtime on Monday was labeled as “sponsor content,” but otherwise looked like a sunny blog post about the church’s expansion.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/the-atlantic-apologizes-for-scientology-ad/', 'snippet': 'A Web page published by The Atlantic around lunchtime on Monday was labeled as “sponsor content,” but otherwise looked like a sunny blog post about the church’s expansion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Atlantic on Tuesday issued a simple three-word apology for publishing an advertisement by the Church of Scientology that resembled a normal article from the acclaimed magazine: “We screwed up.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Atlantic Apologizes for Scientology Ad', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Atlantic Apologizes for Scientology Ad, and Says It Will Rethink Its Policies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:18:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter and Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8ba69d2-89e3-5748-911d-6d95740f5de1', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8ba69d2-89e3-5748-911d-6d95740f5de1'}, {'abstract': 'A new book traces the history of words having to do with food and drink.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/dining/new-dictionary-has-origins-of-dining-terms.html', 'snippet': 'A new book traces the history of words having to do with food and drink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The excuse for buying a dictionary of culinary terms, even if you already have one, is that the vocabulary keeps growing. “Umami,” for example, which “The Diner’s Dictionary: Word Origins of Food & Drink” says was coined in 1908, is still relatively new to the English-speaking world. And “dulce de leche,” the Argentine milk caramel, first appeared in English in 1923 but was not more commonly used until a good 80 years later. These are just two of more than 2,000 words or terms crammed into this compact volume. There are some surprising gaps, like “acai,” the popular Amazonian berry; or “vadouvan,” a kind of curry that’s showing up on menus more often; or “guanciale,” the Italian cured pork cheeks. The perspective is more British than American, but what is offered is useful and fascinating. For example, who knew that there were not one but two terms for turtle meat: calipash and calipee, which refer to different parts of the animal? And that lamb’s wool was a warmed spiced beer with apple that Samuel Pepys mentioned as a nightcap in 1666?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_DICTIONARY/16STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 866, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_DICTIONARY/16STUFF1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1733, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_DICTIONARY/16STUFF_DICTIONARY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_DICTIONARY/16STUFF_DICTIONARY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_DICTIONARY/16STUFF_DICTIONARY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Learn How Calipash Differs From Calipee', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Learn How Calipash Differs From Calipee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dictionaries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ayto, John', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64c6b8b6-8784-5a2b-98c2-716fd9f7287a', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64c6b8b6-8784-5a2b-98c2-716fd9f7287a'}, {'abstract': 'With Danny Granger out, the Pacers’ Paul George has made the most of his opportunities.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/for-indianas-george-a-breakout-season/', 'snippet': 'With Danny Granger out, the Pacers’ Paul George has made the most of his opportunities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an N.B.A. season with a long list of teams learning to cope with sidelined superstars, perhaps no player has made more of his opportunity than Indiana’s Paul George. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/basketball/16george/16george-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/basketball/16george/16george-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/basketball/16george/16george-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/basketball/16george/16george-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/sports/basketball/16george/16george-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Indiana’s George, a Breakout Season', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Granger, Danny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Frank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indiana Pacers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:22:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Cavan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cavan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/917632d5-d824-57b8-af86-69d02a4d2747', 'word_count': 701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/917632d5-d824-57b8-af86-69d02a4d2747'}, {'abstract': 'The signature of Royce’ is a small flavored square, vaguely truffle-like and alluringly creamy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/dining/japanese-chocolates-arrive-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The signature of Royce’ is a small flavored square, vaguely truffle-like and alluringly creamy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steps from the Belgian chocolate giants Neuhaus and Leonidas on Madison Avenue is a new Japanese rival, Royce’, with a very different approach to chocolate-making. Instead of filled bonbons, its signature is a small flavored square, the Nama, vaguely truffle-like and alluringly creamy. Green tea, bitter dark, mild milk and white are some of the options, all made with fresh cream from farms near the factory in Hokkaido, Japan’s northernmost region. The sliver of a boutique sells less than half of the varieties Royce’ makes, but they include novelties like potato chips covered with fromage blanc, cocoa-dipped marshmallows and flat chocolate squares called Prafeuille with gooey fillings of honey, berries and caramel. Ken Romaniszyn, the company’s American partner and an owner of Lady M Cake Boutique uptown, said Royce’, which was founded in 1983 and\xa0has stores all over Asia, is poised to add two more shops in New York in the spring, on 40th Street opposite Bryant Park and on Bleecker Street in Greenwich Village. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN/16STUFF_CHOCSPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Japan, a New Approach to Fine Chocolates', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Japan, a New Approach to Fine Chocolates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Royce'", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:23:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec4dd294-b5cb-5c06-9a0a-642504c9bc0c', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec4dd294-b5cb-5c06-9a0a-642504c9bc0c'}, {'abstract': 'Mutti Tomato Ketchup is sweet, tart, a bit spicy and more robustly tomatoey than the familiar brand and is made without high-fructose corn syrup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/dining/an-italian-ketchup-to-rival-heinz.html', 'snippet': 'Mutti Tomato Ketchup is sweet, tart, a bit spicy and more robustly tomatoey than the familiar brand and is made without high-fructose corn syrup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The race to one-up Heinz continues. And the latest contender, from Italy, should at least get a silver medal. Mutti Tomato Ketchup is properly sweet, tart and a trifle spicy, and if anything, more robustly tomatoey than the iconic brand. It’s also made without the high-fructose corn syrup that is in most Heinz ketchup. Mutti, with headquarters in the Parma area, is an Italian giant that makes only canned tomato products. Its line has just been introduced in the United States and includes tins of juicy baby plum tomatoes, cherry tomatoes, tomato purée and rich, crushed tomato pulp — all worth having in the pantry, especially in winter. Tomato vinegar could easily replace commercial balsamic. There are also several sauces. The ingredient lists on these items are mercifully brief.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 353, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 353}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/dining/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN/16STUFF_TOMATOESSPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Italian Tomatoes Ripe for the Picking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italian Tomatoes Ripe for the Picking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ketchup', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condiments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mutti SpA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb0783c-dceb-5469-9cdb-0ebd8ab24584', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb0783c-dceb-5469-9cdb-0ebd8ab24584'}, {'abstract': 'A mix of good and strange ideas.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/new-yorks-gun-bill/', 'snippet': 'A mix of good and strange ideas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Governor Andrew Cuomo is trying to lead the nation in gun control, but he’s picked a peculiar way of doing it. Mr. Cuomo negotiated in secret with a few other powerful politicians on a dog’s breakfast of legislation that got no public discussion at all and was passed by state senators who had not even read it—because they were not given a chance to do so.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York’s Gun Bill', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Orders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stockman, Steve', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52b580ce-53cf-5848-afb4-d918717176b9', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52b580ce-53cf-5848-afb4-d918717176b9'}, {'abstract': 'The shooting of a teenager along Gaza’s border with Israel underscored the potential for spiraling violence after weeks of restiveness in the area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/world/middleeast/israeli-forces-kill-palestinian-in-west-bank-confrontation.html', 'snippet': 'The shooting of a teenager along Gaza’s border with Israel underscored the potential for spiraling violence after weeks of restiveness in the area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israeli forces fatally shot a Palestinian teenager during a confrontation along the West Bank barrier on Tuesday, underscoring the potential for spiraling violence after weeks of simmering unrest in the area. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source':