': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palestinian Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABBAS, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ramallah (West Bank)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:31:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/861e9c9e-cffd-54c7-b467-0f3b4dbd34b4', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/861e9c9e-cffd-54c7-b467-0f3b4dbd34b4'}, {'abstract': 'New York Times interactive developer Derek Willis describes how he used the mobile version of a government publication to contribute to a story on presidential appointees.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/freeing-the-plum-book/', 'snippet': 'New York Times interactive developer Derek Willis describes how he used the mobile version of a government publication to contribute to a story on presidential appointees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal government produces reams of publications, ranging from the useful to the esoteric. Pick a topic, and in most cases you’ll find a relevant government publication: for example, recent Times articles about presidential appointments draw on the Plum Book. Published annually by either the House or the Senate (the task alternates between committees), the Plum Book is a snapshot of appointments throughout the federal government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Freeing the Plum Book', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowrey, Annie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Government Printing Office', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T18:32:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Derek Willis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Derek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/272ffcbe-d694-517f-8ce4-bfd097503960', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/272ffcbe-d694-517f-8ce4-bfd097503960'}, {'abstract': 'Tazewell Thompson’s “Mary T. & Lizzy K.” opens in Washington in March.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/elizabeth-keckley-takes-to-the-stage-again/', 'snippet': 'Tazewell Thompson’s “Mary T. & Lizzy K.” opens in Washington in March.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pop culture’s recent fascination with Elizabeth Keckley, the former slave who became a successful dressmaker and confidante of Mary Todd Lincoln, continues with a new play scheduled to open in March at the Mead Center for American Theater in Washington.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/theater/16thompson/16thompson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 274, 'width': 184, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/16/theater/16thompson/16thompson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 274, 'width': 184, 'subType':