se-mutual-fund-performance/', 'snippet': 'Employers, not just employees, tend to chase investment returns and pick funds for retirement plans based on what has done well recently, a new analysis finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Individual investors may understand, in theory, that past performance doesn’t predict future results. But they still can’t help moving their retirement money into funds that did well over the last year or two, even though conditions — and investment returns — in the coming year or two may well be different.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Employers Also Chase Mutual Fund Performance', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mutual Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston College', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-15T19:46:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5aab9b62-84d3-58a8-a5ae-873c8550354a', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5aab9b62-84d3-58a8-a5ae-873c8550354a'}, {'abstract': 'Consider the special provision for “qualified small business stock,” which provides a zero percent tax rate on capital gains from certain investments. A better name would be the “angel investor loophole.”', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/15/tax-extenders-that-slip-under-the-radar/', 'snip