ms to birds’ nests — not only make for great nature television, but, since the plans for such constructions seem largely inherited, they also offer an opportunity for scientists to tackle the profoundly difficult question of how genes control complicated behavior in animals and humans.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/science/17burrow/17burrow-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Discovers DNA That Tells Mice How to Construct Their Homes ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Discovers DNA That Tells Mice How to Construct Their Homes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorman, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mice', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-16T18:07:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa825dd8-283f-5e21-a275-3a144ad12e12', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa825dd8-283f-5e21-a275-3a144ad12e12'}, {'abstract': 'John Boehner let the federal aid package pass without majority support from the Republican caucus.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/sandy-and-the-hastert-rule/', 'snippet': 'John Boehner let the federal aid package pass without majority support from the Republican caucus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A quick look at the House vote on $50.7 billion in federal aid to repair the damage from Hurricane Sandy suggests that it passed pretty easily on Tuesday night, 241 to 180. The real news is in how the two parties split: Republicans voted overwhelmingly against it, while all but one Democrat supported it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sandy and the Hastert Rule', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-16T18:17:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b77369f1-ba44-5fd8-b1e4-383d63fab011', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b77369f1-ba44-5fd8-b1e4-383d63fab011'}, {'abstract': 'Why the current balance of power in Washington might be upset in the next few election cycles.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/16/will-gridlock-last/', 'snippet': 'Why the current balance of power in Washington might be upset in the next few election cycles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lately I’ve been arguing that both political parties should probably lower their expectations for what they’re likely to achieve in a divided Washington D.C. This would mean focusing on consolidation (for liberals) and rebuilding (for conservatives), making small-ball deals instead of seeking grand bargains and comprehensive reforms, giving up on the idea that some brilliant legislative gambit will suddenly “break” the House Republicans or “force” the White House to accede to G.O.P. demands, and accepting that the next wave of significant legislating will probably have to await a shift in the balance of power come 2014 or 2016.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Gridlock Last?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-16T18:19:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a054a80-6296-548b-8fba-1d0a546b1984', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a054a80-6296-548b-8fba-1d0a546b1984'}, {'abstract': 'After initially saying no, Chip Kelly, who has no pro football experience as a player or an assistant coach, changed his mind and became the new coach of the Eagles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/sports/football/the-eagles-hire-oregons-chip-kelly.html', 'snippet': 'After initially saying no, Chip Kelly, who has no pro football experience as a player or an assistant coach, changed his mind and became the new coach of the Eagles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday evening, the Philadelphia Eagles sent out a curious statement. They explained that they had no regrets about how much time they had spent interviewing the best college coaches for their vacant head coaching spot, despite knowing there was only a remote chance one of them would leave for the N.F.L. The statement was an indication the Eagles were moving on, having failed to land their top choice. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/sports/y-EAGLES/y-EAGLES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/17/sports/y-EAGLES/y-EAGLES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600,