ian design entrepreneur who helped start the careers of such luminaries as Jasper Morrison, Tom Dixon and Marcel Wanders, has made another find: a young Swedish designer, Johan Lindsten, whom Mr. Cappellini discovered at the Salone Satellite, a showplace of young talents that is part of the International Furniture Fair in Milan. Their first collaboration is a lamp by Mr. Lindsten made of thin-walled, handblown colored glass. Called Meltdown, the lamp is Mr. Lindsten’s comment on the nuclear catastrophe at Fukushima, Japan. Instead of being suspended within the fixture, the light bulb rests against the bottom of the glass and looks ready to dissolve.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17LIGHTING/17CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 351, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17LIGHTING/17CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1186, 'width': 406, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17LIGHTING/17LIGHTING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17LIGHTING/17LIGHTING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Illumination, Inspired by Catastrophe', 'kicker': 'Currents | Lighting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illumination, Inspired by Catastrophe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lighting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lamps and Lampshades', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T00:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d168bdf-06e2-561c-a911-7086fa4fb1b1', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d168bdf-06e2-561c-a911-7086fa4fb1b1'}, {'abstract': 'An installation throughout the new Alexander Hotel reflects the city’s history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/garden/at-the-alexander-hotel-art-imitates-indianapolis.html', 'snippet': 'An installation throughout the new Alexander Hotel reflects the city’s history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Indianapolis Museum of Art curators have filled a nearby hotel with contemporary art that resonates with and sometimes mocks the region. At the\xa0Alexander Hotel, designed by Gensler, which opens Monday, windows here and there are covered with the artist Kim Beck’s vinyl silhouettes of local weeds (above). The sculptor Sonya Clark built a portrait of the Indianapolis hair-care tycoon Madam C. J. Walker out of 3,840 combs (top right). Paul Villinski’s wall-mounted installation is made from vinyl records by musicians including the Gary, Ind., native Michael Jackson (top left). And Mark Fox’s stream-of-consciousness text, cut from reflective stainless steel, notes that John Dillinger is buried about seven and a half miles from the Alexander, and that hotel rooms are zones of “mirrors and copulation.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17OPEN1/17CURR4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17OPEN1/17CURR4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1638, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17OPEN1/17OPEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17OPEN1/17OPEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17OPEN1/17OPEN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Imitates Indianapolis', 'kicker': 'Currents | Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art Imitates Indianapolis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indianapolis Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indianapolis (Ind)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T00:29:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d932e1c8-259a-5638-bcba-197b37086982', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d932e1c8-259a-5638-bcba-197b37086982'}, {'abstract': 'Margaret Roach publishes her second memoir about rural life and gardening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/garden/margaret-roachs-second-memoir-about-rural-life-and-gardening.html', 'snippet': 'Margaret Roach publishes her second memoir about rural life and gardening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Margaret Roach, the garden writer and blogger who lived out the fantasy of not only making it to the top (editorial director of Martha Stewart Living Omnimedia), but also chucking it for an old house in upstate New York, wrote about her first adventures with snakes and solitude in “And I Shall Have Some Peace There: Trading in the Fast Lane for My Own Dirt Road.” Now, in “The Backyard Parables: Lessons on Gardening, and Life” (Grand Central Publishing, $26), she really digs in. Along with observations of wind, caterpillars and bacteria — Mycobacterium vaccae can elevate the mood and stimulate immune cells — she offers details like shoveling alone, through thigh-high snow, in the dark of night, to hammer a hole in the ice of a frog pond so the frogs didn’t suffocate. For fans who crave her gardening advice, don’t worry. She tells how to protect banana plants in winter, and put up a deer fence, too.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17BOOKS/17CURR7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 687, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17BOOKS/17CURR7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1964, 'width': 1318, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/garden/17BOOKS/17CURR7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumb