-eff2-5a03-a4a7-aefb31600625', 'word_count': 1626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35c8ade9-eff2-5a03-a4a7-aefb31600625'}, {'abstract': 'The popular talent show dropped in the category advertisers care most about, continuing the falloff from the huge ratings of earlier seasons.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/ratings-for-american-idol-premiere-are-down-again/', 'snippet': 'The popular talent show dropped in the category advertisers care most about, continuing the falloff from the huge ratings of earlier seasons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It looks like those who predicted “American Idol” would be down another 20 percent this year — despite Fox’s best efforts to pump it up with new judges like Mariah Carey and Nicky Minaj – could get jobs posting betting lines in Las Vegas. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ratings for “American Idol” Premiere Are Down Again', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ratings Down Again For ‘Idol’ Premiere', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings (Audience Measurement)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Mariah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T17:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e81662a7-9617-5f89-9202-4cb85ce29eab', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e81662a7-9617-5f89-9202-4cb85ce29eab'}, {'abstract': 'New York City’s bus strike reveals how years of inertia, shortsighted policies, and competing interests can magnify an already tough problem.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/solving-new-york-city-bus-strike-impact-on-special-needs-students-isnt-simple/', 'snippet': 'New York City’s bus strike reveals how years of inertia, shortsighted policies, and competing interests can magnify an already tough problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City’s bus strike is disproportionately affecting one of its most vulnerable populations: children with special needs, whose symptoms or travel limitations can make it extremely challenging for their parents to get them to school using other methods of transportation. Marie Myung-Ok Lee, who has written for about the challenges and joys of raising a child with developmental disabilities, including autism, in New York City, reminded me yesterday that children like her son depend on continuity and predictability in ways that children without his symptoms do not. And many children in the city’s special education programs present particular challenges for bus drivers and their aides. They may be prone to violent tantrums, or need physical help. It’s a different job, and a different relationship, than the phrase “bus driver” immediately suggests. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Solving Bus Strike’s Impact on Special Needs Students Isn’t Simple', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Education', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T17:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9fbb4ec-fc37-52cf-a442-03589cf1d0ff', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9fbb4ec-fc37-52cf-a442-03589cf1d0ff'}, {'abstract': 'The population of the giant fish has declined by an estimated 90 percent in the Mekong over the last 20 years.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/a-gentle-giant-of-a-catfish-fades-from-sight/', 'snippet': 'The population of the giant fish has declined by an estimated 90 percent in the Mekong over the last 20 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The captain leaned heavily into his pole, nudging the boat’s nose off the sandy bank.\xa0 As the boat swung into the swift current of the Mekong, he scrambled back behind the wheel, the engine roared, and we resumed our downstream journey toward Luang Prabang, Laos.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gentle Giant of a Catfish Fades From Sight', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Catfish', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Luang Prabang (Laos)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mekong River', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T17:26:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Opperman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Opperman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/526a7272-7948-55c7-8768-84da27a3bd27', 'word_count': 1144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/526a7272-7948-55c7-8768-84da27a3bd27'}, {'abstract': 'Erika Andiola, the young immigrant activist who chronicled the arrests of her mother and brother during an immigration raid here, was hired by Representative Kyrsten Sinema to do outreach.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/arizona-congresswoman-hires-immigrant-activist-after-she-details-raid/', 'snippet': 'Erika Andiola, the young immigrant activist who chronicled the arrests of her mother and brother during an immigration raid here, was hired by Representative Kyrsten Sinema to do outreach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — Erika Andiola, the young immigrant activist who prompted outrage on social media last week after her messages and tearful video chronicled the arrests of her mother and brother during an immigration raid here, is the new outreach director for Representative Kyrsten Sinema, the freshman congresswoman known for her staunch opposition to Arizona’s stance on illegal immigration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arizona Congresswoman Hires Immigrant Activist After She Details Raid', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dream Act (Development, Relief and Education for Minors Act)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'