ty', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T20:05:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3063f7b4-e379-5392-9dda-953840a98b6f', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3063f7b4-e379-5392-9dda-953840a98b6f'}, {'abstract': 'Food news from all over the spectrum.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/a-smattering-of-food-links/', 'snippet': 'Food news from all over the spectrum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Potentially big: A superior court in Hyde County, N.C., ruled that the state has authority to require Rose Acre Farms, a facility housing nearly four million egg-laying hens, to be regulated under the federal Clean Water Act because of pollutants released from ventilation fans in the henhouses. Unprecedented. The F.D.A. proposed two sweeping rules aimed at preventing the contamination of produce and processed foods, taking a long-awaited step toward codifying the food safety law that Congress passed two years ago. And New Mexico lawmakers have introduced legislation that calls for the mandatory labeling of G.M.O.s within the state. Washington State joins the state, along with a half-dozen others.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Smattering of Food Links', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farm Bill (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexually Transmitted Diseases', 'rank': 6, 