'2013-01-17T20:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edfd9d1b-70db-505c-a362-e1a8ac5889d5', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edfd9d1b-70db-505c-a362-e1a8ac5889d5'}, {'abstract': '“Alexandre Singh: The Pledge,” at the Drawing Center, is a work based on interviews this artist conducted with a wide range of subjects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/arts/design/alexandre-singh-the-pledge-at-the-drawing-center.html', 'snippet': '“Alexandre Singh: The Pledge,” at the Drawing Center, is a work based on interviews this artist conducted with a wide range of subjects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the first projects done in the United States by the French-born, British-raised artist Alexandre Singh was a sprawling, delirious collaboration with Jonah Freeman and Justin Lowe titled “Hello Meth Lab in the Sun,” installed at the Ballroom in Marfa, Tex., in 2008. (Mr. Freeman and Mr. Lowe went on to show a similar version in New York.) It was a virtuosic display — a warren of varied spaces that included a motel check-in counter, a hippie hide-out and an abandoned meth lab — that was perfectly suited to that decadent art-world moment and that piggybacked on the extreme environments of artists like Mike Nelson, Christoph Büchel and Gregor Schneider. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18DRAWING1/18DRAWING1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18DRAWING1/18DRAWING1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 953, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18DRAWING1/18DRAWING1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18DRAWING1/18DRAWING1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18DRAWING1/18DRAWING1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawing Upon Diverse Interviews', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawing Upon Diverse Interviews', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T20:50:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/084e8a5c-4305-57a8-b347-3663bd153a75', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/084e8a5c-4305-57a8-b347-3663bd153a75'}, {'abstract': 'Pictures the poet Allen Ginsberg took of his Beat Generation contemporaries, including Jack Kerouac and William S. Burroughs, are being exhibited at the Grey Art Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/arts/design/beat-memories-the-photographs-of-allen-ginsberg.html', 'snippet': 'Pictures the poet Allen Ginsberg took of his Beat Generation contemporaries, including Jack Kerouac and William S. Burroughs, are being exhibited at the Grey Art Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Allen Ginsberg (1926-1997) was a great poet but not a great photographer. So while “Beat Memories: The Photographs of Allen Ginsberg” at the Grey Art Gallery is an interesting exhibition, it is in certain ways disappointing. The best you can say about the pictures Ginsberg took during two periods in which he dabbled in the medium — the ’50s and early ’60s and the ’80s and ’90s — is that they are the works of a competent amateur. The bigger disappointment, however, is that much of the history that Ginsberg lived through and did so much to alter as a countercultural activist is missing. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/arts/18BEAT_SPAN/18BEAT_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Beat Poet’s Colorful Crew, in Black and White', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Beat Poet’s Colorful Crew, in Black and White', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ginsberg, Allen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grey Art Gallery', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-17T20:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a830949-063a-54df-8f0d-576d232789c0', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a830949-063a-54df-8f0d-576d232789c0'}, {'abstract': 'The author of award-winning biographies of Maxwell Perkins and Charles Lindbergh has been working for a decade on the book about Wilson.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/a-scott-berg-to-publish-biography-of-woodrow-wilson/', 'snippet': 'The author of award-winning biographies of Maxwell Perkins and Charles Lindbergh has been working for a decade on the book about Wilson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A decade after he began work on it, A. Scot