ect', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiNapoli, Thomas P', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:38:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1ee2939-c8ec-5ac5-b27f-6e5b46c3cb26', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1ee2939-c8ec-5ac5-b27f-6e5b46c3cb26'}, {'abstract': 'The Lede rounded up online reaction to Lance Armstrong’s interview with Oprah Winfrey on Thursday night in real-time, with additional fact-checking and context provided by Times reporters.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/live-updates-on-armstrongs-oprah-interview/', 'snippet': 'The Lede rounded up online reaction to Lance Armstrong’s interview with Oprah Winfrey on Thursday night in real-time, with additional fact-checking and context provided by Times reporters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Lede rounded up online reaction to Lance Armstrong’s interview with Oprah Winfrey on Thursday night in real-time, with additional fact-checking and context provided by Juliet Macur, Sarah Lyall, Brian Stelter, David Carr and Robert Mackey. The second part of the interview is scheduled to be broadcast at 9 p.m. Eastern Time on Friday and will be streamed live on the Oprah Winfrey Network’s Web site. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan. 17 Updates on Armstrong’s Confession', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, LANCE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:47:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/885070fc-32d5-5dc5-bf48-7140500e13a2', 'word_count': 3539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/885070fc-32d5-5dc5-bf48-7140500e13a2'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors charged 12 people on Thursday with running a sex trafficking and prostitution ring across four Southern states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/us/12-are-charged-in-4-state-sex-ring.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors charged 12 people on Thursday with running a sex trafficking and prostitution ring across four Southern states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prosecutors charged 12 people on Thursday with running a sex trafficking and prostitution ring across four Southern states. Authorities say the suspects lured female immigrants to the United States and then threatened them with deportation unless they became prostitutes. The operation spanned Georgia, Florida and North and South Carolina, said Edward J. Tarver, the United States attorney in Savannah. Federal agents on Wednesday rescued 11 women who were victims of the operation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: 12 Are Charged in 4-State Sex Ring', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: 12 Are Charged In 4-State Sex Ring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Robbie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Trafficking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:54:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robbie Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robbie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c9f9f8b-b694-58fa-8d05-54d5133f0514', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c9f9f8b-b694-58fa-8d05-54d5133f0514'}, {'abstract': 'A legal battle involving investors and Argentina highlights a growing problem in countries that fall on hard times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/opinion/when-countries-cant-pay-their-debts.html', 'snippet': 'A legal battle involving investors and Argentina highlights a growing problem in countries that fall on hard times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A recent court decision in a long-running legal battle between investors and the government of Argentina has highlighted problems with restructuring the debts of countries that fall on hard times. The case, which is being fought in federal court in New York, suggests that policy makers must develop a more systematic way to deal with sovereign defaults. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Countries Can’t Pay Their Debts', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Countries Can’t Pay Their Debts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Elliott Management Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aurelius Capital Management', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:54:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/38e31d19-2d72-5a02-b1c8-fdc4401084fd', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38e31d19-2d72-5a02-b1c8-fdc4401084fd'}, {'abstract': 'Letting law students take the bar exam after two years would keep many from drowning in debt — and force law schools to make their third-year curriculums worthwhile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/opinion/practicing-law-should-not-mean-living-in-bankruptcy.html', 'snippet': 'Letting law students take the bar exam after two years would keep many from drowning in debt — and force law schools to make their third-year curriculums worthwhile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TODAY, leaders of the New York bar, judges and law school faculty members will gather at New York University to discuss a proposed rule change. If adopted by the state’s highest court, it could make law school far more accessible to low-income students, help the next generation of law students avoid a heavy burden of debt and lead to improvements in legal education across the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Make Law Schools Earn a Third Year', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Make Law Schools Earn a Third Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:55:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel B. Rodriguez and Samuel Estreicher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dcea774-4ef0-527b-9a58-c641d8d6cf87', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dcea774-4ef0-527b-9a58-c641d8d6cf87'}, {'abstract': 'TV shows about tropical mining obscure an environmental disaster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/opinion/a-rumble-in-the-jungle.html', 'snippet': 'TV shows about tropical mining obscure an environmental disaster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE new reality TV series “Bamazon,” on the History Channel, along with already established shows like “Gold Rush” and “Jungle Gold,” have elevated the global mining bonanza into living-room entertainment. Each focuses on a different group of Americans seeking their fortune in distant, ostensibly dangerous locales: “Bamazon” in Guyana, “Gold Rush” in Alaska and Guyana, and “Jungle Gold” in Ghana. But while these shows claim to depict “reality,” they gloss over the most awful truths of gold mining, particularly as it is practiced in the tropics. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/opinion/0118OPEDhammerstad/0118OPEDhammerstad-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/opinion/0118OPEDhammerstad/0118OPEDhammerstad-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/opinion/0118OPEDhammerstad/0118OPEDhammerstad-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/18/opinion/0118OPEDhammerstad/0118OPEDhammerstad-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/opinion/0118OPEDhammerstad/0118OPEDhammerstad-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rumble in the Jungle', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rumble in the Jungle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bamazon (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guyana', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'History Channel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T01:57:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathan K. Lujan, Devin D. Bloom and L. Cynthia Watson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathan', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Lujan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Devin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/852c7149-91f0-5ddb-aaea-df8ab0559f3a', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852c7149-91f0-5ddb-aaea-df8ab0559f3a'}, {'abstract': 'A discussion of ways that innovation and regulation might take the fear factor out of fracking.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/is-wild-west-era-for-frackingas-drilling-coming-to-an-end/', 'snippet': 'A discussion of ways that innovation and regulation might take the fear factor out of fracking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While in Washington to run a panel at an invaluable conference on disasters and the environment this week, I spent a few minutes in a studio to discuss the issues and opportunities surrounding hydraulic fracturing for natural gas (and oil) on the Current TV show The War Room, hosted by Jennifer Granholm, the former governor of Michigan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is ‘Wild West’ Era for Gas Drilling Coming to an End?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shale', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Granholm, Jennifer M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marcellus Shale', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf51c4ce-b113-51fe-8b40-2de1c3041404', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf51c4ce-b113-51fe-8b40-2de1c3041404'}, {'abstract': 'What is it going to take for a new generation of leaders to come up with a plan to bring this sprawling stretch of suburbia out of the 19th century?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/opinion/long-islands-future-stuck-in-transit.html', 'snippet': 'What is it going to take for a new generation of leaders to come up with a plan to bring this sprawling stretch of suburbia out of the 19th century?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long Island, that strange place jutting east from New York City for 100 miles, was once the very symbol of America’s postwar future, of youth and growth, and is now the opposite of that. It is an old suburb that ran out of places to sprawl, was badly hit by recession a decade ago and again in the latest downturn, and is feeling its age. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Island’s Future, Stuck in Transit', 'kicker': 'Editorial Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Island’s Future, Stuck in Transit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Downes, Lawrence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Long Island Rail Road Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T02:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/593e7744-d5c5-5f61-904c-b5b0db9f4ecc', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/593e7744-d5c5-5f61-904c-b5b0db9f4ecc'}, {'abstract': 'Shell’s drilling program, the subject of two federal inquiries, has had problems environmentalists say highlight the dangers of prospecting for oil in the severe Arctic environment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/business/energy-environment/rivals-watch-travails-of-shell-arctic-drilling.html', 'snippet': 'Shell’s drilling program, the subject of two federal inquiries, has had problems environmentalists say highlight the dangers of prospecting for oil in the severe Arctic environment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — Royal Dutch Shell’s Arctic drilling program is now officially in jeopardy and its prospects will depend on the findings of two continuing federal inquiries. One review is on the grounding of the Kulluk drill ship on New Year’s Eve after it was set adrift for five days in stormy weather, and the other is on the safety management of the entire Shell program. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/business/ARTIC/ARTIC-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/18/business/ARTIC/ARTIC-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/business/ARTIC/ARTIC-