ontent_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T02:46:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8361a90-73d5-5a14-98e5-69cf82441320', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8361a90-73d5-5a14-98e5-69cf82441320'}, {'abstract': 'A computer security company wanted to see how many people working in companies that run the nation’s critical infrastructure would respond to an e-mail that could potentially introduce malware into their computer. The hit rate was enough to make you shudder.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/17/critical-infrastructure-systems-seen-as-vulnerable-to-attack/', 'snippet': 'A computer security company wanted to see how many people working in companies that run the nation’s critical infrastructure would respond to an e-mail that could potentially introduce malware into their computer. The hit rate was enough to make y...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The vast majority of targeted computer attacks now start with a malicious e-mail sent to a company employee. Now evidence suggests that the same technique could be used to attack watersheds, power grids, oil refineries and nuclear plants. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critical Infrastructure Systems Seen as Vulnerable to Attack', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Critical Systems\r\nCalled Vulnerable\r\nTo E-Mail Tricks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T02:49:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/416d51d4-f216-5703-a00c-7f498c4ffaea', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/416d51d4-f216-5703-a00c-7f498c4ffaea'}, {'abstract': 'A rescue attempt undertaken without prior notice to the countries whose citizens were taken hostage typifies Algeria’s independent approach, experts say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/world/africa/limits-of-algerian-cooperation-seen-in-rescue-effort.html', 'snippet': 'A rescue attempt undertaken without prior notice to the countries whose citizens were taken hostage typifies Algeria’s independent approach, experts say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — On the way to Europe in October, Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton made a much-publicized stop in Algeria to enlist that nation’s help in combating militants who had established a sanctuary in neighboring Mali, including fighters from Al Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lack of Warning on Rescue Effort Highlights Limits of Algerian Cooperation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lack of Warning on Rescue Effort Highlights Limits of Algerian Cooperation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzetti, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mali', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T02:50:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon and Mark Mazzetti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazzetti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69316b83-a8e7-594d-8a4a-b4772af9a89f', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69316b83-a8e7-594d-8a4a-b4772af9a89f'}, {'abstract': 'The river remains open for business, thanks to a remarkable feat of engineering that effectively lowered the bottom of the channel by two feet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/us/mississippi-shipping-continues-despite-drought.html', 'snippet': 'The river remains open for business, thanks to a remarkable feat of engineering that effectively lowered the bottom of the channel by two feet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. LOUIS — For months along the Mississippi River here, the withering drought has caused record-breaking low water levels that have threatened to shut down traffic on the world’s largest navigable inland waterway.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/us/RIVER/RIVER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/18/us/RIVER/RIV