he man who is running them: the ultimate New Yorker, Senator Charles E. Schumer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s home states are Hawaii and Illinois, and his second inaugural will be held in Washington, D.C., of course. But the festivities at the Capitol on Monday will have a distinctly New York flair, thanks to the man who is running them: the ultimate New Yorker, Senator Charles E. Schumer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Inauguration Brought to You by the State of New York', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inaugurations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T13:09:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b061a09-160a-52c9-9551-94c60d55f477', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b061a09-160a-52c9-9551-94c60d55f477'}, {'abstract': 'What’s next after winning ‘Kaun Banega Crorepati?’ Opening a coaching institute for game show contestants, naturally.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/indian-who-wants-to-be-a-millionaire-winner-now-trains-others-to-win/', 'snippet': 'What’s next after winning ‘Kaun Banega Crorepati?’ Opening a coaching institute for game show contestants, naturally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those who dream of getting on the game show “Kaun Banega Crorepati” for its 50 million rupee-prize, one former winner, Anil Kumar Sinha, has a warning: This is no get-rich-quick scheme.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indian ‘Who Wants to Be a Millionaire’ Winner Trains Others to Win', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bihar (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T13:19:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amarnath Tewary', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amarnath', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tewary', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e251069-4aac-51f3-9c7c-04924f119433', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e251069-4aac-51f3-9c7c-04924f119433'}, {'abstract': 'Excluding one-time accounting charges, Morgan Stanley had a profit of 45 cents a share, beating estimates of analysts polled by Thomson Reuters of 27 cents a share. | Thomas Weisel, the banker behind Lance Armstrong, said he was unaware that the cyclist had been doping. | The firm that manages Michael S. Dell’s $16 billion fortune, MSD Capital, could play a role in a leveraged buyout of Dell. | So far, the new year has been kind to the euro zone countries most in need of debt financing.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/morgan-stanleys-profit-beats-estimates/', 'snippet': 'Excluding one-time accounting charges, Morgan Stanley had a profit of 45 cents a share, beating estimates of analysts polled by Thomson Reuters of 27 cents a share. | Thomas Weisel, the banker behind Lance Armstrong, said he was unaware that the c...', 'lead_paragraph': 'MORGAN STANLEY’S PROFIT BEATS ESTIMATES \xa0|\xa0 Morgan Stanley reported a fourth-quarter profit of $481 million or 25 cents a share, including charges. Excluding one-time accounting charges, the firm had a profit of 45 cents a share, beating estimates of analysts polled by Thomson Reuters of 27 cents a share. Revenue was $7 billion in the fourth quarter, up 23 percent from the period a year ago, when the firm reported a per-share loss of 15 cents. Morgan Stanley’s chief executive, James P. Gorman, said in a release on Friday that Morgan Stanley had reached a “pivot point” in its turnaround strategy. The conference call is at 10 a.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morgan Stanley’s Profit Beats Estimates', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T13:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/907a17f3-089a-59f3-a304-d0e24a17b273', 'word_count': 1642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/907a17f3-089a-59f3-a304-d0e24a17b273'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: Two connected annoyances bedevil a woman who had just moved: her new roommate’s cat and an unbolted air-conditioner.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/air-conditioner-bombs/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: Two connected annoyances bedevil a woman who had just moved: her new roommate’s cat and an unbolted air-conditioner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Air-Conditioner Bombs', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Conditioning', 'rank': 1, 'major