the-helicopters-somewhat-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'The case for Abenomics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shinzo Abe’s turn toward expansionary policies has, rather oddly, drawn fire from economists you might have expected to be supportive. Adam Posen — who taught me most of what I know about Japan — is against the fiscal stimulus, and wants a sole focus on monetary expansion to end deflation. Richard Koo likes the stimulus but wants to forget about the whole deflation thing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shinzo and the Helicopters (Somewhat Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T14:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8e89ec6-05a4-56a3-95d9-95049a121053', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8e89ec6-05a4-56a3-95d9-95049a121053'}, {'abstract': 'Hostages who escaped or were freed described gunshots ringing out during breakfast, followed by foreigners being separated from Algerians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/world/africa/scenes-of-terror-at-oil-field-hostages-bound-to-explosives.html', 'snippet': 'Hostages who escaped or were freed described gunshots ringing out during breakfast, followed by foreigners being separated from Algerians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The gunmen, dressed in fatigues and wearing turbans, stormed in well before dawn aboard pickup trucks, announcing their arrival with a burst of gunfire.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '