-year-old tech prodigy Aaron Swartz, who hanged himself last Friday, his grief-stricken father said that “Aaron did not commit suicide—he was killed by the government.” Legal bloggers have been debating a less literal version of that accusation: Did Mr. Swartz’s prosecutors go too far? Did their zeal border on bullying?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aaron Swartz and Prosecutorial Discretion', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swartz, Aaron (1986-2013)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T15:06:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lincoln Caplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lincoln', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19c3916c-b2a5-569b-b612-44e490ed887f', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19c3916c-b2a5-569b-b612-44e490ed887f'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation with George Archibald, who is known for persuading an endangered bird to lay an egg,', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/q-and-a-dances-with-cranes/', 'snippet': 'A conversation with George Archibald, who is known for persuading an endangered bird to lay an egg,', 'lead_paragraph': 'George Archibald is fondly known as “The Man who Dances with Cranes.” After helping to found the International Crane Foundation\xa0in Baraboo Wis., in 1973, Dr. Archibald earned his reputation for innovative captive breeding through his work with a young whooping crane named Tex in the late 70s. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A.: Dances With Cranes', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lakes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wetlands', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audubon Society, National', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T15:11:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e166cce3-b0a9-5acd-b907-3e48e22c5cb9', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e166cce3-b0a9-5acd-b907-3e48e22c5cb9'}, {'abstract': 'Preferring some creditors over others is at issue in the Argentine debt litigation. Yet, a similar idea is being suggested by some House Republicans.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/an-argentine-parallel/', 'snippet': 'Preferring some creditors over others is at issue in the Argentine debt litigation. Yet, a similar idea is being suggested by some House Republicans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If a corporation or an individual pays some creditors, but not others, bankruptcy lawyers call that a voidable preference. The preference allows the creditor to get more than it would in the collective bankruptcy process, so the extra bit can be recovered by the bankruptcy trustee.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Argentine Parallel', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T15:22:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5078cc57-0573-5f83-ad85-88a325996d9c', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5078cc57-0573-5f83-ad85-88a325996d9c'}, {'abstract': 'Negotiating experts consider how President Obama and Republican leaders might achieve goals without subjecting the markets, the American people and the global economy to a game of chicken.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/business/negotiation-experts-consider-how-to-reach-a-budget-deal.html', 'snippet': 'Negotiating experts consider how President Obama and Republican leaders might achieve goals without subjecting the markets, the American people and the global economy to a game of chicken.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Republican leadership blinked.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewar