m/2013/01/18/the-canal-to-nowhere/', 'snippet': 'Gen. Ulysses S. Grant’s disastrous plan to bypass Vicksburg, Miss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By January 1863, Union forces had successfully pounded their way down the Mississippi River to Memphis, and had pushed upstream from the Gulf of Mexico to Baton Rouge, La. The only footholds the Confederates had left on the river were Vicksburg, Miss., and Port Hudson, La. As long as they controlled those vital points, the eastern Confederacy and its western Trans-Mississippi Department remained connected. If the Union could capture them, the Confederacy would be split in two, its interior line disrupted and the Mississippi River opened once again to Northern trade. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Canal to Nowhere', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Canals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Ulysses S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vicksburg (Miss)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terry L. Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terry', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97d987ad-c88d-5c00-a425-e523ea4e7539', 'word_count': 2046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97d987ad-c88d-5c00-a425-e523ea4e7539'}, {'abstract': 'Having struggled with vocal problems, the songwriter John Mayer sang in public for the first time in nearly two years on Wednesday evening.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.