ac Events, the biggest promoter of electronic dance music in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, the growing popularity of electronic dance music, or E.D.M., brought out the deal-makers of the music industry as if drawn to the scent of prey. A handful of big dance promoters were sold, and one media mogul, Robert F.X. Sillerman, announced plans to build a $1 billion dance music empire. But the year ended without any big, market-defining deal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Music Companies Are Said to Pursue a Top Dance Promoter', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Live Nation Entertainment Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SFX Entertainment Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:41:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c27a62ac-7f26-5279-a21d-6394c48ab57e', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c27a62ac-7f26-5279-a21d-6394c48ab57e'}, {'abstract': 'The lithium-ion battery in the grounded Boeing 787 Dreamliner is different from the batteries used in electric vehicles.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/wheelies-the-chemistry-edition/', 'snippet': 'The lithium-ion battery in the grounded Boeing 787 Dreamliner is different from the batteries used in electric vehicles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The Chemistry Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alfa Romeo Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mazda Motor Corp', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:42:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4442130-157c-5e54-8b0f-6d356bf04fbb', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4442130-157c-5e54-8b0f-6d356bf04fbb'}, {'abstract': 'Citing environmental safety concerns, former officials of the Obama and Clinton administrations appeal to President Obama to suspend Shell’s plans to drill in the Chukchi and Beaufort seas.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/on-our-radar-a-call-to-halt-arctic-drilling/', 'snippet': 'Citing environmental safety concerns, former officials of the Obama and Clinton administrations appeal to President Obama to suspend Shell’s plans to drill in the Chukchi and Beaufort seas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Podesta, a former chief of staff in the Clinton administration, and Carol Browner, President Obama’s former climate change adviser, call on the president to block Shell’s drilling plans in the Arctic. “Following a series of mishaps and errors, as well as overwhelming weather conditions, it has become clear that there is no safe and responsible way to drill for oil and gas in the Arctic Ocean,” they write. [Bloomberg View]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: A Call to Halt Arctic Drilling', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66019042-faa8-5651-90a4-b6a495a3142f', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66019042-faa8-5651-90a4-b6a495a3142f'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff talks to The Times’s music-business reporter Ben Sisario on copyrights and the shifting power relationships between artists and their corporate minders.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/popcast-new-owners-for-vintage-hits/', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff talks to The Times’s music-business reporter Ben Sisario on copyrights and the shifting power relationships between artists and their corporate minders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'January 2013 is perhaps a month like any other to you. But in the music business, it’s the calm before the storm. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/arts/village/village-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/arts/village/village-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1516, 'width': 1596, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/arts/village/village-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/17/arts/village/village-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/17/arts/village/village-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: New Owners for Vintage Hits?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e80d60f9-835c-5ad4-b7af-3fa2b769cf6a', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e80d60f9-835c-5ad4-b7af-3fa2b769cf6a'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Ackroyd looks at England’s long, tumultuous formative period.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/foundation-by-peter-ackroyd.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Ackroyd looks at England’s long, tumultuous formative period.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Richard III, last of the Plantagenets, was slain at Bosworth Field in 1485, his body was said to have been taken to the church of Grey\xadfriars in Leicester, but after that nothing reliable is heard about what became of it. “He is the only English king, after the time of the Normans, who has never been placed within a royal tomb,” Peter Ackroyd writes in “Foundation,” his rambling, affectionate new history of the remote English past. In September, archaeologists from the University of Leicester dug up a parking lot at the long-razed Grey\xadfriars site and found, well preserved as if by a carefully wrapped shroud, the bones of a man whose skull had been hacked open and whose spine was curved, as Richard’s was said to have been. Sensation ensued among ordinary Britons, who flocked to comment on online forums and thronged the excavation site.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20OLSEN/20OLSEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20OLSEN/20OLSEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1816, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20OLSEN/20OLSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20OLSEN/20OLSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20OLSEN/20OLSEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Realm, This Engla Land', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Realm, This Engla Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ACKROYD, PETER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:46:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Olson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Olson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1a0b9e5-aba3-59ae-943f-7668ab9428e1', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1a0b9e5-aba3-59ae-943f-7668ab9428e1'}, {'abstract': 'The men in two novellas by Jim Harrison experience the triumph and torment of being alive, at 17 and at 60.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/the-river-swimmer-by-jim-harrison.html', 'snippet': 'The men in two novellas by Jim Harrison experience the triumph and torment of being alive, at 17 and at 60.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father, who was a scrupulous and seasoned engineer, said on one of our last road trips that there was a part of a person that was always 17. He’d been telling me another story about his youth on the farm, and he was grinning at the time. I could feel Jim Harrison grinning the same way in his glorious novella “The River Swimmer,” which celebrates the manifold lessons of that heady year by following the outsize adventures of 17-year-old Thad, a strong, dreamy farm boy who is inexorably drawn to water.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Carlson/0120-Carlson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Carlson/0120-Carlson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Carlson/0120-Carlson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Carlson/0120-Carlson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Carlson/0120-Carlson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reeling in the Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reeling in the Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrison, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:47:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Carlson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carlson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb51220d-8df3-5a30-a08a-d0fd3aef5ab8', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb51220d-8df3-5a30-a08a-d0fd3aef5ab8'}, {'abstract': 'In these essays, Adam Phillips explores the alternative “lives we could be leading but for some reason are not.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/missing-out-by-adam-phillips.html', 'snippet': 'In these essays, Adam Phillips explores the alternative “lives we could be leading but for some reason are not.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an interview that was published in Bomb magazine a couple of years ago, a journalist admitted to Adam Phillips, the British psychoanalyst and writer, that though he loved Phillips’s many books and essays, he could “never actually remember anything” that he read in them. Phillips was delighted. “That’s the reading experience I’ve always loved. Certainly, when people say to me, as they often have done, ‘I can’t remember anything afterward,’ I think, Great, that’s the point!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Heti/0120-Heti-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Heti/0120-Heti-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1618, 'width': 1383, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Heti/0120-Heti-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Heti/0120-Heti-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Heti/0120-Heti-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Selves', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second Selves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heti, Sheila', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phillips, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:49:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Heti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d576049-20ec-55b6-a8d9-436e01a6b234', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d576049-20ec-55b6-a8d9-436e01a6b234'}, {'abstract': 'Sheila Heti shares Adam Phillips’s interest not only in unlived lives but also in the lives of others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Sheila Heti shares Adam Phillips’s interest not only in unlived lives but also in the lives of others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sheila Heti, who reviews Adam Phillips’s “Missing Out: In Praise of the Unlived Life” in this week’s issue, shares his interest not only in unlived lives but also in the lives of others. “Writing fiction is a good way to inhabit other minds, if not other lives,” Heti said in an e-mail. Her most recent novel, “How Should a Person Be?,” is told from the perspective of Sheila, a young playwright eager to make art and attain fame, who is not to be confused with the author — at least not completely. For the Sheila of the novel, being famous is synonymous with being blessed. “In a religious context, you’re blessed because God watches over you, but in the secular context the biggest thing that could watch over you is everybody,” Heti explained. “She mistakenly imagines this ‘blessing’ will absolve her from being in difficult and painful relationships with others, and instead she can just kind of float along, untouched and beloved.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Upfront/0120-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 687, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/0120-Upfront/0120-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2010, 'width': 1349, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heti, Sheila', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d45944f-6f32-57f7-8392-4af3d4e85ac4', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d45944f-6f32-57f7-8392-4af3d4e85ac4'}, {'abstract': 'The relationship between poetry and mythology is central to Louise Glück’s “Poems 1962-2012.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/louise-glucks-metamorphoses.html', 'snippet': 'The relationship between poetry and mythology is central to Louise Glück’s “Poems 1962-2012.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poetry has always been the handmaiden of \xadmythology, and vice versa. Sometimes poets are in the business of collecting and tweaking existing myths, as with Ovid’s “Metamorphoses” and the Poetic Edda. Other times poetry applies a mythological glamour to stories and characters from history, legend or even other myths (the hero of the “Aeneid” is a minor character from the “Iliad”). Then there are poets who equate the idea of myth with the supposedly irrational essence of poetry itself. Here is Robert Graves in 1948: “No poet can hope to understand the nature of poetry unless he has had a vision of the Naked King crucified to the lopped oak, and watched the dancers, red-eyed from the acrid smoke of the sacrificial fires, . . . with a monotonous chant of ‘Kill! kill! kill!’ and ‘Blood! blood! blood!’ ” Which might sound more like a strip club picnic gone badly awry, but you get the idea.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20ORR/20ORR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 882, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20ORR/20ORR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1763, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20ORR/20ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20ORR/20ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20ORR/20ORR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Louise Glück’s Metamorphoses', 'kicker': 'On Poetry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Louise Glück’s Metamorphoses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gluck, Louise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d498d704-19da-5d31-bd8d-40b71944c90e', 'word_count': 1880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d498d704-19da-5d31-bd8d-40b71944c90e'}, {'abstract': 'Joyce Johnson explores Jack Kerouac’s heritage and early career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/the-voice-is-all-by-joyce-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'Joyce Johnson explores Jack Kerouac’s heritage and early career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January 1949, Jack Kerouac failed to appear for an afternoon date with a woman called Pauline. He had told Allen Ginsberg he planned to marry her — “the finest woman I’ll ever know” — once she had unshackled herself from her truck-driver husband, who, according to Joyce Johnson, was accustomed to “slapping her around to keep her in line.” In the meantime, Kerouac began an affair with Adele Morales (later to become the second Mrs. Norman Mailer). His failure to keep the rendezvous with Pauline, however, had nothing to do with affection for Adele; rather, he had overslept after a night of sex games with Luanne Henderson, whom Jack’s muse Neal Cassady had married when she was 15, and who, according to their friend Hal Chase, was “quite easy to get . . . into bed.” The tryst had been engineered by Cassady, who was hoping to watch, Johnson says, to show Luanne, by then 18, “how little she meant to him.” Two days later, Kerouac called on Ginsberg and found Luanne “covered with bruises from a beating Neal had given her.” Johnson describes Kerouac as “shocked” by the sight; nevertheless, “they all went out to hear bebop,” partly financed by money stolen by Cassady. In response to being jilted, Pauline confessed her affair to her husband, who tried to burn her on the stove. Kerouac described her in his journal as a “whore.” All the while, Ginsberg can be heard in the background: “How did we get here, angels?” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20CAMPBELL/20CAMPBELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20CAMPBELL/20CAMPBELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 1384, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20CAMPBELL/20CAMPBELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20CAMPBELL/20CAMPBELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20CAMPBELL/20CAMPBELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Road Ready', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Road Ready', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Joyce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerouac, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:53:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Campbell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Campbell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0ad331f-77a6-57ee-ad81-1112e0acfebe', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0ad331f-77a6-57ee-ad81-1112e0acfebe'}, {'abstract': 'The title story in Joyce Carol Oates’s collection involves two aspiring starlets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/black-dahlia-white-rose-by-joyce-carol-oates.html', 'snippet': 'The title story in Joyce Carol Oates’s collection involves two aspiring starlets.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Black Dahlia & White Rose” is Joyce Carol Oates’s 25th book of short stories. Her immense productivity — the torrent of novels and stories that began in the early 1960s — means that every release meets with the same question: Does she have anything fresh and urgent to reveal at this point in her long and very fine career? The answer, with her latest, may depend on how you regard Oates’s descriptions of older women.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20BOYAGODA/20BOYAGODA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20BOYAGODA/20BOYAGODA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1571, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20BOYAGODA/20BOYAGODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20BOYAGODA/20BOYAGODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20BOYAGODA/20BOYAGODA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fame and Misfortune', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fame and Misfortune', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyagoda, Randy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oates, Joyce Carol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:53:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f480bdeb-0563-5b3f-8db4-feaaf14ffe88', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f480bdeb-0563-5b3f-8db4-feaaf14ffe88'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Wilentz further explores Haiti, a country that continues to confound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/books/review/farewell-fred-voodoo-by-amy-wilentz.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Wilentz further explores Haiti, a country that continues to confound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What if conventional wisdom has it exactly wrong? What if Haiti, instead of being mired in retrograde customs and superstitions the developed world cast off centuries ago, is in fact ahead of the curve? What if, as Amy Wilentz posits in her excellent “Farewell, Fred Voodoo,” Haiti has always been the most modern of nations, at the forefront of every major historical trend since Columbus dropped anchor off the Hispaniolan coast?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20FOUNTAIN/20FOUNTAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 828, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20FOUNTAIN/20FOUNTAIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1656, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20FOUNTAIN/20FOUNTAIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20FOUNTAIN/20FOUNTAIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/books/review/20FOUNTAIN/20FOUNTAIN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A World of Its Own', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A World of Its Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilentz, Amy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T16:53:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Fountain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fountain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/da4be68e-3291-5065-b6c4-caa8edfaa1a0', 'word_count': 1268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da4be68e-3291-5065-b6c4-caa8edfaa1a0'}, {'abstract': 'People in the cycling and antidoping communities said they were mostly unmoved by Lance Armstrong’s interview with Oprah Winfrey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/sports/cycling/those-wronged-by-lance-armstrong-see-little-right-in-interview.html', 'snippet': 'People in the cycling and antidoping communities said they were mostly unmoved by Lance Armstrong’s interview with Oprah Winfrey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whatever its impact on the broader public, the first of Lance Armstrong’s two nights of televised confession appeared to have little positive effect on the cycling and antidoping communities.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/sports/cycling/19cycling-armstrong2-img/19cycling-armstrong2-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/sports/cycling/19cycling-armstrong2-img/19cycling-armstrong2-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': No