/', 'snippet': 'Times critics review “Broken City,” “Luv” and “Greenwich Village.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s video, Times critics look at the crime drama “Broken City” with Mark Wahlberg, “Luv” with Common and the documentary “Greenwich Village: Music That Defined a Generation.” See all of this week’s reviews here. And watch more This Week’s Movies videos here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: Jan. 18', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wahlberg, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/979cde3d-974a-5673-a41e-4512a604efd3', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/979cde3d-974a-5673-a41e-4512a604efd3'}, {'abstract': 'Recent federal rulings should and will motivate responsible sovereign debtors and private creditors to resolve their differences at the negotiating table, and not in the courtroom, the author contends.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/courts-are-right-to-hold-argentina-to-equal-debt-treatment/', 'snippet': 'Recent federal rulings should and will motivate responsible sovereign debtors and private creditors to resolve their differences at the negotiating table, and not in the courtroom, the author contends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harry Tether is a retired managing director at JPMorgan Chase.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Courts Are Right to Hold Argentina to Equal Debt Treatment', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:08:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harry Tether', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tether', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44afc1f4-7968-512b-89ea-d2a94f31e174', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44afc1f4-7968-512b-89ea-d2a94f31e174'}, {'abstract': 'An e-commerce merchant dedicates itself to bespoke brick-and-mortar shopping experiences at luxury boutiques across the globe.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/site-to-be-seen-luxup/', 'snippet': 'An e-commerce merchant dedicates itself to bespoke brick-and-mortar shopping experiences at luxury boutiques across the globe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If James Corsellis and company have their way, Luxup.com is going to make it nearly impossible for anyone truly hungry for haute fashion not to score exclusive deals and memorable shopping experiences — long before they’re available to the masses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Site to Be Seen | Luxup', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:11:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Dishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85f8b77-b228-5ffb-9d68-f91d8d215932', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85f8b77-b228-5ffb-9d68-f91d8d215932'}, {'abstract': 'The Paris men’s shows are already underway, but here’s a look back at some of the tweets from the Milan shows that got our attention.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/tweets-from-milan-mens-fashion-week-2/', 'snippet': 'The Paris men’s shows are already underway, but here’s a look back at some of the tweets from the Milan shows that got our attention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Paris men’s shows are already underway, but here’s a look back at some of the tweets from the Milan shows that got our attention. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tweets From Milan Men’s Fashion Week', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:12:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ec4ecb2-6425-5ded-9fc6-ee13fbd7795f', 'word_count': 26, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ec4ecb2-6425-5ded-9fc6-ee13fbd7795f'}, {'abstract': 'Iman Shumpert’s return from a knee injury Thursday gave the Knicks a booster shot of youth and defense.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/shumperts-return-boosts-knicks-defense-depth/', 'snippet': 'Iman Shumpert’s return from a knee injury Thursday gave the Knicks a booster shot of youth and defense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Thursday’s Knicks game felt a little retro, it may have been because of\xa0 Iman Shumpert’s appearance in his first game of the season. These days, the second-year guard keeps his hair in the style of a high-top fade, which was ubiquitous in the late ’80s and early ’90s. Shumpert’s No. 21 was previously worn by Gerald Wilkins, who sported a similar haircut. In fact, Wilkins was nicknamed Doug after rapper Doug E. Fresh because both had high-top fades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/sports/basketball/18shumpert_dribbleblog/18shumpert_dribbleblog-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/sports/basketball/18shumpert_dribbleblog/18shumpert_dribbleblog-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1634, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/sports/basketball/18shumpert_dribbleblog/18shumpert_dribbleblog-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/18/sports/basketball/18shumpert_dribbleblog/18shumpert_dribbleblog-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/sports/basketball/18shumpert_dribbleblog/18shumpert_dribbleblog-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shumpert’s Return Boosts Knicks’ Defense, Depth', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kidd, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shumpert, Iman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Kurylo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurylo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5101c4c8-13e0-50c3-9e4d-8a4e01d31d51', 'word_count': 389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5101c4c8-13e0-50c3-9e4d-8a4e01d31d51'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of culture events this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/arts/jan-20-26.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of culture events this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOUGH Richard Rodgers and Oscar Hammerstein II had many Broadway successes — “Oklahoma” and “South Pacific” among them — their 1957 musical version of “Cinderella” was made for television and, until now, has never been produced on Broadway. But “RODGERS & HAMMERSTEIN’S CINDERELLA,” while promising to honor the soaring romance of the original, features a new book by the witty Douglas Carter Beane (“ Xanadu,” “Lysistrata Jones”) that includes some modern touches and, we can assume, a quirky twist or two. (The show’s Web site features the tag line “Glass slippers are so back.”) Laura Osnes (“Bonnie and Clyde”) plays the title role — Julie Andrews did the honors in the 1957 version — and Santino Fontana (“The Importance of Being Earnest”) will portray her rather neurotic prince. The rest of the company includes Harriet Harris as Cinderella’s stepmother and Victoria Clark as her fairy godmother, with Mark Brokaw (“The Lyons”) directing. Previews begin Friday. Opens March 3, Broadway Theater, 1681 Broadway, (212) 239-6200; cinderellaonbroadway.com .STEVEN McELROY ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1414, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/20/arts/20GUIDE2_SPAN/20GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan. 20 — 26', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c174d89-abfe-5366-b320-fc1fd47b7191', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c174d89-abfe-5366-b320-fc1fd47b7191'}, {'abstract': 'Busted by the two-way mirror.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/01/20/magazine/look-check-me-out.html', 'snippet': 'Busted by the two-way mirror.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Busted by the two-way mirror.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Check Me Out', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mirrors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:23:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7935d4ce-b69e-51f6-9c71-e2c54bbcb3fd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7935d4ce-b69e-51f6-9c71-e2c54bbcb3fd'}, {'abstract': 'Busted by the two-way mirror.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/magazine/new-yorkers-caught-checking-themselves-out.html', 'snippet': 'Busted by the two-way mirror.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The passers-by who lingered to adjust a collar or smooth down unruly hair never knew that a photographer was on the other side of the two-way mirror, dressed in black clothes, a ski mask and gloves so his own reflection wouldn’t show up in the pictures he was taking. According to the photographer, Brad Farwell, the pedestrians he observed at four different Manhattan locations — the Bowery, Midtown, NoLIta and the Lower East Side — were much the same. But the time they spent looking at themselves differed, depending on the day. Sunday Downtown was a time for leisurely strolling and reflecting on what they saw in the mirror. On a weekday in Midtown, though, “the looks were the most unguarded, because they were so much quicker,” Farwell says. “The checking themselves out wasn’t about ego. It was more of an appraisal. They were sort of looking at themselves as they really were.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Yorkers Caught Checking Themselves Out', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Through the Looking Glass', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:23:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83a51187-e9c7-5081-97a0-9b96172e5203', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83a51187-e9c7-5081-97a0-9b96172e5203'}, {'abstract': 'Columnists found Lance Armstrong’s first of two confessional  interviews strangely lacking in revelations or steps toward redemption.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/sports/more-of-the-same-from-lance-armstrong-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'Columnists found Lance Armstrong’s first of two confessional  interviews strangely lacking in revelations or steps toward redemption.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So now that the Great Lance Confession has finally been broadcast, the big remaining question is not whether you will go back for a second helping of arrogance soufflé in Friday night’s not-so-dramatic conclusion of Oprah Winfrey’s two-night bonanza (here’s betting you won’t). No, the question is: What exactly did all of that accomplish? Do you know anything more Armstrong-related than you did before, even if you watched every disingenuous minute of it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'