article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d08ad913-3ede-51aa-91fc-c946a487116e', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d08ad913-3ede-51aa-91fc-c946a487116e'}, {'abstract': 'Kentucky has far and away the most firearm background checks per person in the country.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/18/armed-in-kentucky/', 'snippet': 'Kentucky has far and away the most firearm background checks per person in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I went to a gun store in Lexington, Ky., recently to shoot a variety of guns, I knew I was in gun country. What I hadn’t quite appreciated was the extent to which I had wound up in the beating heart of gun country. But after I got back to New York, my new friend Andrew Silton, a former financial consultant who writes a terrific blog, Meditations on Money Management — and whose analysis of Cerberus and the Freedom Group I quoted in my December 21 column — sent me some eye-opening statistics. He compiled a list of \xa0the 50 states ranked according to how many background checks for firearm purchases had taken place in each state. At the very top is Kentucky, with 15 million checks over the last decade — in a state with only 4.4 million people. That is 3.41 searches per person. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Armed in Kentucky', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lexington (Ky)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T17:47:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8790478-b0a9-5365-97a7-1e114a2d708a', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8790478-b0a9-5365-97a7-1e114a2d708a'}, {'abstract': 'Sooner or later there will come a point where you will need to clean a leek. Melissa Clark shows how.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/dining/100000001994515/cleaning-leeks.html', 'snippet': 'Sooner or later there will come a point where you will need to clean a leek. Melissa Clark shows how.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sooner or later there will come a point where you will need to clean a leek. Melissa Clark shows how.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/dining/video-clarkleeks/video-clarkleeks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/18/dining/video-clarkleeks/video-clarkleeks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/dining/video-clarkleeks/video-clarkleeks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/18/dining/video-clarkleeks/video-clarkleeks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'su