, 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-18T21:33:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Gregor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gregor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a8a3d86-8f4c-58d7-94ee-0d151f71b034', 'word_count': 1451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a8a3d86-8f4c-58d7-94ee-0d151f71b034'}, {'abstract': 'What to do about strangers who try to quiet a baby, dogs in the workplace, table manners and affection in a text message.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/fashion/a-parents-cry-for-help-social-qs.html', 'snippet': 'What to do about strangers who try to quiet a baby, dogs in the workplace, table manners and affection in a text message.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My youngest child is 3 months old and cries much more than my other two young children did. She is often fussy when we run errands, but it is not always possible to pick her up, which she wants. Still, people offer advice about how to quiet her or try to do so themselves by cooing at her or putting her binky in her mouth. I am sure they are trying to help, but I am offended that strangers think they can soothe my daughter when I cannot. What should I do? Porter, Bronxville, N.Y.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag