give a great gift to the city. Sacrificing their winter breaks for intensive coaching, they provide a week of free concerts in the school’s Paul Hall that are valuable reminders of the core values of chamber music: intimacy, responsiveness, vibrancy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/arts/19CHAMBER/19CHAMBER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bang the Drum Relentlessly, and the Keyboards, Too', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bang the Drum Relentlessly, And the Keyboards, Too', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juilliard School', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-19T01:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f94cc1e-163c-5595-93ce-2243b57813e2', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f94cc1e-163c-5595-93ce-2243b57813e2'}, {'abstract': 'The state’s use of capital punishment has been arbitrary and capricious. It is time to get the law off the books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/opinion/maryland-and-the-death-penalty.html', 'snippet': 'The state’s use of capital punishment has been arbitrary and capricious. It is time to get the law off the books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maryland’s governor, Martin O’Malley, chose Jan. 15, the birthday of Martin Luther King Jr., to announce his determination to repeal the state’s law allowing capital punishment. “Hate multiplies hate, violence multiplies violence,” he said, quoting Dr. King. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maryland and the Death Penalty', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Maryland and the Death Penalty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Malley, Martin J", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-19T01:43:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6656535-b23b-590f-bde8-83ddd453b7f5', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6656535-b23b-590f-bde8-83ddd453b7f5'}, {'abstract': 'The retreat in Williamsburg, Va. was most notable for Representative Paul D. Ryan’s front-and-center role, belying expectations that he would lie low as he mulls a 2016 presidential bid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/us/politics/ryan-returns-to-spotlight-at-republican-retreat.html', 'snippet': 'The retreat in Williamsburg, Va. was most notable for Representative Paul D. Ryan’s front-and-center role, belying expectations that he would lie low as he mulls a 2016 presidential bid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WILLIAMSBURG, Va. — When House Republicans arrived from the nation’s capital in the colonial capital this week, they were greeted by a brigade in traditional garb. Men in tricornered hats twittered away (on the fife), and three founding fathers — Thomas Jefferson, Patrick Henry and George Washington — stopped by to give speeches.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ryan Returns to Spotlight at House Republican Retreat', 'kicker': 'Reporter’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ryan Returns to Spotlight at House Republican Retreat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congress', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-19T01:43:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34e11d2e-b3ad-59f1-813a-550a15e5fc3e', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34e11d2e-b3ad-59f1-813a-550a15e5fc3e'}, {'abstract': 'Though President François Hollande of France has the support of African nations in the region, his army’s fight to preserve a country divided leaves many saying he has overreached.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/19/world/europe/hollandes-intervention-in-mali-raises-concerns.html', 'snippet': 'Though President François Hollande of France has the support of African nations in the region, his army’s fight to preserve a country divided leaves many saying he has overreached.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — This was not the war President François Hollande wanted.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/19/world/assess1/assess1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/19/world/assess1/assess1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'x