ck Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/554fb885-4128-5950-be2a-221ddf62b9e5', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/554fb885-4128-5950-be2a-221ddf62b9e5'}, {'abstract': 'Shannon Watts of Zionsville, Ind., has created a group modeled after Mothers Against Drunk Driving that is planning to march across the Brooklyn Bridge to call for stronger legislation against firearms.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/gun-control-advocate-looking-for-a-million-good-moms/', 'snippet': 'Shannon Watts of Zionsville, Ind., has created a group modeled after Mothers Against Drunk Driving that is planning to march across the Brooklyn Bridge to call for stronger legislation against firearms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shannon Watts of Zionsville, Ind., did not want her 12-year-old son to hear about the Sandy Hook massacre. The memory of what had happened last summer after the movie theater shootings in Aurora, Colo., was too raw.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gun-Control Advocate Looking for a Million Good Moms', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gun-Control Advocate Says She Is Looking For a Million Good Moms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watts, Shannon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'One Million Moms for Gun Control', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T16:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02198dfd-e33b-505c-a3d5-30e438035f9a', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02198dfd-e33b-505c-a3d5-30e438035f9a'}, {'abstract': 'Though HBO normally doesn’t offer advance screenings of documentaries to their subjects, the cable network made an exception for the teenage star of “Life According to Sam,” who has progeria, which caues rapid aging in children.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/one-viewers-vote-that-counts-more-than-others/', 'snippet': 'Though HBO normally doesn’t offer advance screenings of documentaries to their subjects, the cable network made an exception for the teenage star of “Life According to Sam,” who has progeria, which caues rapid aging in children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah — Sheila Nevins, the inimitable president of HBO Documentary Films, which has (count ’em) six movies playing Sundance this year, did not mince words about “Life According to Sam.” Her initial reaction to making the film, about children who suffer from a disfiguring and fatal disease?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Viewer’s Vote That Counts More Than Others', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T16:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4941806-7cd9-544e-9218-a5e8273a6d34', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4941806-7cd9-544e-9218-a5e8273a6d34'}, {'abstract': 'But “Mama,” a low-budget thriller from Universal Pictures starring Jessica Chastain, exceeded expectations on the way to No. 1 at North American theaters over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/schwarzenegger-stumbles-at-the-box-office/', 'snippet': 'But “Mama,” a low-budget thriller from Universal Pictures starring Jessica Chastain, exceeded expectations on the way to No. 1 at North American theaters over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What if the Terminator tried to make a movie comeback and almost nobody cared? Arnold Schwarzenegger faced that question over the weekend as “The Last Stand,” his first star vehicle since returning to Hollywood from politics, took in an abysmal $6.3 million at the North American box office. That total was good enough only for 10th place at theaters. Mr. Schwarzenegger found box-office gold time and again as the Terminator, but now Lionsgate finds itself counting on his overseas fans to make up for the roughly $45 million it spent to make this new movie.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schwarzenegger Stumbles at the Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Schwarzenegger Stumbles at the Box Office', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwarzenegger, Arnold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T17:40:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a9e2b1a-0deb-56dd-821c-5fbe493e8b2a', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a9e2b1a-0deb-56dd-821c-5fbe493e8b2a'}, {'abstract': 'I wish I could buy this argument.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/inequality-and-recovery/', 'snippet': 'I wish I could buy this argument.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Stiglitz has an Opinionator piece arguing that inequality is a big factor in our slow recovery. Joe is an insanely great economist, so everything he says should be taken seriously. And given my political views and general concerns about inequality, I’d like to agree.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality and Recovery', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T17:49:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/938eb7c6-6332-5e15-b422-85be4180e485', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/938eb7c6-6332-5e15-b422-85be4180e485'}, {'abstract': 'Notes on the numbers.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/obama-and-redistribution/', 'snippet': 'Notes on the numbers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some notes for myself: how much impact have Obama’s policies actually had on current and prospective inequality?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and Redistribution', '