 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19e57a85-b63a-53f0-a9bf-821c6ce51375', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19e57a85-b63a-53f0-a9bf-821c6ce51375'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Gelfand creates a 28 Across.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/20/sharper-than-90-degrees/', 'snippet': 'Susan Gelfand creates a 28 Across.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Most people probably don’t look at crossword puzzles the way I do, but I’m always amazed at how things in the grid can jump out at me and imitate life. For example, I was CHOWing down on some BAGELS and lox as I solved, and boom! There’s my BAGEL at 5 Across.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharper Than 90 Degrees', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T23:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/931f5518-ef39-5dd1-ad2f-cf050a7adcde', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/931f5518-ef39-5dd1-ad2f-cf050a7adcde'}, {'abstract': 'Environmental groups and others are concerned about a corporate headquarters that is to rise across the Hudson River from the Cloisters, altering the scenic view from that museum of medieval treasures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/21/arts/design/the-cloisters-view-is-threatened-by-lg-electronics-offices.html', 'snippet': 'Environmental groups and others are concerned about a corporate headquarters that is to rise across the Hudson River from the Cloisters, altering the scenic view from that museum of medieval treasures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After John D. Rockefeller Jr. donated land for the Cloisters museum in northern Manhattan, he went a step further in the 1930s and  bought the cliffs across the Hudson River in New Jersey to preserve the museum’s pristine view of the Palisades.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/arts-refer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/arts-refer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/arts-refer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/arts-refer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1282, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/cloisters3_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/cloisters3_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cloisters3_span/cloisters3_span-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Timeless View From the Cloisters Faces a Modern Intrusion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Timeless View From the Cloisters Faces a Modern Intrusion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LG Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Palisades (Hudson River)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cloisters, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Palisades Interstate Park of NY and NJ', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T23:01:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a77d3df6-59d9-5d07-aac7-0e9cb9bae1a1', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a77d3df6-59d9-5d07-aac7-0e9cb9bae1a1'}, {'abstract': 'Over three nights at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, John Cale celebrated Nico and performed his 1973 album, “Paris 1919,” in full.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/21/arts/music/two-john-cale-programs-at-the-brooklyn-academy-of-music.html', 'snippet': 'Over three nights at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, John Cale celebrated Nico and performed his 1973 album, “Paris 1919,” in full.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A drone, sustained and hypnotic, opened John Cale’s first program at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, “Life Along the Borderline: A Tribute to Nico,” on Wednesday. It enveloped Nico’s “Frozen Warnings,” sung by Mr. Cale and Meshell Ndegeocello, to start a concert of Nico’s songs featuring a dozen guest performers. Another drone — Mr. Cale’s sawing, insistent electric viola part in the Velvet Underground’s sadomasochistic “Venus in Furs” (written by Lou Reed) — ended his second program there on Friday (repeated Saturday), featuring Mr. Cale’s own songs backed by the Wordless Music Orchestra, including a full performance of his 1973 album, “Paris 1919.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/21/arts/cale1/cale1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Droning, Rocking, Leading and Paying Tribute to a Dead Collaborator', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Droning, Rocking, Leading and Paying Tribute to a Dead Collaborator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cale, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-20T23:06:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f473b0c-cab6-550f-b562-9cc247ae67e1', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f473b0c-cab6-550f-b562-9cc247ae67e1'}, {'abstract': 'The original television Batmobile was sold at auction on Saturday for $4.2 million, according to The Associated Press.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/21/arts/television/batmobile-is-sold.html', 'snippet': 'The original television Batmobile was sold at auction on Saturday for $4.2 million, according to The Associated Press.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The original television Batmobile was sold at auction on Saturday for $4.2 million, according to The Associated Press. Copies of that crime-fighting car — from the 1960s television show starring Adam West as Batman and Burt Ward as Robin — have previously sold for $50,000 to $150,000, but the Barrett-Jackson auction in Scottsdale, Ariz., was the first time the original had been put up for sale. The car had previously been in the possession of George Barris, the automobile c