ans, we are made for this moment, and we will seize it — so long as we seize it together. For we, the people, understand that our country cannot succeed when a shrinking few do very well and a growing many barely make it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Tuesday, Jan. 22, 2013', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T03:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/54dafa7b-8079-5c5d-8f64-2940728acac2', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54dafa7b-8079-5c5d-8f64-2940728acac2'}, {'abstract': 'Even with his savings gone, Charles Daubek Jr. still lives in the Westchester County house his parents bought in 1944.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/nyregion/helping-a-world-war-ii-veteran-live-independently.html', 'snippet': 'Even with his savings gone, Charles Daubek Jr. still lives in the Westchester County house his parents bought in 1944.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He was on a 10-day furlough when he first saw the house: a boxy two-story home on Heath Place in woodsy Hastings-on-Hudson, Westchester County. He had been stationed in England when his parents, Charles and Elizabeth Daubek, wrote to say they had saved, scrimped and borrowed to buy it — for $5,000. Now, Pvt. Charles Daubek Jr. stood on the front porch, about to surprise his mother.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/22/nyregion/22NEEDIEST/22NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'l