short supply. Subject-verb agreement, basic as it is, remains a daily ch...', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the rare occasions when I’m stuck for an After Deadline topic, there are a few perennials I can always rely on, with examples easy to find. Dangling modifiers are never in short supply. Subject-verb agreement, basic as it is, remains a daily challenge. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Favorite Grammar Gaffes', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T13:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07ec8b24-ec08-5737-b014-14103a24a16e', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07ec8b24-ec08-5737-b014-14103a24a16e'}, {'abstract': 'It’s that time of year again in Davos, Switzerland, as the World Economic Forum gets under way. | Aerospace is the latest frontier for the Chinese government’s investment of its foreign reserves. | Many in the private equity sector acknowledge that tax rules are likely to change. | Mary Jo White, a former United States attorney, may be tapped to lead the S.E.C.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/inside-davos/', 'snippet': 'It’s that time of year again in Davos, Switzerland, as the World Economic Forum gets under way. | Aerospace is the latest frontier for the Chinese government’s investment of its foreign reserves. | Many in the private equity sector acknowledge tha...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s that time of year again — when business and policy leaders descend on Davos, Switzerland, for several days of networking, meetings and celebrity spotting at the World Economic Forum. The panel discussions will be full of speakers making provocative predictions, but, please, don’t believe everything you hear, Andrew Ross Sorkin writes in the DealBook column. “The predictions that have emanated from Davos always have a ring of plausibility to them, in part because of the credibility of the speakers. But all too often they fall short.” Some have been directionally accurate, but others — including a couple by Bill Gates — have not panned out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside Davos', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T13:11:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0890757-f4db-5f7d-b784-ed6b2b3de27a', 'word_count': 1605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0890757-f4db-5f7d-b784-ed6b2b3de27a'}, {'abstract': 'While global capital remains a driving force of our age, it won’t solve big world issues. For those tasks, we must rely either on the nation state or on international institutions whose effectiveness is regularly questioned by the Davos crowd.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/at-davos-crisis-is-new-normal/', 'snippet': 'While global capital remains a driving force of our age, it won’t solve big world issues. For those tasks, we must rely either on the nation state or on international institutions whose effectiveness is regularly questioned by the Davos crowd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — In certain ways, the very setting of the World Economic Forum reflects the restless, challenged state of human affairs. Our footing is uncertain, as on this ski resort’s slithery streets, and we have steep slopes to climb, as the Magic Mountain will remind the global elite this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Davos, Crisis Is the New Normal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T13:12:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b500349a-c9ec-546e-8d32-b23dc0aa4f44', 'word_count': 1356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b500349a-c9ec-546e-8d32-b23dc0aa4f44'}, {'abstract': 'After a record number of submissions in the foreign film category and most of the European awards now accounted for, how does the competition for “Amour” stack up?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/oscar-anticipation-in-the-subtitle-category/', 'snippet': 'After a record number of submissions in the foreign film category and most of the European awards now accounted for, how does the competition for “Amour” stack up?', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS—With the multiple nominations for “Lincoln,” “Django Unchained” and “Zero Dark Thirty,” Europeans may see this year’s Academy Award field as something of a celebration of American history, in the age of Obama. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20