alue': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T16:46:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/602893bf-6f90-5856-a22e-8a9f4022c279', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/602893bf-6f90-5856-a22e-8a9f4022c279'}, {'abstract': 'The Fisher Body Craftsman’s Guild, an annual car-building and design competition sponsored by General Motors, provided scholarships for many boys.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/a-reunion-of-car-model-makers/', 'snippet': 'The Fisher Body Craftsman’s Guild, an annual car-building and design competition sponsored by General Motors, provided scholarships for many boys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — By 1953, Anthony Joslin’s parents had saved $3,000, and earmarked it for sending their son to college. But Mr.\xa0 Joslin was able to pay for his own tuition, room and board at North Carolina State University after winning a scholarship for the 1/12-scale car model he designed and built.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reunion of Car Model Makers', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T16:47:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edsall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/852f16b1-01bb-5d46-ad32-b356008cdc86', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852f16b1-01bb-5d46-ad32-b356008cdc86'}, {'abstract': 'On Friday, officials at the Metropolitan Museum of Art announced that attendance at its 14-and-a-half-month-old galleries devoted to the arts of the Islamic world had topped the one-million mark — meaning an average of about 2,550 people a day, and 14 percent of the total attendance for the museum during the same period.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/met-reports-one-millionth-visitor-to-new-islamic-galleries/', 'snippet': 'On Friday, officials at the Metropolitan Museum of Art announced that attendance at its 14-and-a-half-month-old galleries devoted to the arts of the Islamic world had topped the one-million mark — meaning an average of about 2,550 people a day, an...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 14-and-a-half months since the Metropolitan Museum of Art opened its new galleries devoted to the arts of the Islamic world — which it calls the galleries for the Art of the Arab Lands, Turkey, Iran, Central Asia and Later South Asia — they have become one of the Met’s most popular attractions. On Friday, officials there reported that attendance at the galleries had topped the one-million mark — meaning an average of about 2,550 people a day, and 14 percent of the total attendance for the museum during the same period. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Met Reports One Millionth Visitor to New Islamic Galleries', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Met Islamic Galleries Draw Millionth Visitor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T16:55:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c1f6bd2-51ef-5f4c-9531-f73b8a244683', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c1f6bd2-51ef-5f4c-9531-f73b8a244683'}, {'abstract': '“My Micro NY” will create a building of 55 micro-units each measuring between 250 and 370 square feet, with 40 percent of the units priced to be affordable, the mayor’s office said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/city-unveils-winner-of-tiny-apartment-competition/', 'snippet': '“My Micro NY” will create a building of 55 micro-units each measuring between 250 and 370 square feet, with 40 percent of the units priced to be affordable, the mayor’s office said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 5:42 p.m. | The apartment of New York City’s future, as the city imagines it, has all the amenities of modern life: wheelchair-accessible bathroom, a full kitchen, space for entertaining and access to a gym, communal lounge, front and back porches and a rooftop garden — all in 250 to 370 square feet.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/nyregion/22cityroom-micro1/22cityroom-micro1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'arti