ral Motors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T18:42:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68119757-d592-5e87-a35f-36a15552be77', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68119757-d592-5e87-a35f-36a15552be77'}, {'abstract': 'An ounce of prevention; an ounce of compassion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/magazine/must-i-get-a-flu-shot.html', 'snippet': 'An ounce of prevention; an ounce of compassion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The calculus of getting a flu shot is pretty straightforward: If I don’t, I could get sick — and potentially get a bunch of other people sick too. I work in an open-air office, eat in crowded restaurants and take the subway. But here’s the thing: I don’t want a flu shot. I don’t feel right about it. My mother, an M.D., who now practices alternative medicine, was — and is — anti-vaccine. Like a kid inheriting his parents’ faith, I grew up with a strong sense of that value system. Am I ethically obligated to get a flu shot? BEN REININGA, NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width