e': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:08:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez and Emma G. Fitzsimmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Fitzsimmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acdd9cfc-f80f-54e0-87e8-c13f6fc5f93e', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acdd9cfc-f80f-54e0-87e8-c13f6fc5f93e'}, {'abstract': 'The violent drama averaged 10.4 million viewers in the coveted 18- to 49-year-old audience, and delivered a bit of good news to Fox after a dismal fall season for the network.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/the-following-on-fox-opens-with-good-ratings/', 'snippet': 'The violent drama averaged 10.4 million viewers in the coveted 18- to 49-year-old audience, and delivered a bit of good news to Fox after a dismal fall season for the network.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a dismal fall, the Fox network finally got some good news Monday with the premiere of its highest-profile project of the year, “The Following.” Initial ratings for the intense, hyperviolent drama were solid, especially on a tough competitive night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Following’ on Fox Opens With Good Ratings', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings (Audience Measurement)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:13:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfdec742-0b8c-5625-8744-94e0db951d0f', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfdec742-0b8c-5625-8744-94e0db951d0f'}, {'abstract': 'Two defendants convicted in insider trading cases face efforts by Wall Street firms to claw back legal fees and other expenses.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/how-companies-can-sue-defendants-in-insider-trading-cases/', 'snippet': 'Two defendants convicted in insider trading cases face efforts by Wall Street firms to claw back legal fees and other expenses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Insider trading was once said to be a victimless crime. Yet, many parties can claim that they were harmed by such actions, including those who were on the other side of the actual trades, the stock market, the corporation whose shares were involved in the inside trades or even a hedge fund used as the vehicle for the trading. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Companies Can Sue Defendants in Insider Trading Cases', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gupta, Rajat K', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:17:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b64404bd-a84e-5fc4-9961-5645e0100b4a', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b64404bd-a84e-5fc4-9961-5645e0100b4a'}, {'abstract': 'The tendency to puff ourselves up is a conspicuous and enduring symbol of our democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/not-so-exceptional/', 'snippet': 'The tendency to puff ourselves up is a conspicuous and enduring symbol of our democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American presidential inaugurations, which some critics deride as coronations — the cannons! the fancy dress balls! — are, for others, “the most conspicuous and enduring symbol of our democracy.” That’s how Senator Lamar Alexander put it Monday, in his brief speech shortly before the president’s swearing in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not So Exceptional', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALEXANDER, LAMAR', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:26:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00222201-6f33-5c15-8160-8cf1f5784b05', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00222201-6f33-5c15-8160-8cf1f5784b05'}, {'abstract': 'Judy Battista, the N.F.L. reporter for The Times, will answer questions from readers about the Super Bowl and other topics.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/have-an-n-f-l-question-judy-battista-has-answers-7/', 'snippet': 'Judy Battista, the N.F.L. reporter for The Times, will answer questions from readers about the Super Bowl and other topics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Has Joe Flacco convinced any remaining doubters? And Colin Kaepernick? How do the ravens prepare for the 49ers (and vice versa)? Judy Battista, the N.F.L. reporter for The Times, will answer questions from readers about the Super Bowl and other football topics. Use the comment field below for your questions. Answers will appear next week on The Fifth Down.Comments for this feature are now closed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have an N.F.L. Question? Judy Battista Has Answers', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:27:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ec7a802-df76-5f78-b64c-c8db63b43254', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ec7a802-df76-5f78-b64c-c8db63b43254'}, {'abstract': 'My qualifications for friendship are high. I don’t tell you when to go to bed. You don’t tell me when you’re done pooping. So my children were not my friends — until now.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/children-are-not-our-friends-until-they-are/', 'snippet': 'My qualifications for friendship are high. I don’t tell you when to go to bed. You don’t tell me when you’re done pooping. So my children were not my friends — until now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my children were little, I was always a bit baffled by parents who talked about being friends with their children. Maybe I expect too much from my friends, but I like to hang out with people who read chapter books and bathe without being told. I’m big on reciprocity in my friendships — we exchange views and experience and occasionally good advice. I don’t tell you when to go to bed. You don’t tell me when you’re done pooping.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Children Are Not Our Friends (Until They Are)', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:35:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Blake', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blake', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13c3da55-31df-5409-9953-7504941e347a', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13c3da55-31df-5409-9953-7504941e347a'}, {'abstract': 'The focus of Chef David Myers’s latest restaurant? A globe-trotting update on seasonal California cooking.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/table-talk-hinoki-the-bird-in-los-angeles/', 'snippet': 'The focus of Chef David Myers’s latest restaurant? A globe-trotting update on seasonal California cooking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first thing you notice upon entering the new Los Angeles restaurant Hinoki & the Bird is the hinoki, a Japanese cedar wood that lines the walls and perfumes the air with a crisp, citrusy bouquet. Veneer-thin sheets of it — set on fire to infuse black cod filets with their smoke — waft like incense as the plates cross the room, sending curious noses skyward in their wake.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Table Talk | Hinoki & the Bird in Los Angeles', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Table Talk: Around the World and Back To Los Angeles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:47:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47f9184a-e817-5883-ab86-c10b73cbe34a', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47f9184a-e817-5883-ab86-c10b73cbe34a'}, {'abstract': 'The Scottish novelist Ali Smith’s slim new book, “Artful,” is equal parts ghost story and academic treatise: a set of lectures by a dead writer placed, like the filling of a dumpling, inside a love story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/books/artful-by-ali-smith-a-novel-with-a-ghost.html', 'snippet': 'The Scottish novelist Ali Smith’s slim new book, “Artful,” is equal parts ghost story and academic treatise: a set of lectures by a dead writer placed, like the filling of a dumpling, inside a love story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Anthony Minghella’s 1990 film, “Truly Madly Deeply,” one of the great weepies in cinematic history, a bereaved woman (Juliet Stevenson) is visited by the ghost of her dead, cello-playing boyfriend (Alan Rickman). It doesn’t go well. He brings friends over. She asks: “Are you telling me there are dead people in my living room watching videos?”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 983, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 983}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 626, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1251, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23BOOK/23BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Love and Lectures, Living on From the Grave', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love and Lectures, Living On From the Grave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garner, Dwight', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Ali', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T20:49:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07dacc02-4826-52bd-9577-1103c9cb2a12', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07dacc02-4826-52bd-9577-1103c9cb2a12'}, {'abstract': 'Mandatory reading at the World Economic Forum should be two reminders from JPMorgan Chase and the Federal Reserve that we are far from understanding or preventing financial crises, the Deal Professor writes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/financial-reports-reveal-economic-challenges-but-few-solutions/', 'snippet': 'Mandatory reading at the World Economic Forum should be two reminders from JPMorgan Chase and the Federal Reserve that we are far from understanding or preventing financial crises, the Deal Professor writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The important and self-important of global finance are again gathering at the annual World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. This year, the mandatory reading should be two recent reminders from JPMorgan Chase and the Federal Reserve that we are light years from understanding or preventing financial crises.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reports Reveal Financial Challenges, but Few Solutions', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Required Reading for Davos From the Fed and JPMorgan', 'name'