’ve got a favorite cheese, and a fragrant way with words, we here at the Dining section invite you to submit your wittiest description.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s something about cheese. It inspires people. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are You a Master of Cheese Description?', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bedford Cheese Shop (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T22:53:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc34d8f0-1e14-5564-b9b3-fac00262d2c1', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc34d8f0-1e14-5564-b9b3-fac00262d2c1'}, {'abstract': 'The swag that has been promoted at Sundance comes with news releases packed with brand names.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/where-swag-and-publicity-go-hand-in-hand/', 'snippet': 'The swag that has been promoted at Sundance comes with news releases packed with brand names.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:12 p.m. | Updated  PARK CITY, Utah – One of the biggest bummers about Sundance in the eyes of many longtime festivalgoers is the Great Glom On — brands trying to bask in the media spotlight generated by the premieres of more than 100 feature films. Robert Redford, Sundance’s founder, contemptuously refers to them as “rogue marketers.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Swag and Publicity Go Hand in Hand', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T22:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/794f09ca-0c1a-5439-a8e6-142015ce30ed', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/794f09ca-0c1a-5439-a8e6-142015ce30ed'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Wilson is directing a new chamber opera based on the life of the outsider artist Clementine Hunter, a black woman born in the 19th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/arts/music/robert-wilsons-zinnias-the-life-of-clementine-hunter.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Wilson is directing a new chamber opera based on the life of the outsider artist Clementine Hunter, a black woman born in the 19th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Wilson, the director and visual artist best known for experimental theater pieces like “Einstein on the Beach,” is directing a new chamber opera at Peak Performances at Montclair State University in Montclair, N.J. “Zinnias: The Life of Clementine Hunter,” based on the life of that outsider artist, has its world premiere on Saturday and runs through Feb. 3. Hunter, a black woman who lived from 1887 to 1988, picked cotton on a Louisiana plantation and began painting in her 50s, though she never had formal training. With a libretto and music by Bernice Johnson Reagon and Toshi Reagon, and a story and book by Jacqueline Woodson, “Zinnias” also reunites Mr. Wilson with Sheryl Sutton, the performer who starred in the provocative “silent opera” “Deafman Glance,” which brought them both a lot of attention in the 1970s. Mr. Wilson and Ms. Sutton were interviewed together at Mr. Wilson’s downtown Manhattan studio; Mr. Wilson was also interviewed between rehearsals and by telephone. These are excerpts from the conversations: ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23WILSON/23WILSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Field Hand Who Was an Artist From Midnight to 6', 'kicker': 'A Word With: Robert Wilson and Sheryl Sutton', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Field Hand Who Was an Artist From Midnight to 6', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords':