4.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bradford City in ‘Dreamland’', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fourth-Tier Club in Final', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bradford City (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T23:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa80cab4-c6b3-5717-856c-377c05d7ec39', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa80cab4-c6b3-5717-856c-377c05d7ec39'}, {'abstract': 'The dancer-choreographer Miguel Gutierrez’s 50-minute “Storing the Winter” proved to be the best discovery of the just concluded American Realness festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/arts/dance/miguel-gutierrezs-storing-the-winter-in-american-realness.html', 'snippet': 'The dancer-choreographer Miguel Gutierrez’s 50-minute “Storing the Winter” proved to be the best discovery of the just concluded American Realness festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American Realness festival, which ended on Sunday, was in many ways not just a demonstration but also a celebration of the alternative and unorthodox. Of the 15 performances I attended (almost all at the Abrons Arts Center on the Lower East Side), 4 featured drag, 5 featured nudity or striking degrees of physical exposure, and 8 featured players both talking and dancing. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1464, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/arts/23GUTIERREZ/23GUTIERREZ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrating the Unorthodox With Startling Footwork and Incessant Talk', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Celebrating the Unorthodox With Startling Footwork and Incessant Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gutierrez, Miguel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Realness Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T23:19:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/57361b9e-14fc-5fcf-a33a-98ff21efe6c8', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57361b9e-14fc-5fcf-a33a-98ff21efe6c8'}, {'abstract': '“Broadway Romance,” a program of theater songs performed by Howard McGillin, Rebecca Luker and Ted Sperling, brings nostalgia to the Café Carlyle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/arts/music/broadway-romance-at-cafe-carlyle.html', 'snippet': '“Broadway Romance,” a program of theater songs performed by Howard McGillin, Rebecca Luker and Ted Sperling, brings nostalgia to the Café Carlyle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The very title of the winter-season opening show at the Café Carlyle, “Broadway Romance,” promises cuddly nostalgia with minimal anxiety and hysteria. Three reliable Broadway hands — the singers Howard McGillin and Rebecca Luker, with the musical director and occasional vocalist Ted Sperling on piano — deliver on that promise. The sequence of mostly well-known theater songs offers a sophisticated variation on an old story: boy meets girl, boy loses girl, boy gets girl back, with marriage and children added.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boy Meets Girl. Song Ensues.', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boy Meets Girl. Song Ensues.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sperling, Ted', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGillin, Howard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luker, Rebecca', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T23:20:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1eee0867-7555-5aa4-bbda-8680242d35d0', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eee0867-7555-5aa4-bbda-8680242d35d0'}, {'abstract': 'Ivan Fischer led the Budapest Festival Orchestra in works by Rachmaninoff, Shostakovich and Bernstein.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/arts/music/budapest-festival-orchestra-at-avery-fisher-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Ivan Fischer led the Budapest Festival Orchestra in works by Rachmaninoff, Shostakovich and Bernstein.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a time when it seems that every other movie is enhanced with 3-D technology, it is rare to be awed by a stereophonic experience. No glasses were required at Sunday’s concert by the Budapest Festival Orchestra under the direction of Ivan Fischer, who created volleys of sound so vividly three-dimensional that you sometimes had to resist the impulse to duck. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '3-D Experience for the Ear', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '3-D Experience for the Ear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Budapest Festival Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Ivan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T23:20:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51c19120-12a2-5793-ac0e-aa0f4c0f8a00', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51c19120-12a2-5793-ac0e-aa0f4c0f8a00'}, {'abstract': 'Exterior changes to the flagship Fifth Avenue building of the New York Public Library would include alterations to windows and room for a new loading bay.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/22/landmarks-commission-approves-library-changes/', 'snippet': 'Exterior changes to the flagship Fifth Avenue building of the New York Public Library would include alterations to windows and room for a new loading bay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Landmarks Preservation Commission voted 6 to 2 on Tuesday to approve changes to the exterior of the New York Public Library’s Fifth Avenue flagship building.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Landmarks Commission Approves Library Changes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Commission Approves Library Changes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Norman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bryant Park (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-22T23:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77465cb4-b27e-50d2-b705-65962235b238', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77465cb4-b27e-50d2-b705-65962235b238'}, {'abstract': 'While some inside the network were pushing to publish an article about the girlfriend hoax and others were pushing back, another outlet broke the story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/sports/ncaafootball/as-debate-raged-at-espn-manti-teo-story-slipped-from-its-hands.html', 'snippet': 'While some inside the network were pushing to publish an article about the girlfriend hoax and others were pushing back, another outlet broke the story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 16, a fierce debate raged inside ESPN. Reporters for the network had been working for almost a week trying to nail down an extraordinary story: Manti Te’o’s girlfriend — the one whose death from leukemia had haunted and inspired him during a triumphant year on the field for Notre Dame — might be a hoax.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/sports/YESPN/YESPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/sports/YESPN/YESPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/sports/YESPN/YESPN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/0