web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO2) SING YOUR SONG (2012) In 1956 Harry Belafonte, above, became the first recording artist to sell more than a million LPs with his album “Calypso” and in the process broke color barriers as he rose to success in an era of segregation. He won Grammy, Tony and Emmy Awards but was also blacklisted during the McCarthy era, spied on by the C.I.A. and the F.B.I., and threatened by the Ku Klux Klan, state troopers and Las Vegas Mafia bosses. This documentary from Susanne Rostock distills more than 700 hours of interviews, excerpts from F.B.I. files and archival film to chronicle the life and times of Mr. Belafonte, now 85, from his birth in Harlem and his childhood in Jamaica to his work with the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., his activism against apartheid in South Africa and his actions to counter gang violence and the incarceration of youth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-23T06:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9314016f-1553-5ae1-a871-8f8ec13fe89d', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9314016f-1553-5ae1-a871-8f8ec13fe89d'}, {'abstract': 'With arguments starting Thursday, it remains an open question whether a fast track for a high-profile can lay the groundwork for sweeping changes.'