nk': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Foreign Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-23T14:01:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdfbcaf0-806a-5f4a-b78f-8b21fde05965', 'word_count': 4479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdfbcaf0-806a-5f4a-b78f-8b21fde05965'}, {'abstract': 'The tech industry has come up with a lot of wacky ideas for recruiting engineers, but WibiData, a San Francisco start-up that develops big data applications, has come up with a unique gimmick for telegraphing its engineering street cred to job applicants: a custom version of Portal 2, a video game from Valve.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/23/start-up-uses-portal-game-as-recruiting-tool/', 'snippet': 'The tech industry has come up with a lot of wacky ideas for recruiting engineers, but WibiData, a San Francisco start-up that develops big data applications, has come up with a unique gimmick for telegraphing its engineering street cred to job app...', 'lead_paragraph': ' If you want to signal to software engineers that your tech start-up is a cool place to work, you can let them bring their dogs to the office, offer free energy drinks or put up a billboard with a Web address that can only be accessed after solving a math equation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/technology/23bits-wibi/23bits-wibi-jumbo.png', 'height': 271, 'width': 472, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'