re other prominent women designers, but their numbers are small, and Ms. Crasset, 47, whose signature mushroom-cap hair style has led some to call her the Joan of Arc of design, has created a special niche marked by a rigorous aesthetic. She began her career in 1991 in the office of the Italian designer Denis Santachiara. Two years later, she went to work for Philippe Starck on high-tech projects for Thomson, the consumer electronics company. She set up her business in 1998.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1321, 'width': 1959, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24QNA_SPAN/24QNA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mix Master', 'kicker': 'Currents | Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Concrete Thoughts On Design', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crasset, Matali', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concrete', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-23T23:51:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dabefc1-659a-5f7e-90a7-56c25e4b786a', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dabefc1-659a-5f7e-90a7-56c25e4b786a'}, {'abstract': 'Colorful blankets by Zuzunaga.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/garden/colorful-blankets-by-zuzunaga.html', 'snippet': 'Colorful blankets by Zuzunaga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A colorful spot in snow-covered Paris was a display of blankets from Zuzunaga, a company based in Barcelona and London, which took the top prize for textiles at the International Contemporary Furniture Fair last year. Founded in 2010 by Cristian Zuzunaga, a 34-year-old designer, the company specializes in bright, woven grids and organic abstractions. Mr. Zuzunaga has noted that his first love was biology, and that his early experience with microscopes led to an interest in close-ups. He named this collection Bitmap because of its pixilated motif, but he was also thinking about 21st-century modern architecture found in Tokyo, New York, Shanghai and London, he said.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 1688, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24TEXTILES/24CURRENTS4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Warming Trend', 'kicker': 'Currents | Textiles', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Warming Trend', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zuzunaga', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-23T23:51:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/438a3af9-b61c-5944-b1c3-89f58dff5161', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/438a3af9-b61c-5944-b1c3-89f58dff5161'}, {'abstract': 'Arik Levy introduces new fixtures for Forestier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/garden/lamps-by-arik-levy-for-forestier.html', 'snippet': 'Arik Levy introduces new fixtures for Forestier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Maison et Objet, a trade fair in Paris that ended Tuesday, Forestier, a French lighting manufacturer, showed its new collaborations with Arik Levy, the Paris-based designer, who was born in Israel. The results were predictably polished. Mr. Levy, who divides his time between high art (he’s had several gallery shows of his sculptures) and industrial production for clients including Lanvin, Boucheron, Renault and Vitra, has enjoyed a long relationship with Forestier. This year he designed a hanging lantern of Tyvek and steel for outdoors that will be available at the MoMA store this spring. His Antenna series for Forestier (pendant version, shown) employs delicate metal wire, which was hand-woven to create subtle lighting screens. Mr. Levy also designed a table version of Antenna, in three sizes. All the lights take compact fluorescent light bulbs. Antenna is not yet available in the United States. Information: 011-33-1-40-36-13-10 or forestier.fr.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 425, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 849, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/garden/24LIGHTING/24LIGHTING-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lamps That Leave an Afterglow', 'kicker': 'Currents | Lighting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lamps That Leave an Afterglow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maison Et Objet (Trade Show)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light Bulbs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lighting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levy, Arik', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Forestier', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lamps and Lampshades', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-23T23:52:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2153d69-1f76-573f-aac0-7e2f96f8f910', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2153d69-1f76-573f-aac0-7e2f96f8f910'}, {'abstract': 'Ron Gilad’s walnut chests for Japan Creative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/garden/chests-by-ron-gilad-for-japan-creative.html', 'snippet': 'Ron Gilad’s walnut chests for Japan Creative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Japan Creative, a nonprofit organization, supports traditional Japanese crafts, and the businesses that produce them, by matching leading Japanese artisans with Western design lions. Last year, Jasper Morrison created cast-iron cookware with the Oigen foundry, and Inga Sempé collaborated with Koubei-gama (founded 1804) on ceramics with the traditional green glaze of Oribe ware. At Maison et Objet, Japan Creative introduced an alliance between\xa0Fujisato Woodcraft and the Israeli designer Ron Gilad. The result was monastically simple walnut chests with interiors of paulownia, a wood typically used for storing kimonos. Mr. Gilad devised the elegant wrought-iron hardware. The pieces are still prototypes and the company has not as yet set prices.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24FURNITURE/24CURRENTS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 333, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/24/garden/24FURNITURE/24CURRENTS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargew