, 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a955e121-4514-5ddf-a50c-aea059149b04', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a955e121-4514-5ddf-a50c-aea059149b04'}, {'abstract': 'The DSM 5, to be published in May, has generated an unusual amount of heat. Two changes, in particular, could have considerable impact on older people and their families.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/grief-over-new-depression-diagnosis/', 'snippet': 'The DSM 5, to be published in May, has generated an unusual amount of heat. Two changes, in particular, could have considerable impact on older people and their families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the American Psychiatric Association unveils a proposed new version of its Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, the bible of psychiatric diagnoses, it expects controversy. Illnesses get added or deleted, acquire new definitions or lists of symptoms. Everyone from advocacy groups to insurance companies to litigators — all have an interest in what’s defined as mental illness — pays close attention. Invariably, complaints ensue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grief Over New Depression Diagnosis', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis, Allen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Journal of Psychiatry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN PSYCHIATRIC ASSN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T11:40:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ada3e404-abef-55ae-a5bf-227bd0ba6ff1', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ada3e404-abef-55ae-a5bf-227bd0ba6ff1'}, {'abstract': 'Facing a sluggish economy and tougher capital requirements, Commerzbank says it will cut up to 10 percent of its work force in a bid to increase earnings, joining other European banks that have announced restructuring plans in recent months.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/commerzbank-to-cut-up-to-6000-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Facing a sluggish economy and tougher capital requirements, Commerzbank says it will cut up to 10 percent of its work force in a bid to increase earnings, joining other European banks that have announced restructuring plans in recent months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Commerzbank, the second-largest lender in Germany, is planning to cut up to 6,000 jobs in a bid to increase earnings, joining other European banks that have announced restructuring plans in recent months.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commerzbank to Cut Up to 6,000 Jobs', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commerzbank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N