enying-american-scots-their-holiday-haggis/', 'snippet': 'Scottish-Americans will be celebrating their heritage this week at annual Burns Night suppers. But, as in previous years, one vital ingredient will be missing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Scots at home and abroad will be sitting down on Friday night for Burns Night suppers to commemorate their national poet with a feast of haggis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1370, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/blogs/24rdv-haggis/24rdv-haggis-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Denying American Scots Their Holiday Haggis', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T14:13:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6a32b4c-6568-5264-aee6-40c73125a6d8', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6a32b4c-6568-5264-aee6-40c73125a6d8'}, {'abstract': 'The GOP’s arithmetic problem.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/an-insurance-company-with-an-army-3/', 'snippet': 'The GOP’s arithmetic problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jonathan Chait and Greg Sargent both weigh in on the absurd Republican claim that they’ll produce a plan to balance the budget in 10 years, without a penny in additional revenue. Chait points out that the Ryan plan, even if you accepted all its magic asterisks, still didn’t produce a balanced budget until 2040. Sargent, armed with numbers from the Center on Budget and Policy Priorities, points out that if the GOP were to honor its promises not to cut military spending or benefits for those over 55, you’d have to impose savage cuts on everything else.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Insurance Company With an Army', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T14:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dd4f4aa-845e-5732-bfec-7b04edf047ca', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dd4f4aa-845e-5732-bfec-7b04edf047ca'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Gonzales’s first collection of stories is shot through with outlandish events.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/supernatural-tales-manuel-gonzales-talks-about-the-miniature-wife/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Gonzales’s first collection of stories is shot through with outlandish events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stories in Manuel Gonzales’s first collection, “The Miniature Wife” (Riverhead), are shot through with outlandish events relayed in an understated tone. In the first story, passengers in an airplane circle above the same city for more than 20 years. In the title story, a husband accidentally shrinks his wife to the size of a coffee mug. In a recent e-mail interview, Mr. Gonzales discussed his use of the supernatural, the writing center he runs in Austin, Tex., and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/books/24miniaturewife/24miniaturewife-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/24/books/24miniaturewife/24miniaturewife-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/24/books/24miniaturewife/24miniaturewife-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cro