', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ea01e081-282e-5870-94ba-c48bf614a0cb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ea01e081-282e-5870-94ba-c48bf614a0cb'}, {'abstract': 'United Airlines passengers can now have checked bags delivered directly to their final destinations within 100 miles of their arrival airports.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/luggage-to-your-doorstep/', 'snippet': 'United Airlines passengers can now have checked bags delivered directly to their final destinations within 100 miles of their arrival airports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'United Airlines passengers can now skip the wait at the baggage carousel and have checked bags delivered directly to their homes, hotels, offices or other final destinations within 100 miles of their arrival airports. The optional service, operated through BagsVIP, is available on flights from any domestic airport that arrives in Boston, Chicago, Honolulu, Houston, Los Angeles and Orlando, and is expected to expand to more than 190 domestic airports in coming months. Delivery rates begin at $29.95, and are in addition to standard checked bag charges.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Luggage to Your Doorstep', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Airlines: Luggage to Your Doorstep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T16:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c0dd0a2-3830-565a-9db5-775a49f60115', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c0dd0a2-3830-565a-9db5-775a49f60115'}, {'abstract': 'The theater veteran talks about her role as a biophysicist battling dementia in Joe Mantello’s Broadway play, “The Other Place.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/talking-talent-laurie-metcalf/', 'snippet': 'The theater veteran talks about her role as a biophysicist battling dementia in Joe Mantello’s Broadway play, “The Other Place.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The theater veteran Laurie Metcalf, best known as Jackie, Roseanne Barr’s high-strung sidekick sister on the popular 1990s sitcom “Roseanne,” is back on Broadway. Metcalf, an Emmy Award-winning actress, is currently reprising her leading role as Juliana Smithton, a biophysicist battling dementia in Sharr White’s “The Other Place,” running through March 3 at Manhattan Theater Club‘s Samuel J. Friedman’s Theater. In the new drama directed by Joe Mantello — and in approximately 80 minutes — Metcalf takes the audience on a gut-wrenching ride as her nefarious disease leaves a path of heartbreaking destruction. Her performance is haunting and left T with many questions, which Metcalf generously answered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking Talent | Laurie Metcalf', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Metcalf, Laurie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Other Place (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T16:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Tabach-Bank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabach-Bank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d662e1be-5fd7-5209-a711-6d15dbed13a8', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d662e1be-5fd7-5209-a711-6d15dbed13a8'}, {'abstract': 'Weighing the pro-life movement’s role in the ongoing rise of out-of-wedlock births.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/the-bristol-palin-effect-vs-the-roe-effect/', 'snippet': 'Weighing the pro-life movement’s role in the ongoing rise of out-of-wedlock births.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Naomi Cahn and June Carbone, whose book “Red Families, Blue Families” I’ve written about\xa0favorably in the past, have a new piece for Slate making the case that the continuing rise in out-of-wedlock birth rates is an unintended consequence of the pro-life movement’s modest success at stigmatizing abortion. The piece starts by acknowledging a connection that social conservatives often have reason to point out: The biggest reason why blue states often have lower out-of-wedlock birth rates than more conservative states is that the blue states have higher abortion rates, and the relative stability of the “blue model” of post-sexual revolution family life — which is really the upper middle class blue model, in which serial monogamy in your late teens and twenties is followed by late marriage and parenting — depends on a “willingness to abort” as well as a facility with contraception. But where pro-life writers like myself tend to raise this point in order to cast doubt on the comforting-but-dubious idea that liberal policies will inevitably lower the abortion rate, Cahn and Carbone invoke it to cast doubt on the idea that one can simultaneously be opposed to abortion and in favor of stable two-parent families. After all, if a “willingness to abort” helps keep out-of-wedlock birth rates low among well-educated blue staters, then surely opposition to abortion can plausibly be held responsible for the steady rise in single parent households in the rest of the country — and especially in white, conservative-tilting middle America:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bristol Palin Effect vs. The Roe Effect', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Bristol', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brookings Institution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T16:04:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0ef48b4-3978-527e-ab91-3d2d085f5b32', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0ef48b4-3978-527e-ab91-3d2d085f5b32'}, {'abstract': 'It’s been seven years since a Japanese wrestler has won a grand championship.  Sumo is dominated by foreign-born athletes today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000002021892/a-slump-for-japanese-sumo-wrestlers.html', 'snippet': 'It’s been seven years since a Japanese wrestler has won a grand championship.  Sumo is dominated by foreign-born athletes today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been seven years since a Japanese wrestler has won a grand championship.  Sumo is dominated by foreign-born athletes today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/multimedia/video-sumo/video-sumo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/23/multimedia/video-sumo/video-sumo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/23/multimedia/video-sumo/video-sumo-