ws_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9579001-b88f-52f5-b875-c2fb8b30a835', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9579001-b88f-52f5-b875-c2fb8b30a835'}, {'abstract': 'Having women in infantry roles will foster competition, teamwork and innovation inside the military, and help lead to more real equality in our culture. It’s the right thing to do for the country, it’s the right thing to do for the military. Let’s not mess it up.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/how-to-make-womens-service-in-combat-roles-a-success/', 'snippet': 'Having women in infantry roles will foster competition, teamwork and innovation inside the military, and help lead to more real equality in our culture. It’s the right thing to do for the country, it’s the right thing to do for the military. Let’s...', 'lead_paragraph': ' For much of the seven years I served in the United States Army infantry, I believed that women would make poor infantry soldiers. I believed that efforts to put women in uniform on my left, carrying a machine gun, or on my right, firing a grenade launcher, were wildly misguided at best, and downright dangerous at worst. I believed this throughout Ranger School and Reconnaissance School. I believed this while reading articles about how women had experienced combat as part of counterinsurgency efforts, and therefore deserved to join infantry or Marine infantry units. I believed this while walking along ridgelines in Afghanistan at 9,000 feet with 100 pounds of armor and gear on my shoulders, cursing the day I was born. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Make Women’s Service in Combat Roles a Success', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T18:33:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrian Bonenberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bonenberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d30c7ba7-f802-5f45-8d6e-573609de4b9c', 'word_count': 1470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d30c7ba7-f802-5f45-8d6e-573609de4b9c'}, {'abstract': '634 submissions later, we announce the four group and six individual winners of our rap lyrics contest. Congratulations, and thank you participants and teachers.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/student-contest-winners-the-2012-year-in-rap/', 'snippet': '634 submissions later, we announce the four group and six individual winners of our rap lyrics contest. Congratulations, and thank you participants and teachers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Newtown and Aurora, Hurricane Sandy and the election: those were the topics we expected when we invited students to write raps about the news of 2012.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN-LN/20TWIN_SPAN-LN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 318, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 