ine': {'main': 'Ascendant and Ambitious, a Pianist Honors the Masters', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ascendant and Ambitious, A Pianist Honors the Masters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schweitzer, Vivien', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyson, Andrew (1986- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Merkin Concert Hall', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Young Concert Artists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T22:22:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96d39695-0ab6-584c-ad3f-b2fda6d67edc', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96d39695-0ab6-584c-ad3f-b2fda6d67edc'}, {'abstract': 'A new Met exhibition includes 50 French paintings from the years between neo-Classicism and Romanticism, with a particular love for gleaming oil sketches that were done outdoors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/arts/design/the-path-of-nature-at-the-metropolitan-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'A new Met exhibition includes 50 French paintings from the years between neo-Classicism and Romanticism, with a particular love for gleaming oil sketches that were done outdoors.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Sparkle with repose” was the effect the British artist John Constable (1776-1837) said he strove for in his landscape paintings. His French contemporaries, following his lead, had the same idea. If success is measured by fame, Constable achieved his goal, and they didn’t. He’s a star. But León Pallière? Adrien Dauzats? Alexandre-Hyacinthe Dunouy? Only wonkish scholars are likely to know their names now.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1538, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25SUBNATURE_SPAN/25SUBNATURE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great Outdoors', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Great Outdoors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T22:22:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fb23fc8-b1bd-508c-9077-cae89b7f4a36', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fb23fc8-b1bd-508c-9077-cae89b7f4a36'}, {'abstract': 'It has become much more common for Americans over 65 to continue working, if they can.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/24/older-but-not-yet-retired/', 'snippet': 'It has become much more common for Americans over 65 to continue working, if they can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The labor force participation rate has fallen drastically in the last few years, partly because a huge chunk of the American population — the baby boomers — is rolling into retirement age. But the aging of Americans born from 1946 to 1964 has not actually had as big a drag on labor-force participation rates as demographers might have guessed a few decades ago. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Older, but Not Yet Retired', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T22:25:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c49df07-d5cc-5a70-8d74-465701c25944', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c49df07-d5cc-5a70-8d74-465701c25944'}, {'abstract': 'Two fairs are running concurrently at the Metropolitan Pavilion: the Metro Show, formerly known as the American Antiques Show, and the Editions/Artists’ Book Fair, devoted primarily to producers of contemporary prints and books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/arts/design/metro-show-and-editions-artists-book-fair.html', 'snippet': 'Two fairs are running concurrently at the Metropolitan Pavilion: the Metro Show, formerly known as the American Antiques Show, and the Editions/Artists’ Book Fair, devoted primarily to producers of contemporary prints and books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t be fooled by the name. The Metro Show, the art and antiques fair going on this week at the Metropolitan Pavilion in Chelsea, is not a display of sophisticated urbanity. A two-year-old reincarnation of the event formerly known as the American Antiques Show, it does feature 34 dealers trafficking in an extraordinarily wide range of materials, from ancient Andean textiles at William Siegal’s booth to a wooden model of a French World War II fighter plane created for wind tunnel testing, which is at Bernard Goldberg.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/0125METRO01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/0125METRO01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/0125METRO01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/0125METRO01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/25METRO1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/25METRO1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/arts/25METRO1_SPAN/25METRO1_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Homespun to Hallucinogenic in Two Fairs', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Homespun to Hallucinogenic in Two Fairs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conventions, Fairs and Trade Shows', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Fairs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-24T22:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ke