', 'snippet': 'Baltimore’s enduring love affair with its previous franchise, the Colts, and its players has remained with the Super Bowl-bound Ravens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — If Harry Swayne could choose, he would appoint Lenny Moore as the Baltimore Ravens’ honorary captain for the pregame coin toss at Super Bowl XLVII.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/25/sports/BALTIMORE1/BALTIMORE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ravens Maintain a History Longer Than Their Existence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ravens Maintain a History Longer Than Their Existence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Colts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T02:18:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillel Kuttler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kuttler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/035b02b1-e7f2-528e-b021-3b25923058a9', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/035b02b1-e7f2-528e-b021-3b25923058a9'}, {'abstract': 'As the winners of our meritocracy hold the reins of progressive power, they may struggle to mitigate the inequality their own ascendance has helped produce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/opinion/brooks-the-great-migration.html', 'snippet': 'As the winners of our meritocracy hold the reins of progressive power, they may struggle to mitigate the inequality their own ascendance has helped produce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the features of the Obama years is that we get to witness an enormous race, which you might call the race between meritocracy and government. On the one side, there is the meritocracy, which widens inequality. On the other side, there is President Obama’s team of progressives, who are trying to mitigate inequality. The big question is: Which side is winning?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great Migration', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Great Migration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T02:20:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/53885b0d-e293-54db-a86f-674c326a7b53', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53885b0d-e293-54db-a86f-674c326a7b53'}, {'abstract': 'The movie theater in Aurora, Colo., where a gunman went on a rampage, reopened last week, but life for surviving families is far from being back to normal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/opinion/auroras-suffering-beyond-the-spotlight.html', 'snippet': 'The movie theater in Aurora, Colo., where a gunman went on a rampage, reopened last week, but life for surviving families is far from being back to normal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cinema complex in Aurora, Colo., reopened last week, six months after a gunman killed 12 patrons, wounded 58 and left deep scars on a community still struggling to recover. “Everyone heals, some slower, some in different ways,” contended Gov. John Hickenlooper of Colorado in the face of angry complaints from some of the victims’ families that the reopening, with prayers, a “night of remembrance” and a screening of “The Hobbit: An Unexpected Journey,” was insensitive and crass.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aurora’s Suffering Beyond the Spotlight', 'kicker': 'Editorial Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aurora’s Suffering Beyond the Spotlight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aurora, Colo, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Aurora (Colo)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T02:21:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2256dddf-0be2-5f33-84b8-7a2976ad503a', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2256dddf-0be2-5f33-84b8-7a2976ad503a'}, {'abstract': 'No sooner had a Democratic state senator left for the inauguration than Republicans, in a power grab, passed a partisan redistricting map.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/opinion/hubris-in-virginia.html', 'snippet': 'No sooner had a Democratic state senator left for the inauguration than Republicans, in a power grab, passed a partisan redistricting map.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dark art of statehouse gerrymandering reached a new low in Virginia this week. Senate Republicans in Richmond used the absence of a Democratic member, who traveled to the presidential inauguration, to ram through a surprise redistricting map patently designed to aid the G.O.P.’s grab for majority power in the next election. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hubris in the Commonwealth', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hubris in the Commonwealth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonnell, Robert F', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsh, Henry L III', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T02:23:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd09a5f8-fb0e-5078-9833-c0e9e8a93839', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd09a5f8-fb0e-5078-9833-c0e9e8a93839'}, {'abstract': 'By Albany standards, Governor Cuomo’s budget is mostly low trauma, but it does need a few fixes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/opinion/new-yorks-low-buzz-budget.html', 'snippet': 'By Albany standards, Governor Cuomo’s budget is mostly low trauma, but it does need a few fixes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo of New York presented an extra-lean state budget this week, his third conservative spending plan since taking office in 2011. The total outlays for fiscal year 2013-14, including federal funds to rebuild after Hurricane Sandy, will be almost $143 billion. The budget contains some real bonuses, including a plan to raise the minimum wage and a proposal for a workable database for gun ownership. But it could also raise the debt load, especially in struggling upstate areas.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Cuomo’s Low-Buzz Budget', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Cuomo’s Low-Buzz Budget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date':