'pub_date': '2013-01-25T15:34:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d28553f-573b-57e5-9f10-e5b28e7d964d', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d28553f-573b-57e5-9f10-e5b28e7d964d'}, {'abstract': 'Summarizing the latest bait and switch.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/consumer-spending-and-inequality-denial/', 'snippet': 'Summarizing the latest bait and switch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been debating rising inequality with the usual suspects for a couple of decades (despite what it says on this piece, it was actually published in 1992). And what you always encounter from the right is a sort of defense in depth: claims, based on tortured statistics, that it isn’t happening; claims, based on similarly tortured statistics, that it doesn’t matter; and claims that anyway it’s a good thing. You might think that people would have to choose one of these lines: you can’t simultaneously say that inequality isn’t rising and that the rise is a good thing, can you? Well, yes you can; we’re engaged in a political defense of the 1% here, not research.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Consumer Spending and Inequality Denial', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T15:36:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3efb4257-9d3f-5b2c-8287-1f07cc195e3a', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3efb4257-9d3f-5b2c-8287-1f07cc195e3a'}, {'abstract': 'A day after his policies came under attack from several mayoral hopefuls, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg hit back on Friday, saying the critics “had no idea what they’re talking about.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/after-mayoral-forum-bloomberg-says-candidates-sound-ridiculous/', 'snippet': 'A day after his policies came under attack from several mayoral hopefuls, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg hit back on Friday, saying the critics “had no idea what they’re talking about.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It could be a long year for Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mayor Says Some 2013 Candidates ‘Just Sound Ridiculous’', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mayor Says ’13 Hopefuls ‘Just Sound Ridiculous’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lhota, Joseph J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu, John C', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, William C Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East New York (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T15:38:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a6b6351-e3d6-5644-996e-f2e082e96916', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a6b6351-e3d6-5644-996e-f2e082e96916'}, {'abstract': 'Driverless cars: now we’re talking.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/look-ma-no-human-hands/', 'snippet': 'Driverless cars: now we’re talking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Felix Salmon has been converted to the cult of the self-driving car; indeed, this is starting to look like a real thing. And I’m impressed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look Ma, No (Human) Hands', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T15:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ebe8afb-8364-5155-a1e1-9564040be6b8', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ebe8afb-8364-5155-a1e1-9564040be6b8'}, {'abstract': 'Toyota’s green car champion, the impact of being in day care on behavior later, Twitter’s new video feature and other consumer news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/friday-reading-the-greenest-cars-of-2013/', 'snippet': 'Toyota’s green car champion, the impact of being in day care on behavior later, Twitter’s new video feature and other consumer news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: The Greenest Cars of 2013', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T15:57:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae9d9303-1269-5d74-a213-50149b5c0146', 'word_count': 85, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae9d9303-1269-5d74-a213-50149b5c0146'}, {'abstract': 'The Paris-based hat label Larose collaborates with young French designers to create a unique variety of men’s caps.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/feeling-for-laroses-hats/', 'snippet': 'The Paris-based hat label Larose collaborates with young French designers to create a unique variety of men’s caps.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We really believe in brands that specialize in one item and do it very, very well,” says Marc Beaugé, one of the founders of the Paris-based hat label Larose. Beaugé, a style writer, and Isaac Larose Farmer, an event planner/entrepreneur, created the label as a side project. It hinges on a single item: a cap with a five-panel formula for men (though women can certainly wear them, too). Pauline Brosset, a chapelière (hat maker) who has also worked with Kenzo, completes the team and executes the designs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling For | Larose’s Hats', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hats and Caps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc28186a-c442-54db-94a0-5af99865e986', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc28186a-c442-54db-94a0-5af99865e986'}, {'abstract': 'In 1986, Arsenal of England’s Premier League made a trip to the New York area to play a game of indoor soccer.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/25/when-arsenal-played-long-island/', 'snippet': 'In 1986, Arsenal of England’s Premier League made a trip to the New York area to play a game of indoor soccer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The announcement of the New York Cosmos’ bid for a soccer stadium as part of the Elmont Town Crossings project at Belmont Park, along with their return to play at Hofstra’s Shuart Stadium in the North American Soccer League fall season, brings Long Island’s Nassau County — long a hotbed for youth soccer — back into play as a potential host for high-profile international friendlies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Arsenal Played Long Island', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Arsenal (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nassau Coliseum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kilpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilpatrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/838a1c48-463e-5bf1-8a7c-7ea0a9e204ca', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/838a1c48-463e-5bf1-8a7c-7ea0a9e204ca'}, {'abstract': 'The latest controversies have become television entertainment events, complete with consultants and makeup artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/sports/busiest-arena-the-talk-show-couch-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'The latest controversies have become television entertainment events, complete with consultants and makeup artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that Manti Te’o has made his appearance as daytime television fodder — complete with studio audience, Katie Couric questioning him as if he were a wayward third grader and parents crying on cue — can we please squelch this little trend of turning sports controversies into television entertainment events? Somehow, sports has wandered into this weird realm of image consultants and makeup artists, where Oprah Winfrey is snubbed and we are leaked tidbits of what somebody said in an interview taped two days ago so it can be milked for optimal promotional value.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/sports/ncaafootball/26leadingpic/26leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/26/sports/ncaafootball/26leadingpic/26leadingpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/sports/ncaafootball/26leadingpic/26leadingpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/sports/ncaafootball/26leadingpic/26leadingpic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '