oice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:22:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/19b1ceea-d344-554a-895a-bc95f92c9ce3', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19b1ceea-d344-554a-895a-bc95f92c9ce3'}, {'abstract': 'Joe Queenan considers his relationship with the printed word.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/books/review/one-for-the-books-by-joe-queenan.html', 'snippet': 'Joe Queenan considers his relationship with the printed word.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By Joe Queenan’s reckoning, in his 62 years of life he has read at least 6,128 books. Should he continue to read at his current clip (100 to 200 books annually), he calculates that given natural life expectancy, he has only some 2,138 books to go. The clock is ticking, he warns, for him and for us all. If that makes you want to abandon this review immediately and grab the nearest Dostoyevsky, no hard feelings.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 626}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 742, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 742, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MISHAN/27MISHAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading for His Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reading for His Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishan, Ligaya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Queenan, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2784f1de-85b8-53ca-b517-0753dd64e559', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2784f1de-85b8-53ca-b517-0753dd64e559'}, {'abstract': 'Edward Ball explains how Eadweard Muybridge’s work for a railroad magnate led to the movies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/books/review/the-inventor-and-the-tycoon-by-edward-ball.html', 'snippet': 'Edward Ball explains how Eadweard Muybridge’s work for a railroad magnate led to the movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Genius, it seems, is almost always accompanied by eccentricity, if not madness. Those rare instances of genuine brilliance that we find scattered throughout history — in the music of Ludwig van Beethoven, the paintings of Vincent van Gogh, the mathematical equations of John Nash — often appear to have come at great cost to the minds that produced them. The work of Eadweard Muybridge is no exception. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 626}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1964, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27MILLARD/27MILLARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/book