eek, Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss the album, what it means to be post-regional in a genre so obsessed with place, and what Rocky’s success might mean for hip-hop in general.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of ASAP Rocky', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:25:47+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/a952b078-3ec1-5af0-ac22-65571149e4b9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/a952b078-3ec1-5af0-ac22-65571149e4b9'}, {'abstract': 'Candice Millard is best known for her two books, “The River of Doubt” and “Destiny of the Republic.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Candice Millard is best known for her two books, “The River of Doubt” and “Destiny of the Republic.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Candice Millard has written for a number of publications, including National Geographic, Time and The New York Times Book Review, but she is probably best known for her two books, “The River of Doubt” and “Destiny of the Republic,” the first about Theodore Roose\xadvelt’s expedition to South America, the second about the assassination of James A. Garfield.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Upfront/0127-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 975, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Upfront/0127-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 975}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i