dissolve families, dissect communities, and split nations. With a topic as incendiary as immigration at the story’s core, Askew could have trod heavily over her subject matter and garnered passionate responses from both sides of the political divide. Instead she personalizes the issue, exploring with a deft hand and an unflinching moral vision the gray areas of an argument so often presented in black and white.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 703}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1281, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Evison/0127-Evison-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Neighbor Versus Neighbor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Neighbor Versus Neighbor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evison, Jonathan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Askew, Rilla', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:41:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Evison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Evison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8a97fdc-3a60-5c3c-b62b-d4445bf28651', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8a97fdc-3a60-5c3c-b62b-d4445bf28651'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a graffiti crew reunites after 18 years to avenge one of its members.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/books/review/rage-is-back-by-adam-mansbach.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a graffiti crew reunites after 18 years to avenge one of its members.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I first came to live in New York, in 1976, I was enthralled by the graffiti in the subways. It seemed a language from some secret world beneath the city, letters that were barely letters, shaped by hands you never saw: a call to resistance, maybe, notes from the underground.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 702}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/0127-Baker/0127-Baker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adventures Underground', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Adventures Underground', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-25T16:42:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4b1ab6f-6437-5662-b0e4-1e106c524b7f', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4b1ab6f-6437-5662-b0e4-1e106c524b7f'}, {'abstract': 'Melanie Challenger travels in search of the species, cultures and industries touched by extinction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/books/review/on-extinction-by-melanie-challenger.html', 'snippet': 'Melanie Challenger travels in search of the species, cultures and industries touched by extinction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This past summer I found myself traveling with a strange man straight out of Melanie Challenger’s book “On Extinction.” He was an Alaska homesteader, firefighter and fishing guide, and for a week he led me down a remote rivulet in Alaska’s interior. The ostensible purpose of our trip was to fish the biggest remaining sockeye salmon run in the world and at the same time to make a case for opposing what would be the continent’s largest open-pit mine to be built atop said salmon run. But the more I tried to establish a narrative about this man, the more he broke up my effort with a kind of wilderness attention deficit disorder. No sooner had he hunched over to differentiate the identities of the shades of sage and white in the blooming tundra than he would hear the call of a ptarmigan. This in turn would lead to a discourse on the connection between migrations and snow melt and the desperation of grizzly bears in particularly snowy years. He was so profoundly knowledgeable, and everything he said felt incredibly nuanced and relevant. But at the same time his accounts were often tedious and circular and without any “point” that an impatient New Yorker could discern. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/books/review/27GREENBERG/27GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_