': '2013-01-26T00:18:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Winerip', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winerip', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7385e3d1-bea5-5439-931f-87e9d4d8414d', 'word_count': 1515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7385e3d1-bea5-5439-931f-87e9d4d8414d'}, {'abstract': 'The Twitter account of the Shabab militant group in Somalia, which used to trumpet the group’s battlefield prowess and once had thousands of followers, has been shut down.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/world/africa/somalia-twitter-shuts-rebels-account.html', 'snippet': 'The Twitter account of the Shabab militant group in Somalia, which used to trumpet the group’s battlefield prowess and once had thousands of followers, has been shut down.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Twitter account of the Shabab militant group in Somalia, which used to trumpet the group’s battlefield prowess and once had thousands of followers, has been shut down. “Account suspended,” read a message on Twitter’s Web site on Friday. American officials had been looking closely at the Shabab’s use of Twitter, saying that it could be a potential recruiting tool. The Shabab recently sent out a Twitter message vowing to kill Kenyan hostages if the Kenyan government did not meet its demands. Shabab fighters, who used to control large areas of Somalia, have been routed in recent months by African Union peacekeepers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somalia: Twitter Shuts Rebels’ Account', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somalia: Twitter Shuts Rebels’ Account', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'G