arts/music/lola-astanova-with-the-orchestra-of-st-lukes.html', 'snippet': 'Lola Astanova was the soloist on Wednesday in a program of concertos by Tchaikovsky and Rachmaninoff, and works by Suppé and Smetana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Performing Tchaikovsky’s Piano Concerto No. 1 and Rachmaninoff’s Piano Concerto No. 2 on the same evening requires athletic stamina. But if a pianist lacks the muscle to complete the race, it becomes an endurance test for both soloist and listeners. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1526, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/SUB26LOLA/SUB26LOLA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grand Overtures, Dances and Romantic Concertos', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grand Overtures, Dances and Romantic Concertos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schweitzer, Vivien', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Astanova, Lola', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Orchestra of St Luke's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:05:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3fed7b4-a078-523b-974f-de02de8644af', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3fed7b4-a078-523b-974f-de02de8644af'}, {'abstract': 'Leaders of the National Gay and Lesbian Task Force assured members that they would keep the pressure on the president to do more on gay rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/us/politics/at-gay-conference-applause-for-obama-is-spirited-but-wary.html', 'snippet': 'Leaders of the National Gay and Lesbian Task Force assured members that they would keep the pressure on the president to do more on gay rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — Here at the nation’s largest gathering of politically minded gays and lesbians, President Obama’s historic inclusion of sexual orientation in his inauguration speech just days earlier would seem to be cause for celebration.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Gay Conference, Applause for Obama Is Spirited but Wary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Gay Conference, Applause for Obama Is Spirited but Wary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Gay and Lesbian Task Force', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexual', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:08:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58a42951-bf6c-5183-951a-b7a021395646', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58a42951-bf6c-5183-951a-b7a021395646'}, {'abstract': 'France has decreed that Twitter-using citizens should refrain from using the word “hashtag” in favor of the newly coined French term “mot-dièse.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/world/europe/france-striking-a-blow-for-the-mother-tongue.html', 'snippet': 'France has decreed that Twitter-using citizens should refrain from using the word “hashtag” in favor of the newly coined French term “mot-dièse.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' In its determined if quixotic mission to keep the French language French — that is, to keep out the English language, which creeps in more each day — France has decreed that Twitter-using citizens should refrain from using the word “hashtag” in favor of the newly coined French term “mot-dièse.” The new term, which effectively means “hashtag-word,” was developed by a government committee on vocabulary and published this week in the Journal Officiel, where laws and government rules are recorded. Linguistic purists have noted that “dièse” is, in fact, a musical term meaning “sharp symbol” and that the word “croisillon,” which means “hash symbol,” might have been a better choice. In any case, for the moment French Twitter users still “tweet” — “tweeter” in French (pronounced “twee-tay”) — when they post messages to the service. Many have been posting snarky notes about the new word under the hashtag “#motdièse.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France: Striking a Blow for the Mother Tongue', 'kicker': 'Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France: Striking a Blow for the Mother Tongue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:08:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe0fc8c7-9d09-5d58-bc0f-a3d203dc2569', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe0fc8c7-9d09-5d58-bc0f-a3d203dc2569'}, {'abstract': 'As of Friday morning, New York City had been murder-free for nine days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/nyregion/cold-wave-cut-murders-in-new-york-city-significantly.html', 'snippet': 'As of Friday morning, New York City had been murder-free for nine days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s an old saw that murders spike during hot summers, when city dwellers flee their apartments for the streets and tempers soar along with the mercury. Less, however, has been said about the effect of extreme cold spells on mankind’s capacity for violent crimes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It Has Been Frigid Outside, but Also a Lot Less Dangerous ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Has Been Frigid Outside, but Also a Lot Less Dangerous', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temperature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime Rates', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:08:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5fafafd-6494-5dfd-ae1a-09b84e7ac4b6', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5fafafd-6494-5dfd-ae1a-09b84e7ac4b6'}, {'abstract': 'The energy giant Statoil on Friday confirmed the deaths of three of the company’s five employees who had been missing since Islamist militants attacked a gas installation in Algeria last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/world/europe/norway-3-missing-employees-confirmed-dead-in-algeria.html', 'snippet': 'The energy giant Statoil on Friday confirmed the deaths of three of the company’s five employees who had been missing since Islamist militants attacked a gas installation in Algeria last week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The energy giant Statoil on Friday confirmed the deaths of three of the company’s five employees who had been missing since Islamist militants attacked a gas installation in Algeria last week. Statoil’s chief executive officer, Helge Lund, said the three workers were Tore Bech, 58; Thomas Snekkevik, 35; and Hans M. Bjone, 55. Few hold out more than the slimmest hopes for the remaining two. The company offered no details about the circumstances of their deaths. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Norway: 3 Missing Gas Workers Confirmed Dead in Algeria', 'kicker': 'Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Norway: 3 Missing Gas Workers Confirmed Dead in Algeria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Statoil ASA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:09:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7de7acd-8f75-5257-9c2c-8d0364d8fede', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7de7acd-8f75-5257-9c2c-8d0364d8fede'}, {'abstract': 'Germany announced on Thursday that it would reinstate the annual pledge of 200 million euros to the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/world/europe/germany-funds-for-global-health-fund-reinstated.html', 'snippet': 'Germany announced on Thursday that it would reinstate the annual pledge of 200 million euros to the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Signaling that it was pleased with changes in the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria, Germany announced Thursday that it would reinstate the annual pledge of 200 million euros (about $267 million) it had made since the fund’s early days. In 2011, Germany temporarily held back half its contribution and threatened to hold back future funds in protest against inefficiencies, thefts by some grantee countries and infighting among the fund’s top executives. Other contributions dried up, forcing the fund to cancel a planned round of grants. Since then, both the fund’s executive director and inspector general have departed, and it was run for one year by a Brazilian banker; he devised an overhaul of the grant-making process that is to take effect next month. In November, Dr. Mark Dybul, the Bush administration’s global AIDS czar, became the new executive director. The fund recently announced that it cut its operating expenses by 5 percent in 2012 while still making 26 percent more in grants than it did in 2011. Of the grants that had been audited, Dr. Dybul said, only 0.5 percent had been lost to fraud. The fund plans a new fund-raising round for later this year. The United States is the fund’s largest supporter, providing roughly a third of its budget. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany: Funds for Global Health Fund Reinstated', 'kicker': 'Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Germany: Funds for Global Health Fund Reinstated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T01:11:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/010e85b7-bbe0-5b1f-8d46-0d7e31019969', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/010e85b7-bbe0-5b1f-8d46-0d7e31019969'}, {'abstract': '“The Seven-Per-Cent Solution” by Nicholas Meyer provided the template for the sensitive, neurotic Sherlock Holmeses of today’s films and television.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/movies/how-the-seven-per-cent-solution-reinvented-sherlock-holmes.html', 'snippet': '“The Seven-Per-Cent Solution” by Nicholas Meyer provided the template for the sensitive, neurotic Sherlock Holmeses of today’s films and television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re lousy with Sherlock Holmeses right now: the Robert Downey Jr. version on the big screen, the competing television interpretations of Benedict Cumberbatch (“Sherlock”) and Jonny Lee Miller (“Elementary”) and all the Holmes-inspired geniuses in current and recent TV shows like “The Mentalist,” “Psych,” “House” and “Monk.” So “The Seven-Per-Cent Solution,” released this week in a new Blu-ray and DVD package, enters a crowded market.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/26/arts/26PERCENT/26PERCENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_