ontent_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Connecticut', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T05:19:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df80204-cd78-54ae-9af5-225e9e2ba3c4', 'word_count': 4764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df80204-cd78-54ae-9af5-225e9e2ba3c4'}, {'abstract': 'The rate of violent and property crimes has fallen more in New York City than in the country as a whole.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/01/26/nyregion/an-exception-to-the-national-crime-trend.html', 'snippet': 'The rate of violent and property crimes has fallen more in New York City than in the country as a whole.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rate of violent and property crimes has fallen more in New York City than in the country as a whole.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Exception to the National Crime Trend', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T05:26:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0c8d3060-ecbb-54c3-a033-de12c5ee825c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0c8d3060-ecbb-54c3-a033-de12c5ee825c'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo pulled financing for a group that gives a voice to New York minors caught in the court system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/27/nyregion/small-savings-on-the-backs-of-foster-children.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo pulled financing for a group that gives a voice to New York minors caught in the court system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1853, Charles Loring Brace, a patrician reformer, devised a plan to remediate the circumstances of New York City’s 30,000 homeless children, many of whom passed their days as desperate salesmen hawking rags and newspapers, and who had acquired the label, absent a world of ethnic sensitivities, of “street Arabs.” Founding the Children’s Aid Society, Brace controversially arranged for abandoned children to be sent, by rail, to farm families — “kind, Christian homes in the country” as he put it, typically where they would work. By 1929, approximately 100,000 city children had been relocated across the country, often with no sense of where they would be going, in what became known as the Orphan Train movement and was the precursor to the American foster care system.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27JPBIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27JPBIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27JPBIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27JPBIG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27BIG_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27BIG_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/27/nyregion/27BIG_SPAN/27BIG_SPAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cost Cut, on the Backs of Children', 'kicker': 'Big City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cost Cut,\r\nOn the Backs\r\nOf Children', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foster Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Court Appointed Special Advocates (CASA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T05:34:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5bb5ec8-842e-51eb-86b0-d42b62be987c', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5bb5ec8-842e-51eb-86b0-d42b62be987c'}, {'abstract': 'In 2005, the Mets gave a multiyear deal to Carlos Beltran, a Scott Boras client. Now, with less money and less overall talent, they appear to be eyeing Michael Bourn, another Boras’s client.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/mets-need-a-center-fielder-but-at-what-cost/', 'snippet': 'In 2005, the Mets gave a multiyear deal to Carlos Beltran, a Scott Boras client. Now, with less money and less overall talent, they appear to be eyeing Michael Bourn, another Boras’s client.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eight years ago, the Mets pursued a free-agent center fielder represented by Scott Boras, baseball’s most formidable and unyielding agent. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets Need a Center Fielder, but at What Cost?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beltran, Carlos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T05:54:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Schreiber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schreiber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff3972cd-67d0-5440-9244-d1ebfd5fa9c9', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff3972cd-67d0-5440-9244-d1ebfd5fa9c9'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Sundance) SECRETARY (2002) Maggie Gyllenhaal, right, portrays Lee Holloway, a repressed young secretary with masochistic tendencies and a stint in a mental hospital who blossoms during spanking sessions with her boss, E. Edward Grey (James Spader), a grim lawyer who prefers the typewriter to the computer. Spelling mistakes result in corporal punishment, and soon Edward, like a cruel Pygmalion, is telling Lee how to dress, answer the telephone and even eat. But his instructions help shape and direct her life — and his advances set her aflame. “Secretary,” directed by Steven Shainberg and adapted by Erin Cressida Wilson from a novella by Mary Gaitskill, could be seen “as a slap in the face to orthodox feminist thinking, since the concept of sexual harassment doesn’t seem to occur to anybody.” Stephen Holden wrote in The New York Times. “I prefer to see ‘Secretary’ as a wacky new-wave romantic comedy in which two lonely people locked inside their private fantasies discover a miraculous erotic harmony. In today’s post-Freudian, do-your-own-thing era of free sexual expression, the movie stands to be a wholesome self-help fable about the unlocking of shame and its magical transformation into pleasure and personal liberation. So what if its vision is finally too good to be true?”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "What's On Today", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-26T06:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ec2e5f3-5d34-51ed-a7f5-15f56b46012a', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ec2e5f3-5d34-51ed-a7f5-15f56b46012a'}, {'abstract': 'Stocks return to favor; Mandela protégé returns; U.S. fears Europe techno