article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe03faf0-1c8f-5c89-bb0b-077c93730fa1', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe03faf0-1c8f-5c89-bb0b-077c93730fa1'}, {'abstract': 'To avoid union opposition, the postwar temp industry exploited cultural ambivalence about middle-class white women’s work. Then it became a model for corporate America.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/26/the-rise-of-the-permanent-temp-economy/', 'snippet': 'To avoid union opposition, the postwar temp industry exploited cultural ambivalence about middle-class white women’s work. Then it became a model for corporate America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politicians across the political spectrum herald “job creation,” but frightfully few of them talk about what kinds of jobs are being created. Yet this clearly matters: According to the Census Bureau, one-third of adults who live in poverty are working but do not earn enough to support themselves and their families.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of the Permanent Temp Economy', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rise of the Permatemp', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temporary Employment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kelly Services Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'n