stake in Industrial and Commercial Bank of China, the largest Chinese bank.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/goldman-sachs-to-sell-1-billion-stake-in-i-c-b-c/', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs is selling a $1 billion stake in Industrial and Commercial Bank of China, the largest Chinese bank.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs is selling a $1 billion stake in Industrial and Commercial Bank of China, the largest Chinese bank.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Sachs to Sell $1 Billion Stake in Chinese Bank', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Industrial and Commercial Bank of China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T12:11:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8275a5ad-bd11-5316-98e3-a1c1105ad725', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8275a5ad-bd11-5316-98e3-a1c1105ad725'}, {'abstract': 'A study conducted with smart meters showed that consumption of electricity dipped during last year’s game.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/is-there-a-green-side-to-the-super-bowl/', 'snippet': 'A study conducted with smart meters showed that consumption of electricity dipped during last year’s game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Super Bowl is known for unpredictable half-time shows, fabulous TV commercials and, of course, football. But how about energy savings?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is There a Green Side to the Super Bowl?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy Efficiency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opower', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T12:16:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49a10bd3-45ce-518b-8834-79b91001b64b', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49a10bd3-45ce-518b-8834-79b91001b64b'}, {'abstract': 'SAC Capital Advisors is working hard to contain the fallout from a Justice Department inquiry. | After fleeing equities after the financial crisis, individual investors are now pouring money into stock mutual funds. | BlackRock is said to be buying a stake in Twitter that values the company at more than $9 billion. | Wall Street executives in Davos pondered whether the financial industry was experiencing a brain drain.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/sacs-efforts-to-retain-clients-and-staff-members/', 'snippet': 'SAC Capital Advisors is working hard to contain the fallout from a Justice Department inquiry. | After fleeing equities after the financial crisis, individual investors are now pouring money into stock mutual funds. | BlackRock is said to be buyin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven A. Cohen of SAC Capital Advisors does not appear concerned about the ongoing insider trading investigation, with plans to show up at a hedge fund conference in Florida on Monday after making the rounds in Davos, Switzerland, last week. But beneath the calm exterior, employees and lawyers of SAC are working hard to contain the fallout from the Justice Department’s inquiry into the firm, DealBook’s Peter Lattman reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SAC’s Efforts to Retain Clients and Staff Members', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T12:59:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be2ee38e-e49c-5476-90d4-a0a825c74700', 'word_count': 1890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be2ee38e-e49c-5476-90d4-a0a825c74700'}, {'abstract': 'The bicentennial of the book’s publication has prompted a full calendar of events.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/austen-fans-to-celebrate-200-years-of-pride-and-prejudice/', 'snippet': 'The bicentennial of the book’s publication has prompted a full calendar of events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jane Austen lovers around the world have begun breaking out their bonnets for a yearlong celebration of the 200th anniversary of the publication of “Pride and Prejudice,” which loosed its famous first line on the world on Jan. 28, 1813.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Austen Fans to Celebrate 200 Years of ‘Pride and Prejudice’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Austen, Jane, Society of North America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T13:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/921fb205-0ee5-59a3-8235-11a28b8f9e91', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/921fb205-0ee5-59a3-8235-11a28b8f9e91'}, {'abstract': 'Two Harvard graduates are trying to reconcile students’ viewing habits with the desire of cable companies and programmers to be paid for wireless content; NBC is looking for newsmen and newswomen to star in a planned reality show; and WBEZ, the Chicago public radio station, is hoping that Chicagoans will help create a new generation of listeners.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/the-breakfast-meeting-courting-future-cable-subscribers-a-newspaper-reality-show-and-a-saucy-ad-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Two Harvard graduates are trying to reconcile students’ viewing habits with the desire of cable companies and programmers to be paid for wireless content; NBC is looking for newsmen and newswomen to star in a planned reality show; and WBEZ, the Ch...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tivli, a start-up founded by two Harvard graduates, is trying to reconcile students’ viewing habits, which increasingly involve mobile devices, with the desire of cable companies like Comcast and programmers like HBO to be paid for wireless content. As Brian Stelter reports, resident students at Harvard and, of as of last week, Yale, can use a service created by Tivli to stream local TV stations, a couple of dozen cable channels and the universities’ own in-house channels to computers, tablets or video game consoles. This ability, available anywhere on campus, may be part of the answer to a conundrum for the television industry. Young people watch less TV than they used to, and some say they do not see the point of an expensive cable or satellite subscription. If they become accustomed to viewing programming through services like HBO GO while in college, however, some believe it could lead them to become cable subscribers after they graduate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Courting Future Cable Subscribers, a Newspaper Reality Show, and a Saucy Ad Campaign', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comcast Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montana', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T13:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/710f5f6f-1195-58fd-9311-7c56f659498b', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/710f5f6f-1195-