 of people trapped by rising floodwaters in the Australian state of Queensland after a cyclone and heavy rains. [BBC]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Rising Floodwaters in Australia', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T15:25:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f48e0574-3e5d-5c62-afb0-dffa0dc8316b', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f48e0574-3e5d-5c62-afb0-dffa0dc8316b'}, {'abstract': 'The president’s greatest challenge often came not from the South, but from the Peace Democrats.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/lincoln-and-the-copperheads/', 'snippet': 'The president’s greatest challenge often came not from the South, but from the Peace Democrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January 1863, Abraham Lincoln made a remarkable confession. He was, he told a senator, more worried about “the fire in the rear” than he was about the Confederates to his front.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lincoln and the Copperheads', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T15:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer L. Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48ad78b8-65a6-59be-975a-a94c6548d7a6', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48ad78b8-65a6-59be-975a-a94c6548d7a6'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/what-were-reading-616/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mother Jones: Quinoa, the “it” plant that is the new darling of gluten-intolerant omnivores, has its dark side: land issues, environmental impact and sudden luxury prices that bar Bolivians themselves from access to their longtime nutritional staple. – Glenn Collins', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T15:31:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ecef157-eef1-58c1-9af6-ff2be104ff27', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ecef157-eef1-58c1-9af6-ff2be104ff27'}, {'abstract': 'Account assignments, executive appointments and miscellaneous news from advertising agencies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/business/media/news-from-the-advertising-industry.html', 'snippet': 'Account assignments, executive appointments and miscellaneous news from advertising agencies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Accounts', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News From the Advertising Industry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'News From the Advertising Industry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T15:34:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67ce3d3d-7c9a-514d-8cb9-2d8438af4fcc', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67ce3d3d-7c9a-514d-8cb9-2d8438af4fcc'}, {'abstract': 'The nomination of Mary Jo White as S.E.C. chief has been hailed as a sign a tough regulator will patrol Wall Street. But there are questions about whether her defense work will raise conflicts of interest.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/how-mary-jo-whites-connections-could-complicate-her-s-e-c-job/', 'snippet': 'The nomination of Mary Jo White as S.E.C. chief has been hailed as a sign a tough regulator will patrol Wall Street. But there are questions about whether her defense work will raise conflicts of interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nomination of Mary Jo White as chairwoman of the Securities and Exchange Commission has been hailed as a signal that a tough regulator will be patrolling Wall Street. But there are also questions being raised about whether her work as a leading white-collar defense lawyer over the last decade might hamper her effectiveness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Mary Jo White’s Connections Could Complicate Her S.E.C. Job', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, John J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Mary Jo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T15:37:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9cd3216-e2db-5c83-9ee3-cbaf36cbec7e', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9cd3216-e2db-5c83-9ee3-cbaf36cbec7e'}, {'abstract': 'Google argues that government requests for user information have been increasing, particularly in the United States, where law enforcement does not need a judge’s consent to ask for private online information.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/google-says-electronic-snooping-by-governments-should-be-more-difficult/', 'snippet': 'Google argues that government requests for user information have been increasing, particularly in the United States, where law enforcement does not need a judge’s consent to ask for private online information.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If a government wants to peek into your Web-based e-mail account, it is surprisingly easy, most of the time not even requiring a judge’s approval. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/28/technology/28bits-google/28bits-google-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 406, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 