d shotguns from Newark into a line of sleek bangle bracelets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jessica Mindich is a jewelry designer who has begun transforming recycled pistols, rifles and shotguns from Newark into a line of sleek bangle bracelets. Twenty percent of the proceeds will go directly to the gun buyback program in Newark, she said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Connecticut Mother Makes Bangles of Recycled Guns', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T19:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Ellin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ellin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fc35189-bcc9-5bb1-814f-8a5494f55409', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fc35189-bcc9-5bb1-814f-8a5494f55409'}, {'abstract': 'Tate Donovan, a star of “Argo,” recounts what it was like to live with his fellow actors in preparation for their roles as Americans trapped in Iran.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/video-tate-donovan-on-living-like-its-1979/', 'snippet': 'Tate Donovan, a star of “Argo,” recounts what it was like to live with his fellow actors in preparation for their roles as Americans trapped in Iran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The multilingual Middle Eastern verisimilitude of “Argo” that Ben Affleck spoke about in his SAG acceptance speech was mostly achieved in and around Hollywood. Mr. Affleck had hoped to shoot in Turkey, he said over the weekend, but most of “Argo” was actually filmed here in Los Angeles, with local actors. As George Clooney, a producer of the film, pointed out, there is a large Persian population here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Tate Donovan on Living Like 1979', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T19:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d45476f1-a493-5f6c-be51-d99c4726d84b', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d45476f1-a493-5f6c-be51-d99c4726d84b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Naked Statistics,” Charles Wheelan shows how health has nothing in common with the laws of physics and everything in common with lottery cards, mutual funds and tomorrow’s weather forecast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/29/science/naked-statistics-by-charles-wheelan-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Naked Statistics,” Charles Wheelan shows how health has nothing in common with the laws of physics and everything in common with lottery cards, mutual funds and tomorrow’s weather forecast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The chances are that you turn to this part of the newspaper in search of some reliable tools for optimizing your health. The chances are that you periodically visit a doctor for the same reason.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/29/science/29scibooks/29scibooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 912, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/29/science/29scibooks/29scibooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 912}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',