', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabetta Povoledo and Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabetta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Povoledo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b502667b-5323-5014-be6c-940a3d570502', 'word_count': 1480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b502667b-5323-5014-be6c-940a3d570502'}, {'abstract': 'A dish with an Eastern European accent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/dining/what-to-eat-with-slovene-white-wines.html', 'snippet': 'A dish with an Eastern European accent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’re bound to find a cheese — something hard, runny, funky, sharp or mild — to nibble with a Slovene white wine, so you could serve cheese and be done with it. But finding a dish is not a particular challenge, either. Just pick something pleasant and white-wine friendly, because what will be in the glass will be intriguing enough. The Eastern European geography led me to think of stuffed cabbage, but with a light chicken filling and dressed with sour cream. You may have to grind your own chicken; most butchers will not do it, but your food processor will. Freezing the cabbage overnight eliminates the need to boil the leaves to soften them. FLORENCE FABRICANT', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/dining/30PAIR/30PAIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/30/dining/30PAIR/30PAIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xl