people in some patients in a vegetative state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In late 2011, Canadian researchers using a novel method of interpreting electroencephalograms asked 16 patients in an irreversible “vegetative” state to move their hands or toes. In three people, the scientists found brain activity similar to that of healthy people given the same instructions. The result suggested that the “vegetative” patients in fact might be hearing and understanding what was said.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/29/health/well_prognosis/well_prognosis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 223, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/29/health/well_prognosis/well_prognosis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 223, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/29/health/well_prognosis/well_prognosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/05/29/health/well_prognosis/well_prognosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/29/health/well_prognosis/well_prognosis-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Interpreting Brain Waves', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prognosis: Interpreting Brain Waves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'na