losing the office that was working on closing the prison at Guantánamo Bay.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/a-broken-promise/', 'snippet': 'The Obama administration is closing the office that was working on closing the prison at Guantánamo Bay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m sure you’ve had one of those moments when you hear that a celebrity or other public figure has just died and you think, “Really? He was still alive?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Broken Promise', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fried, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-28T22:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2792467a-c438-5dd3-92f6-bccf69486bf1', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2792467a-c438-5dd3-92f6-bccf69486bf1'}, {'abstract': 'At the Huntington Theater Company in Boston, staging Ralph Ellison’s “Invisible Man,” about a young black man trying to navigate a racist world, proves to be a challenging task.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/29/theater/reviews/ralph-ellisons-invisible-man-at-the-huntington-theater.html', 'snippet': 'At the Huntington Theater Company in Boston, staging Ralph Ellison’s “Invisible Man,” about a young black man trying to navigate a racist world, proves to be a challenging task.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The ringing speeches and singing sermons never seem to stop in the stage version of “Invisible Man,” Ralph Ellison’s landmark 1952 novel about a young African-American in search of himself, now at the Huntington Theater Company’s\xa0Boston University Theater after stints in Chicago and Washington. Although the book’s protagonist does not find his voice as a professional orator until midway through his harrowing journey in a racially divided America, in Oren Jacoby’s faithful but lifeless theatrical adaptation the hero often seems to be presiding at an invisible podium, providing us with crisp talking points about the cruel truths of the black experience.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/29/arts/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp/SUB-29INVISIBLE-jp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnai