me': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orlando Magic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Nets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lopez, Brook', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-29T03:47:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65edbb03-f556-56e2-8f8e-af6eeab3b116', 'word_count': 836, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65edbb03-f556-56e2-8f8e-af6eeab3b116'}, {'abstract': 'Ravens Coach John Harbaugh seemed a lot happier to be in New Orleans than his brother, 49ers Coach Jim Harbaugh.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/28/the-ravens-are-happy-and-ray-lewis-is-retiring-repeat-as-needed/', 'snippet': 'Ravens Coach John Harbaugh seemed a lot happier to be in New Orleans than his brother, 49ers Coach Jim Harbaugh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — By the time Coach John Harbaugh of the Baltimore Ravens addressed the news media here on Monday night, his brother Jim, the coach of the San Francisco 49ers, had already faced the masses twice. In terms of the media circus of Super Bowl XLVII, an argument can be made that arriving first is not much of an advantage.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ravens Are Happy and Ray Lewis Is Retiring. Repeat as Needed.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going, Going...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harbaugh, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-29T03:59:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b469174-8b6d-5c1c-807b-64b78757e504', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b469174-8b6d-5c1c-807b-64b78757e504'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Kidd’s abrupt departure on Sunday underlined the tightrope walked by the Knicks, the league’s oldest team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/29/sports/basketball/knicks-will-ask-less-of-jason-kidd.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Kidd’s abrupt departure on Sunday underlined the tightrope walked by the Knicks, the league’s oldest team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After playing five minutes in Sunday’s game against the Atlanta Hawks, Jason Kidd headed to the bench, put on a white Knicks T-shirt and began talking with his teammates. He never made it back on the court. Instead, he went in the opposite direction, to the team’s locker room, where he spent the second half as his teammates battled to a 106-104 victory. Now the question is what direction Kidd’s health is going with half of the regular season remaining. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knicks to Ask Less of Kidd After Asking for Whole Lot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Knicks to Ask Less of Kidd After Asking for Whole Lot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Nate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'n