ion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05a4aea8-ed69-5197-9c47-b81c6427562d', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05a4aea8-ed69-5197-9c47-b81c6427562d'}, {'abstract': 'In his first published speech, Xi Jinping did not mention China’s quarrel with Japan over islands in the East China Sea but said he would not bargain over “core” territorial and security interests.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/world/asia/incoming-chinese-leader-will-not-to-bargain-on-disputed-territory.html', 'snippet': 'In his first published speech, Xi Jinping did not mention China’s quarrel with Japan over islands in the East China Sea but said he would not bargain over “core” territorial and security interests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — China will never bargain over what it deems to be “core” territorial and security interests, the country’s top leader, Xi Jinping, said in his first published speech setting out his foreign policy views since taking over as head of the Communist Party. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Leader Affirms Policy on Islands', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Leader Affirms Policy On Islands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-29T09:51:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f6746aa-e750-5c74-845e-94b575c8997b', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f6746aa-e750-5c74-845e-94b575c8997b'}, {'abstract': 'A union resurgence is taking place. Is that enough to save organized labor?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/magazine/do-unions-have-a-shot-in-the-21st-century.html', 'snippet': 'A union resurgence is taking place. Is that enough to save organized labor?', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 25 years, Monty Newcomb has worked at the same chemical plant in Calvert City, Ky., making products that hold pills together and remove sediment from beer. In his early years, Newcomb watched his union, the International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers, lose one battle after another in its ongoing struggle with management. In the ’80s and ’90s, workers throughout the country justifiably feared that their plants might close and move to a union-free state. Or that they might simply import products from low-wage, nonunionized countries like Mexico and China. Newcomb told me that at his company, International Specialty Products, workers were disgruntled and the work suffered — for example, more than 20 percent of one chemical they produced didn’t pass inspection.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03economy/mag-03Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03economy/mag-03Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03economy/mag-03Economy-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'hei