ed Monday, zaps the story from 16th-century Mantua to Las Vegas of the early 1960s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly into Act I of the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of Verdi’s “Rigoletto,” which opened on Monday night, the boyishly exuberant tenor Piotr Beczala, playing the Duke as a Frank Sinatra-like playboy in a stylish white tuxedo, grabs a microphone — fake, of course. He then sings the aria “Questa o quella” to a rowdy, decadent crowd at the Duke’s gaudy casino.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1266, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/01/30/arts/music/30rigoletto-1/30rigoletto-1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/01/30/art