e never seen a United Nations report alleging killings at the Line of Control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following the recent killings of Indian and Pakistani soldiers near the Kashmir border, a local newspaper reported classified United Nations documents show that the cycle of violence between troops of the two countries has continued despite the cease-fire in 2003.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Knowledge of Pakistan Complaints, Indian Officials Say', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T13:40:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Betwa Sharma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Betwa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sharma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83b234ba-70da-5e1a-b7bb-0f05a767c290', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83b234ba-70da-5e1a-b7bb-0f05a767c290'}, {'abstract': 'Your Disney questions answered, you can learn to be a butcher, W-2 forms list employer cost of health plans and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/wednesday-reading-your-disney-questions-answered/', 'snippet': 'Your Disney questions answered, you can learn to be a butcher, W-2 forms list employer cost of health plans and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: Your Disney Questions Answered', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T13:56:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ab7a14a-1c1b-5201-adf0-a628a5282a25', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ab7a14a-1c1b-5201-adf0-a628a5282a25'}, {'abstract': 'The classically trained soprano has melded sacred music with a baroque pop sensibility on her debut solo album, “The Deserters.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/tune-in-to-rachel-zeffira/', 'snippet': 'The classically trained soprano has melded sacred music with a baroque pop sensibility on her debut solo album, “The Deserters.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rachel Zeffira is nothing if not a lateral thinker. When she was 17, a bureaucratic error nearly resulted in her deportation from Britain and caused her to lose her place at a music school in London. So Zeffira, who is from Canada, reinvented herself as a 26-year-old substitute French teacher (“a language I don’t speak”) and stayed in England until she raised enough money to continue her studies in Verona, Italy. Years later, when she and her bandmate from Cat’s Eyes, Faris Badwan (the frontman of the Horrors), were looking for a venue to play their first gig, she used the contacts she’d made in Italy to secure an appearance at a Vatican Mass attended by several cardinals. “I told them we were a choir,” she says. “They thought our backup singers looked a little too sexy. At the last minute we had to rush out and buy opaque tights and shawls.” In her latest example of creative adaptation, the classically trained soprano and multi-instrumentalist has melded sacred music with a baroque pop sensibility on her debut solo album, “The Deserters.” It was released late last year in Britain (the North American release, on Paper Bag Records, is scheduled for March) and is earning rave reviews, with the BBC memorably describing it as “tummy meltingly wonderful.” Dreamy, seductive and sparse, “The Deserters” has a quiet lushness that’s markedly different from the girl-group-tinged sound of Cat’s Eyes, though Zeffira hesitates to specify how. “I like to leave it to the listener to describe the music,” she says. “I don’t like it to be spelled out.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tune in To | Rachel Zeffira', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2875d027-9466-5de1-adb9-4aa5e722ed8a', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2875d027-9466-5de1-adb9-4aa5e722ed8a'}, {'abstract': 'Introducing the Gun Report, which will track gun deaths around the country.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/the-gun-report-january-30-2013/', 'snippet': 'Introducing the Gun Report, which will track gun deaths around the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After my column on Tuesday about a week’s toll of gun violence, a number of readers suggested that I continue to publish examples of gun deaths that resulted from accidental shootings, momentary rage, a feud with a neighbor, a drive-by shooting—and all the other reasons American kill each other with guns. I think it is a good idea. For the foreseeable future, my assistant, Jennifer Mascia, and I, will be culling one or two news stories each day, and linking to them on my blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: January 30, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64514d0f-4c7b-504a-89a2-9513886970c8', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64514d0f-4c7b-504a-89a2-9513886970c8'}, {'abstract': 'The Netherlands used to have a reputation as a paradise for artists, but this month dozens of organizations there have had to shut their doors. How should the country’s arts groups go about rebuilding themselves?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/dutch-arts-groups-shut-down-as-funding-vanishes/', 'snippet': 'The Netherlands used to have a reputation as a paradise for artists, but this month dozens of organizations there have had to shut their doors. How should the country’s arts groups go about rebuilding themselves?', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMSTERDAM — In the 1980s and ’90s, the Netherlands had a reputation as a kind of paradise for artists. Graduates of fine arts academies could receive long-term grants and special housing subsidies to support them so they wouldn’t have to get day jobs. Edgy theater groups and small contemporary classical ensembles were fully financed to create innovative and experimental work. The government even bought art directly from artists who weren’t particularly commercial, maintaining it in large storage facilities, simply to support artistic production.', '