times.com/2013/01/31/nyregion/commuters-injured-after-new-jersey-train-hits-truck.html', 'snippet': 'There were no immediate reports of fatalities, and the police did not know details about what might have led to a New Jersey Transit train crash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A New Jersey Transit commuter train smashed into a tractor-trailer truck hauling paint at a railroad crossing in Little Falls, N.J., early Wednesday morning, injuring several commuters, the police said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commuters Injured After New Jersey Train Hits Truck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Commuters Injured After New Jersey Train Hits Truck', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Transit', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Little Falls (NJ)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:15:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2f9dc8b-1a74-568e-94ad-82fab2d567ca', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2f9dc8b-1a74-568e-94ad-82fab2d567ca'}, {'abstract': 'Blocking someone from following you on Twitter does not mean that your posts are completely hidden from that person.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/qa-tweeting-in-public-or-private/', 'snippet': 'Blocking someone from following you on Twitter does not mean that your posts are completely hidden from that person.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Tweeting in Public or Private', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Post on Twitter\r\nTo a Chosen Few', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d41250b6-87aa-584b-a92d-ee02ae12dbab', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d41250b6-87aa-584b-a92d-ee02ae12dbab'}, {'abstract': 'A controversy over pejorative language in a German children’s book suggests that “German” is still seen more as designating ethnicity rather than citizenship.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/the-little-witch-and-a-big-problem/', 'snippet': 'A controversy over pejorative language in a German children’s book suggests that “German” is still seen more as designating ethnicity rather than citizenship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Germany’s most beloved witch has been making mischief for more than 50 years, but now she’s caused an uproar that goes to the heart of what it means to be German in the 21st century.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Little Witch and a Big Problem', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'German Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:53:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lucian Kim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lucian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16820512-1250-59af-acdc-c352e22d5378', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16820512-1250-59af-acdc-c352e22d5378'}, {'abstract': 'The charges against Jesse Litvak, a former securities trader at Jefferies & Company, serve as a warning to Wall Street that misleading customers – including sophisticated ones – can result in criminal action, even for a broker who does not owe a fiduciary duty to clients.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/a-warning-to-wall-street-about-misleading-clients/', 'snippet': 'The charges against Jesse Litvak, a former securities trader at Jefferies & Company, serve as a warning to Wall Street that misleading customers – including sophisticated ones – can result in criminal action, even for a broker who does not owe a f...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A former trader of residential mortgage-backed securities at Jefferies & Company, Jesse Litvak, was indicted on charges of lying to investors about the prices and commissions of securities sold as part of a government program to prop up the mortgage bond market. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Warning to Wall St. About Misleading Clients', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8925dfd8-49e8-5d81-a3ff-64d77251d71e', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8925dfd8-49e8-5d81-a3ff-64d77251d71e'}, {'abstract': 'Updates from the Senate Judiciary Committee hearing on gun violence.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/30/updates-from-senate-hearing-on-gun-violence/', 'snippet': 'Updates from the Senate Judiciary Committee hearing on gun violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her brief but moving and emotional remarks, Gabrielle Giffords, the former Representative from Arizona who was shot in the head during a mass killing in 2011, told members of the Senate Judiciary Committee to be “bold and courageous” as they considered ways to reduce gun violence. The Lede followed the testimony of Ms. Giffords, her husband Mark E. Kelly and other witnesses, including Wayne LaPierre, chief executive of the National Rifle Association. As my colleague Jennifer Steinhauer reports, this was the first Senate hearing since the Dec. 14 mass shooting at an elementary school in Newtown, Conn., that killed 20 schoolchildren and 6 staff members.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Updates on the Senate Hearing on Gun Violence', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-30T14:54:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de4cbc6f-543a-5079-a64f-18edb6fa53ee', 'word_count': 2729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de4cbc6f-543a-5079-a64f-18edb6fa53ee'}, {'abstract': 'Brenda S. Sprague of the State Department lists services you can use both before and during your trip overseas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/travel/going-abroad-how-the-state-department-can-help.html', 'snippet': 'Brenda S. Sprague of the State Department lists services you can use both before and during your trip overseas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s some perspective on how drastically our travel habits have changed in recent years: In 1989, seven million Americans had passports, according to the State Department. Today 113 million do. Gone, too, are days when Americans stuck almost exclusively to the London-Paris-Rome circuit, said Brenda S. Sprague, the State Department’s deputy assistant secretary for passport services. “That gives them the potential to get in more trouble,” she said. “But now they’re better able to keep in touch with us, and we with them.” Below are edited excerpts from a conversation with Ms. Sprague on what help the State Department can offer you abroad. \xa0', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/travel/03QNA1/03QNA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/0