rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chip Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/518042f9-2844-5907-b830-9a8dda7bbfaf', 'word_count': 5895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/518042f9-2844-5907-b830-9a8dda7bbfaf'}, {'abstract': 'It’s hard to remember when Middle Eastern food barely existed outside the Middle East. That was before Claudia Roden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/magazine/a-time-before-tabbouleh.html', 'snippet': 'It’s hard to remember when Middle Eastern food barely existed outside the Middle East. That was before Claudia Roden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I had been cooking for only a few years when, in 1972, a friend gave me “A Book of Middle Eastern Food,” by a woman named Claudia Roden. In my cooking life, there was no more important influence than that book. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1009, 'width': 1434, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/magazine/03eat1/mag-03Eat-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Time Before Tabbouleh', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Time Before Tabbouleh', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bittman, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roden, Claudia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07b05884-dd32-5c70-a25c-f0570896d78b', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07b05884-dd32-5c70-a25c-f0570896d78b'}, {'abstract': 'Should they just know how to sell? Or do they need to be taught?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/how-much-training-do-sales-representatives-need/', 'snippet': 'Should they just know how to sell? Or do they need to be taught?', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the last She Owns It business group meeting, Beth Shaw, who owns YogaFit, wondered why her new sales representative wasn’t selling what she was brought on to sell — YogaFit conference sponsorships, and the company’s branded merchandise, which includes clothing, yoga mats and DVDs. Initially, the conversation focused on compensation and learning curves. But as the discussion continued, a group member, Alexandra Mayzler, the owner of Thinking Caps Tutoring, raised the issue of training.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Much Training Do Sales Representatives Need?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YogaFit Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c20cdd7-9829-56d5-ba86-d08b0f1f71bd', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c20cdd7-9829-56d5-ba86-d08b0f1f71bd'}, {'abstract': 'The more distant World War II becomes, the more Russians seem to commemorate it.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/the-russian-obsession-with-world-war-ii/', 'snippet': 'The more distant World War II becomes, the more Russians seem to commemorate it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Last week Prime Minister David Cameron described what distinguishes Britain from other European countries: “argumentative,” “strong-minded,” “an island nation.” This was in a speech in which he promised a referendum on whether Britain should stay in the European Union and discussed how the great trading bloc should be reformed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Medals and Memories', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:04:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Bullough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bullough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33c9d9e5-5c1f-5e88-aada-47ee8a3b9240', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33c9d9e5-5c1f-5e88-aada-47ee8a3b9240'}, {'abstract': 'The Financial Services Authority said it had reviewed the sale of certain interest-rate products to small businesses and found that more than 90 percent were sold improperly, in a case that could prove costly for banks.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/british-banks-face-another-legal-headache/', 'snippet': 'The Financial Services Authority said it had reviewed the sale of certain interest-rate products to small businesses and found that more than 90 percent were sold improperly, in a case that could prove costly for banks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Britain’s biggest banks might have to face up to another expensive mishap. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Banks Face a Fresh Legal Headache', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Services Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC.', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lloyds Banking Group Plc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Bank of Scotland PLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:23:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd0ff4ae-124a-56e6-b851-2bbcdcf2cc42', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd0ff4ae-124a-56e6-b851-2bbcdcf2cc42'}, {'abstract': 'Free programs on the Web can let you edit Microsoft Word files, even if you do not have the program installed on your computer.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/tip-of-the-week-use-word-on-the-web/', 'snippet': 'Free programs on the Web can let you edit Microsoft Word files, even if you do not have the program installed on your computer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even if you do not have Microsoft Word installed on your computer, you can edit and collaborate on Word documents others have sent or shared with you just by using your Web browser. One way to do this is with Microsoft’s free SkyDrive cloud service — and its included Office Web Apps versions of Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OneNote. To open and edit the document, just log into your SkyDrive account through your Web browser and upload the file; SkyDrive users can also share files through the service.(If you use the SkyDrive desktop program, you can also drag the file into the SkyDrive folder to upload it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Use Word on the Web', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:25:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31d134b4-a65a-5df5-b80d-0a39abb60b9e', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31d134b4-a65a-5df5-b80d-0a39abb60b9e'}, {'abstract': 'The Republic of Congo has set aside a new national park for some 15,000 gorillas.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/lowland-gorillas-protected-in-a-green-abyss/', 'snippet': 'The Republic of Congo has set aside a new national park for some 15,000 gorillas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The locals said it could not be done. It was 1999, and the hardheaded ecologist J. Michael Fay was determined to tromp across 2,000 miles of the Congo Basin. In a northeastern patch, he ran into a swampy tangle so thick that he struggled to advance by a single mile in under 12 hours.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lowland Gorillas, Protected in a Green Abyss', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poaching (Wildlife)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fay, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samper, Cristian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wildlife Conservation Society', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:32:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec99d0b6-8b40-5ea9-b586-94a4d308a816', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec99d0b6-8b40-5ea9-b586-94a4d308a816'}, {'abstract': 'Prominent secular opponents of President Mohamed Morsi gathered with the Egyptian leader’s Islamist allies on Thursday after days of political chaos threatening the transition to democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/01/world/middleeast/egypt-rivals-hold-rare-meeting-call-for-dialogue.html', 'snippet': 'Prominent secular opponents of President Mohamed Morsi gathered with the Egyptian leader’s Islamist allies on Thursday after days of political chaos threatening the transition to democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — With Egypt’s political elites warring and street violence taking on a life of its own, young revolutionaries on Thursday tried to step into the country’s leadership vacuum, organizing a rare meeting of political forces that, in Egypt’s polarized state, was a victory in itself. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt Rivals Hold Rare Meeting and Call for Dialogue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rivals Across Egypt’s Political Spectrum Hold Rare Meeting, Urging Dialogue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheikh, Mayy El', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:43:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim and Mayy El Sheikh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mayy', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Sheikh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76257706-6287-5088-9d1e-83561fa88469', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76257706-6287-5088-9d1e-83561fa88469'}, {'abstract': 'The company attributed the net loss to writing down the value of ST-Ericsson, an unprofitable smartphone component venture with the French chip maker STMicroelectronics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/01/technology/ericsson-sales-rise-on-spending-to-upgrade-mobile-networks.html', 'snippet': 'The company attributed the net loss to writing down the value of ST-Ericsson, an unprofitable smartphone component venture with the French chip maker STMicroelectronics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Ericsson, the world’s biggest maker of mobile telephone network equipment, said Thursday that its sales and profit grew faster than expected in the fourth quarter as phone operators in the United States and Canada spent heavily to upgrade wireless networks.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ericsson Posts Loss, but Sales Increase', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ericsson Posts Loss, But Sales Increase', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Kevin J", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Telefonaktiebolaget L M Ericsson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jolly, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nokia Oyj', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': "By Kevin J. O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d8c25b5-8fe8-5ba3-8967-252f69382bee', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d8c25b5-8fe8-5ba3-8967-252f69382bee'}, {'abstract': 'Deutsche Bank posts a $3 billion loss in the fourth quarter. | Facebook’s earnings give investors reasons for optimism and some cause for concern. | A government lawyer who secured a conviction of Raj Rajaratnam is headed to a private firm. | Research in Motion changes its name to BlackBerry, underscoring its bet on that brand.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/deutsche-banks-restructuring-weighs-on-results/', 'snippet': 'Deutsche Bank posts a $3 billion loss in the fourth quarter. | Facebook’s earnings give investors reasons for optimism and some cause for concern. | A government lawyer who secured a conviction of Raj Rajaratnam is headed to a private firm. | Rese...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a difficult turnaround for Deutsche Bank. The big German financial firm said on Thursday that it lost 2.2 billion euros ($3 billion) in the fourth quarter, as it was hit by legal costs and expenses related to its restructuring. “The results underline the task ahead for Jürgen Fitschen and Anshu Jain, the co-chief executives who took over the bank less than seven months ago and have declared their intention to deal more severely with the legacy of the financial crisis,” Jack Ewing writes in DealBook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Restructuring Costs Hobble Deutsche Bank', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:52:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/465c4fd6-363f-57c1-a979-c9e72b3e7011', 'word_count': 2086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/465c4fd6-363f-57c1-a979-c9e72b3e7011'}, {'abstract': 'The language of the Indian state is often sentimental, but in reality it is a practical corporation that tries to appease in the easiest ways possible its most valued consumers. Which is not a bad thing. But, like most practical people, the state is a coward. It wants to completely eliminate imaginary risks to its survival and is willing to do even stupid things that have no meaning to achieve that.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/the-indian-state-is-a-coward/', 'snippet': 'The language of the Indian state is often sentimental, but in reality it is a practical corporation that tries to appease in the easiest ways possible its most valued consumers. Which is not a bad thing. But, like most practical people, the state ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI – When the Indian state wants to laugh it probably reads Hegel’s hypothesis, “The state is the actuality of the ethical Idea.” The language of the Indian state is often sentimental, but in reality it is a practical corporation that tries to appease in the easiest ways possible its most valued consumers. Which is not a bad thing. But, like most practical people, the state is a coward. It wants to completely eliminate imaginary risks to its survival and is willing to do even stupid things that have no meaning to achieve that. That is the reason why there is no substantial free speech in India. The state sides with those who are offended even if their claim is farcical. This is the subject of my latest Letter from India.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Indian State is a Coward', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jaipur (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manu Joseph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joseph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17e15dec-78c2-5dbf-a1f5-c3eb4f61d097', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17e15dec-78c2-5dbf-a1f5-c3eb4f61d097'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck Hagel, a Republican former senator and President Obama’s nominee to be secretary of defense, met with sharp questions at his confirmation hearing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/01/us/politics/sharp-exchanges-expected-in-hearing-on-hagel-nomination.html', 'snippet': 'Chuck Hagel, a Republican former senator and President Obama’s nominee to be secretary of defense, met with sharp questions at his confirmation hearing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Chuck Hagel, President Obama’s nominee to be secretary of defense, faced sharp and sometimes angry questioning from fellow Republicans — especially his old friend Senator John McCain — at a contentious confirmation hearing on Thursday that focused on his past statements on Iran, the influence of pro-Israel organizations in Washington and the Iraq war. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hagel Has Rough Outing Before Ex-Colleagues', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hagel Has Rough Outing Before Ex-Colleagues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bumiller, Elisabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagel, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Armed Services', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T12:56:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Bumiller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66b6880d-4398-5f4a-aecc-2e5096e10142', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66b6880d-4398-5f4a-aecc-2e5096e10142'}, {'abstract': 'Museums should stop treating pre-20th-century American folk art as inferior to the academic art of the same period.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/arts/design/american-folk-vs-academic-art.html', 'snippet': 'Museums should stop treating pre-20th-century American folk art as inferior to the academic art of the same period.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A MODEST PROPOSAL for this country’s great repositories of pre-20th-century American art: Why don’t you, as Diana Vreeland might have asked, mix folk art in with the more realistic, academically correct kind that has so dominated museums since the 19th century? Despite rising interest in and scholarship about folk art — and even after the wholesale rethinking of several major American wings on the East Coast — the isolation of folk from academic is still the norm. Given that we live in a time of eroding aesthetic boundaries and categories, when many curators are experimenting with integrative approaches in international biennials and commercial galleries, it seems past time for the folk-academic division to soften. It undoubtedly has at some institutions, especially those with modest collections. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1450, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/arts/design/03folk_337/03folk_337-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Curator, Tear Down These Walls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curator, Tear Down These Walls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T13:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9acbf9a-e6ea-5bd5-8917-629f39e7d7c4', 'word_count': 1829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9acbf9a-e6ea-5bd5-8917-629f39e7d7c4'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/opinion-report-cats/', 'snippet': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the editorial page', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: Cats', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T13:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1ff56d8-fc84-5c86-a801-906daf38ab1a', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1ff56d8-fc84-5c86-a801-906daf38ab1a'}, {'abstract': 'The Friday cry at suitcase schools: Don’t go! Colleges are working to reverse the weekend exodus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/education/edlife/at-suitcase-schools-around-the-country-friday-means-its-time-to-leave.html', 'snippet': 'The Friday cry at suitcase schools: Don’t go! Colleges are working to reverse the weekend exodus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON any given Saturday, Central Connecticut State University is nearly empty. No Frisbee flinging, no lingering over lattes at Jazzman’s Cafe and Bakery. The library closes at 3:45, the cafeteria at 6:30 sharp.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 986, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 986}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 624, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1888, 'width': 1149, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/education/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0/edl-03suitcase-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off Off Off Campus', 'kicker': 'Education Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off Off Off Campus: The Friday