': 'Leather and Leather Goods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T21:19:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a104786c-9ad7-5695-8742-94a05a492fe0', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a104786c-9ad7-5695-8742-94a05a492fe0'}, {'abstract': 'Screening room programmers, known for showing critically acclaimed movies, have found there is an audience for box-office flops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/01/nyregion/box-office-duds-have-a-following-too.html', 'snippet': 'Screening room programmers, known for showing critically acclaimed movies, have found there is an audience for box-office flops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a cold night in early December a young crowd packed into the cozy 72-seat screening room at the 92Y TriBeCa. The film that was about to be shown would be accompanied by live commentary from the hosts of “The Flop House,” a podcast dedicated to bad movies. Those hosts were two moonlighting writers from “The Daily Show,” Dan McCoy and Elliott Kalan, and their laconic friend Stuart Wellington. After a short introduction, the three sat in the front row as the lights went down.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/01/arts/01BAD1/01BAD1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 856, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/01/arts/01BAD1/01BAD1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 856}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':