 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Henry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Autonation Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Museum of American History', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opel, Adam, AG', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Smithsonian Institution', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-01-31T21:48:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/234e9b3c-d46c-520e-b23c-1e7e7b0dbca1', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/234e9b3c-d46c-520e-b23c-1e7e7b0dbca1'}, {'abstract': 'Christoph Irmscher studies the contributions and contradictions of the pioneering scientist Louis Agassiz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/03/books/review/louis-agassiz-by-christoph-irmscher.html', 'snippet': 'Christoph Irmscher studies the contributions and contradictions of the pioneering scientist Louis Agassiz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the California earthquake of 1906, the marble statue of Louis Agassiz toppled off the second story of Stanford University’s zoology building and plunged headfirst into the ground. The great scientist, with his head buried in concrete, his upturned body sticking up into air, became an iconic image of the earthquake. Agassiz is often remembered as a fallen man, Christoph Irmscher tells us. His rejection of Darwinian evolution and his conviction that America belonged to the whites only are an embarrassment to science. A decade ago, an eighth grader at the Agassiz School in Cambridge, Mass., came across a description of Agassiz’s racism and suggested the school change its name. It did, calling itself after the school’s first African-\xadAmerican principal, Maria L. Baldwin. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1861, 'width': 1508, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/03/books/review/03STOTT/03STOTT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Microsc